var e={"concept:barbarians":{desc:`Barbarians are stateless tribes raiding from camps on the edges of the world. Villages are gifts of the past: any land unit may enter one, and it usually pays.`,body:`How restless barbarians are is chosen when a game is created, from none at all to raging hordes. Camps appear in unsettled land far from cities and send out raiders; raids also come by sea. When the first civilization enters the Middle Ages, an uprising may break out — a sudden wave of barbarians.

A barbarian walking into an undefended city plunders it — a share of the treasury or the work in progress, a citizen only when there is nothing else to take — and leaves. Having beaten a defender, it tears down the [[building:walls|City Walls]]. A dispersed camp yields a little gold. Your units attack barbarians with a bonus set by the [[concept:difficulty|difficulty level]], and [[wonder:the_great_wall|the Great Wall]] strengthens them further.

A village may give gold, a tech, a map of the area, a new city, a settler or mercenaries, but now and then barbarians come out of it. For [[trait:expansionist|Expansionist]] civs villages are never hostile, and their starting [[unit:scout|Scout]] gets to many of them first.

Barbarians take no part in victories or score and bring neither a [[concept:golden_age|golden age]] nor [[concept:leaders|leaders]]. Keep at least one defender in every city and break up camps near your borders before they grow.`,fact:`The word “vandalism” was coined in 1794 by a French bishop, Henri Grégoire, who blamed revolutionary mobs for smashing art “like Vandals.” The real Vandals, who sacked Rome in 455, have suffered from it ever since.`},"concept:colonies":{desc:`A worker can raise a building on a tile: an outpost to watch, a colony on a distant resource, an airfield or a radar tower. Each costs the worker, and borders decide its fate.`,body:`The [[job:outpost|Outpost]] comes with [[tech:masonry|Masonry]]; it stands on your own or unclaimed land and sees far, farther still from high ground. A [[job:colony|Colony]] goes on a luxury or strategic resource outside every border; once a road links it to a city, the resource joins your [[concept:trade_network|trade network]].

An [[job:airfield|Airfield]], after [[tech:flight|Flight]], bases any number of aircraft on your own or unclaimed land. A [[job:radar|Radar Tower]], after [[tech:advanced_flight|Advanced Flight]], goes only on your own land and boosts the attack and defence of your units nearby.

Building one spends the worker. It needs a land tile without a city, fortress or another such building. Borders are checked once a round: an outpost or tower inside foreign borders is destroyed; an airfield passes to the land's owner if it knows Flight, otherwise it is destroyed with its aircraft. A colony disappears as soon as any borders take its tile — yours included, but then the resource is yours anyway. An enemy land unit or a barbarian stepping onto a colony overruns it.

A colony is a quick way to get iron or horses beyond your borders, but without a road it is useless and without a guard it is short-lived.`},"concept:combat":{desc:`Combat runs in rounds until one side is out of hit points. Attack against defence, the fighters' experience and every bonus the defender enjoys decide the outcome.`,body:`Each round the attacker wins with a chance equal to its share of attack plus defence; the loser of the round drops one hit point. The fight goes on until one of the two dies. Terrain, rivers, fortifying, cities and walls raise defence — see [[concept:defense|defensive bonuses]]. A stack is defended by its best defender, and its defeat does not doom the others.

Seasoned units have more hit points: conscript, regular, veteran, elite. A victory may promote the winner, and an elite victory may bring a [[unit:leader|great leader]]. [[building:barracks|Barracks]] turn out veterans. An attacker faster than the defender may withdraw when the next lost round would be its last, and experienced troops get away more often.

The winner never advances onto the enemy tile: to take a city you have to walk in with a separate move. An [[unit:army|army]] fights with its best member at a time. Defenceless workers and settlers are captured, and some units drag the beaten off into slavery. Every attack and every loss adds to war weariness.

Tip: check the forecast before attacking — no amount of experience will save an archer who decided to storm musketmen on a hill.`,fact:`Wargames began as officer training: the Prussian Kriegsspiel of the 1820s was played on maps with dice, wooden blocks and umpires, and generals took it very seriously.`},"concept:corruption":{desc:`Part of a city's trade is lost to corruption and part of its shields to waste. The farther a city is from a centre and the further down your list of cities, the more it loses.`,body:`Corruption and waste depend on the government, on the distance to the nearest centre — the [[building:palace|palace]] or the [[wonder:forbidden_palace|Forbidden Palace]] — and on the city's place among all your cities ranked by distance: each one down the line loses more than the one before. How fast that share climbs is set by the optimal number of cities, which depends on the difficulty level and the civilization's traits. Without a palace corruption is at its worst, and anarchy takes everything.

Governments differ a lot: under [[gov:despotism|despotism]] distant cities give almost nothing, under [[gov:democracy|democracy]] losses are small, and under [[gov:communism|communism]] they are the same in every city wherever it stands.

A [[building:courthouse|courthouse]] and a [[building:police_station|police station]] cut corruption and waste in their city. A We Love the King Day halves them. Waste never takes a city's last shield.

Tip: a second centre at the far edge of the empire reaches further than a courthouse in every city. And if you do end up building courthouses everywhere, take comfort: at least someone in the country is working on corruption.`,fact:`The word “candidate” comes from the Latin candidatus, “clothed in white”: Roman office-seekers wore whitened togas to look spotless.`},"concept:culture":{desc:`Culture comes from temples, libraries, wonders and other buildings. It pushes borders out, shapes how neighbours see you and can win foreign cities over.`,body:`Each turn a city adds the culture of its buildings, and nothing while in disorder. A building that has stood for many centuries of the calendar gives twice as much, so an early temple keeps paying off for a very long time. A city's culture accumulates forever, and a civilization's culture is the sum of its cities; it also sets the civilization's cultural level.

Borders grow with a city's culture: a new city holds only its nearest tiles, then its whole work area, and later the border reaches further still. A city works only tiles inside its borders. A disputed tile goes to the nearest city, on a tie to the more cultured one, then to the older one. You always see your own land.

Culture matters in diplomacy too: neighbours respect cultured civilizations and may even stand in awe of them, and their cities defect to you more readily — see [[concept:flips|city flips]]. A captured city starts gathering its new owner's culture from zero, but the old culture returns if the city is retaken. Enormous culture can even bring a [[concept:victory|cultural victory]].

Tip: a [[building:temple|temple]] in a new city buys order and its first borders in one go.`,fact:`When Rome conquered Greece, Greek culture conquered Rome. As Horace put it, “captive Greece took captive her savage conqueror.”`},"concept:flips":{desc:`A city full of foreign citizens in the shadow of a foreign culture may defect to its neighbour on its own. The risk shows in advance; a garrison and a courthouse lower it.`,body:`Once a round every city checks whether it defects to another civilization. The chance rises with that civilization's citizens in it (resisters count double), with how many of its tiles lie inside that civilization's borders and with that civilization's culture compared to yours. Disorder doubles the risk, a We Love the King Day halves it. A nearby capital of your own protects a city, a nearby foreign one does the opposite. A capital never defects.

Every land unit in the city able to fight lowers the chance, and a [[building:courthouse|courthouse]] halves it. A city leaves with its buildings and people but loses whatever it was building; all your units inside are lost, and the new owner gets free defenders. Propaganda can take a city the same way — see [[concept:espionage|espionage]].

Captured cities are at the greatest risk: foreign citizens inside, foreign culture all around. In time content foreigners assimilate, faster under some governments than others.

The game shows the risk to your cities in advance. Tip: a city you cannot hold can be razed when you take it — but the world does not forget that.`,fact:`Berwick-upon-Tweed changed hands between England and Scotland more than a dozen times in the Middle Ages before settling on the English side.`},"concept:defense":{desc:`A defender gains bonuses from terrain, rivers, fortifying, fortresses and cities. They add up, so the right tile can matter more than a stronger unit.`,body:`Defensive strength is the unit's defence raised by the sum of all its bonuses. Almost every terrain gives something, hills give a lot and mountains the most. A fortified unit gets its own bonus, and inside a city it always counts as fortified. A river helps only when the attack comes across its bank, and never against bombardment or aircraft. A [[job:fortress|fortress]] and a [[job:barricade|barricade]] strengthen whoever stands in them.

Cities defend by size: a town gives nothing, a city gives a bonus, a metropolis twice as much. [[building:walls|Walls]] work only in a town and soak up land bombardment first; once the town grows into a city they stop working. A [[building:coastal_fortress|coastal fortress]] helps against attacks from the sea and fires at enemy ships sailing past.

If a city holds resisting citizens, its city bonuses vanish; terrain, river, fortress and the unit's own fortifying remain. Enemy walls can be seen from afar, the rest of a rival's buildings cannot.

Tip: the best defender in a stack does the defending, so make sure that is the one in the city. A fortified [[unit:spearman|spearman]] on a hill behind a river has ruined many a horseman's afternoon.`},"concept:difficulty":{desc:`The difficulty level decides how many citizens are born content and how big a head start the computer gets: cheaper builds, extra starting units, free upkeep.`,body:`Levels run from Chieftain to Sid. For every player the level sets how many citizens are born content, how many cities an empire holds before extra [[concept:corruption|corruption]] sets in, and how much harder your units hit barbarians. The higher the level, the sooner cities slide into [[concept:happiness|disorder]] and the more a sprawling empire costs.

The computer gets its own adjustments. On the lowest levels it pays even more than you for buildings, techs and growth; on the highest, much less. From Monarch up the AI starts with extra defenders and attackers, later with settlers and workers too, supports more units for free and sits through less [[gov:anarchy|Anarchy]]. Computers also trade with each other more readily than with you.

The level multiplies your [[concept:victory|score]] as well, so a win on a hard level counts for more.

Newcomers will be happiest on the lowest levels, where the AI gets no bonuses. On the top ones the hard part is surviving the early ages: the rival grows fast, and its early rush is dangerous.`,civ3:`The default level here is Monarch rather than Regent as in the original game (D69).`},"concept:diplomacy":{desc:`Treaties, trade and wars with other civilizations. Your reputation and your neighbours' attitude decide whether they trade with you or band together against you.`,body:`Civilizations are at peace from the moment they meet. That makes a war without cause a broken treaty: your reputation drops with everyone and the victim nurses a grudge for a long time. A cause can come, for example, from a refusal to withdraw troops: you may enter foreign borders, but the owner may demand that you leave. Silence in answer to a demand counts as refusal — diplomats call it a frank exchange of views.

At the table you trade techs, gold at once or per turn, [[concept:trade_network|resources]], maps, cities and embassies. Payments and resource deliveries run for a limited term, and a resource can be given only if your network reaches the partner's capital. You can ask "what would you want for this?" and receive a counteroffer.

At peace and with an embassy, agreements open up: an alliance against a common enemy, a right of passage (without it, foreign roads do not serve you), a mutual protection pact and an embargo. Breaking one costs reputation. Once the [[wonder:the_united_nations|United Nations]] stands, elections are held, and winning one is a diplomatic victory.

A neighbour's attitude grows from culture, military strength, reputation and grievances. A gift softens a grievance; a razed city is never forgiven, by its owner or by anyone watching.`,fact:`Envoys have been protected for thousands of years: Greek heralds were sacred, and the Mongols destroyed whole states that killed their ambassadors.`},"concept:espionage":{desc:`Espionage here is no unit but missions paid in gold through an embassy: investigating cities, stealing techs, propaganda, sabotage. A caught agent becomes a scandal.`,body:`It all starts with an embassy in the target civilization. Through it, diplomats investigate cities and try to steal techs in peacetime. In war the embassy is closed.

Serious operations need the [[wonder:intelligence_agency|Intelligence Agency]], a small wonder that [[tech:espionage|Espionage]] opens, and a spy planted through the embassy. The spy stays even in war. It can steal the world map or the war plans — showing you all of the target's troops for a turn — sabotage a city's production, expose an enemy spy, or spread propaganda until a city defects to you.

Each mission runs at one of three levels of care: the safer it is, the more it costs. The price grows with the distance between capitals. Under [[gov:communism|Communism]] and [[gov:fascism|Fascism]] agents are veterans, more successful and cheaper. [[gov:democracy|Democracy]] is immune to propaganda, a [[building:courthouse|Courthouse]] and a capital resist it, and a city in disorder gives in more easily.

Failure is an incident: a grievance and lost reputation, and in peacetime the target gains a cause for war. Investigating a city always works and goes unnoticed, so begin there.`},"concept:city_growth":{desc:`A city grows when it gathers more food than its citizens eat. Size brings more workers, but runs into limits that an aqueduct and a hospital lift.`,body:`Every citizen eats the same ration. The surplus goes into the city's food store; when the store is full a new citizen is born, and each growth step needs more food than the last. A shortage drains the store, and once it is empty the city loses a citizen. A city in disorder has no surplus — it can only starve.

Food comes from tiles: grassland, flood plains, coast, irrigated fields and resources such as [[resource:wheat|wheat]] and [[resource:fish|fish]]. A [[building:harbor|harbor]] adds food to sea tiles, and a [[building:granary|granary]] keeps part of the store after growth. A city works only tiles inside its civilization's borders.

Without an [[building:aqueduct|aqueduct]] a city cannot pass the first size limit; a river on the city tile or a lake next to it lifts it for free. A [[building:hospital|hospital]] lifts the second. A city at its limit keeps its store full and waits.

A [[unit:settler|settler]] or a [[unit:worker|worker]] takes citizens away, so a city only completes one when it has more people than it must give up. And growth is not always a blessing: every new citizen wants to be content too — see [[concept:happiness|happiness]].`,fact:`Today more than half of humanity lives in cities; around 1800 the share was well under a tenth.`},"concept:golden_age":{desc:`A season of plenty in which every tile already making shields or trade makes more. A civ gets one golden age per game, so when it starts matters.`,body:`A golden age begins in one of two ways. The first is the first victory of your unique unit, attacking or defending — a Roman [[unit:legionary|Legionary]], say; wins over barbarians do not count. The second is great wonders: the age starts once the wonders you built together cover both of your civilization's traits and the new one matches at least one. Egypt needs nothing more than the [[wonder:the_pyramids|Pyramids]]. Captured wonders do not help.

While it lasts, every tile that already yields shields or trade yields more of them. Food is untouched. Under [[gov:despotism|Despotism]] the bonus comes before the despot's penalty, so tiles already pressed against the cap gain nothing.

There is one age per game and it starts on its own; you cannot cancel or postpone it. If your unique unit arrives early while you have only a few cities, keep it out of fights for a while: a golden age spent on three villages is a golden age wasted. The best moment is a large empire with improved land and a government past despotism.`,fact:`In Abbasid Baghdad, from the eighth to the tenth century, a great translation movement put Greek science and philosophy into Arabic, and much of that learning later reached Europe through those translations.`},"concept:governments":{desc:`Your government sets the rules for the whole empire: corruption, army upkeep, how to hurry, how high taxes and science may go. Changing it means a spell of anarchy.`,body:`You start under [[gov:despotism|Despotism]], which takes a bite out of rich tiles and suffers heavy corruption. Techs open the others: [[gov:monarchy|Monarchy]], [[gov:feudalism|Feudalism]], [[gov:republic|Republic]], [[gov:democracy|Democracy]], [[gov:communism|Communism]] and [[gov:fascism|Fascism]].

They differ in almost everything: how much [[concept:corruption|corruption and waste]] distant cities suffer, how many units a city supports for free and what each extra one costs, whether troops calm the unhappy, whether you hurry with citizens or gold, how fast workers toil, and how much a war wears the people down. Republic and Democracy add trade to tiles that already make some but tolerate long wars poorly. Communism spreads corruption evenly, near cities and far alike.

Changing government means revolution: the empire falls into [[gov:anarchy|Anarchy]] for a few turns. Corruption eats nearly all trade, nothing can be hurried and workers dawdle, though buildings cost no upkeep. The length of anarchy is random; [[trait:religious|Religious]] civs get through it quickly and predictably.

A peaceful trading empire suits Republic or Democracy; a warring one, Monarchy, Communism or Fascism. Revolt soon after you learn the tech, while your empire is still small.`},"concept:leaders":{desc:`Great leaders rise from the victories of elite troops: they form armies and finish buildings at once. Scientific leaders come with discoveries and bring an age of science.`,body:`A [[unit:leader|Leader]] may appear after an elite unit beats another civilization, as long as you have no living leader. The chance is small; the [[wonder:heroic_epic|Heroic Epic]] raises it, and [[trait:militaristic|Militaristic]] civs get leaders twice as often. Wins over barbarians do not count.

In one of your cities a leader either forms an [[unit:army|Army]] or instantly completes the building or small wonder being built there — never a great wonder or a unit. An army holds a small squad, fights with its best living member at a time, and its hit points are its members' combined. Units that join never leave. Each army needs several cities of yours; the [[wonder:military_academy|Military Academy]] builds armies without a leader, and [[wonder:the_pentagon|the Pentagon]] adds a slot to each.

A [[unit:scientific_leader|Scientific Leader]] is born in your capital when you are the first in the world to research a tech; trades and huts do not count. It completes any building, great wonders included, starts an age of science in which every city makes more beakers for a while, or founds an [[building:academy|Academy]] in its city for good. It cannot form armies. [[trait:scientific|Scientific]] civs see more of them.

While a leader lives, no new one appears, so spend it rather than hoard it.`,fact:`Alexander the Great founded around twenty cities named Alexandria and, according to Plutarch, one named Bucephala after his horse.`},"concept:happiness":{desc:`Citizens are happy, content or unhappy. When the unhappy outnumber the happy, the city falls into civil disorder and stops working for the empire.`,body:`At first only a few citizens are content — how many depends on the [[concept:difficulty|difficulty level]] — and the rest are unhappy. War weariness, compatriots of a hostile power, forced labour and the draft make things worse. Improvements such as the [[building:temple|temple]], [[building:colosseum|colosseum]] and [[building:cathedral|cathedral]] make them better, as do wonders, martial law under some governments, luxury resources on the trade network and the luxury rate. Entertainers work no tiles but cheer the city up.

When the unhappy outnumber the happy, disorder breaks out: shields go nowhere, there is no tax, science or culture, and the food store does not grow. The governor turns citizens into entertainers until order returns.

When a large enough city has no unhappy citizens at all and at least as many happy ones as the rest, it celebrates a We Love the King Day. From the next turn it suffers less corruption and waste, is harder to win over, and under some governments its tiles yield more.

A [[building:marketplace|marketplace]] strengthens luxuries from resources: each new kind cheers more than the last. Tip: a couple of luxuries on the trade network often beat one more temple.`,fact:`Jefferson’s “life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness” is often compared with John Locke’s trio of “life, liberty and estate,” though how much he borrowed from Locke is still debated.`},"concept:naval_air":{desc:`Fleets carry troops and shell the shore; aircraft strike from afar. Both need bases — ports, cities, airfields, carriers — and a sense of where the enemy keeps its anti-air.`,body:`A ship may sail any water, but early vessels — the [[unit:galley|Galley]], [[unit:curragh|Curragh]] and [[unit:caravel|Caravel]] — can sink with their cargo if they end a turn in unsafe water. [[wonder:the_great_lighthouse|The Great Lighthouse]] removes the risk at sea, and [[trait:seafaring|Seafaring]] civs sink less often. Only amphibious units such as [[unit:marine|Marines]] can attack straight from a deck. A [[building:coastal_fortress|Coastal Fortress]] shields a city from the sea and fires on enemy ships sailing past.

An aircraft is based in your city, on an [[job:airfield|airfield]] or on a [[unit:carrier|Carrier]], strikes within its operational range and rebases twice as far. It may be shot down first: each flak unit or [[building:sam_missile_battery|SAM battery]] at the target and each enemy fighter nearby raises the chance, and stealth aircraft are hit less often. A [[unit:helicopter|Helicopter]] drops infantry within its range; paratroopers jump from your cities.

Nuclear strikes open with [[wonder:the_manhattan_project|the Manhattan Project]]: one kills the troops on and around the target, halves a city and pollutes the land. [[wonder:strategic_missile_defense|Strategic Missile Defense]] may stop it.

Knock out the anti-air before you bomb: a lost bomber costs more than a couple of extra turns.`,civ3:`Air power is simpler here: there are no separate recon or air superiority missions, and interception is passive — the chance grows with the anti-air sources around the target, up to a cap (D42).`},"concept:pollution":{desc:`Industry and big cities foul the land around them, and a polluted tile stops yielding anything. If the world gets dirty enough, global warming sets in.`,body:`Each turn a city may pollute a random land tile in its work area. The chance grows with its pollution points. Those come from industrial buildings — the [[building:factory|Factory]], [[building:coal_plant|Coal Plant]], [[wonder:iron_works|Iron Works]], [[building:manufacturing_plant|Manufacturing Plant]] and others — and from the population of a large city, but only after [[tech:industrialization|Industrialization]]. Nuclear strikes and volcanic lava leave pollution too.

A polluted tile gives no food, shields or trade, and its citizen walks off it, until a [[unit:worker|Worker]] [[job:clean|cleans it]]. [[building:mass_transit_system|Mass Transit]] removes the pollution caused by citizens, a [[building:recycling_center|Recycling Center]] most of what buildings cause. The [[building:hydro_plant|Hydro]], [[building:solar_plant|Solar]] and [[building:nuclear_plant|Nuclear]] plants add at least what a coal plant adds without fouling anything themselves.

Once polluted land in the world passes a threshold, global warming may strike each round: land all over the world degrades — grassland to plains, plains to desert, forests and jungles vanish. Everyone pays, not just the polluters.

Keep spare workers in industrial cities for cleanup and build clean plants where you can. Warming is permanent; the cleanup is not.`,civ3:`Warming here never turns marsh into coast: land is never flooded, although the original game's terrain table allows it (D67).`,fact:`Light pollution now hides the Milky Way from most people in Europe and North America.`},"concept:production":{desc:`Shields from worked tiles build units, improvements and wonders. You can hurry a build with gold, and under some governments with your citizens' labour.`,body:`Each turn a city's shields, less waste (see [[concept:corruption|corruption and waste]]), go into the current project. Switching projects is free — the stored shields move over whole — but anything beyond the cost is lost on completion, so don't overpay on the last turn. After rushing with gold you cannot switch to a wonder that turn. A unit is built again and again until the queue says otherwise; improvements and wonders are built once. A city in disorder finishes nothing. Hills, forests and mines give the most shields, and a [[building:factory|factory]] and a power plant multiply the total.

Hurrying with gold is open under almost any government: the price grows faster than the shortfall, a unit costs twice as much as an improvement, and an empty box doubles it again — put at least one turn of work in first. Under [[gov:despotism|despotism]] and a few other regimes you can whip citizens into work instead: the city loses people, and the resentment of forced labour lingers. What you buy appears at the start of next turn. No wonder can be bought; a [[unit:leader|great leader]] completes anything except a great wonder, and a [[unit:scientific_leader|scientific leader]] even that. A city with resisters cannot hurry at all.

With nothing to build, a city can produce wealth, turning part of its shields into gold. Under mobilization cities build only troops and military improvements, but faster.

Tip: the queue spares you the eternal "what next?", but look it over after every discovery.`,fact:`At its height the Venetian Arsenal is said to have assembled a whole galley in a single day, moving hulls along a canal past workshops that added each part in turn.`},"concept:speed":{desc:`Two paces of one game. Sigma is the main mode: all of history in an evening or two. Classic is the full campaign, true to the original. The pace is chosen when a game is set up.`,body:`Sigma does not cut the game down; it compresses time. Every era, technology, wonder and kind of victory is still there, but buildings, wonders and discoveries cost less, cities grow faster, workers finish sooner, and anarchy, the [[concept:golden_age|golden age]] and deals are shorter. Units get cheaper less than buildings, so armies are leaner and every regiment counts. [[concept:culture|Culture]] thresholds are lower, and the [[concept:space_race|space race]] is cheaper than anything else — so the ship can still take off in time. Every people, yours and your rivals’ alike, sets out with the tribe’s stock: the first city starts with grain in store, and the first warrior, worker and settler come together faster than a small village could manage.

What decides things on the map stays honest: movement, combat, healing, corruption and upkeep work as in Classic. Gold keeps its worth, too: rushing a build costs what Classic charges for the same amount of work.

Only in Sigma can a captured city be kept as a puppet: it builds on its own, shares its trade and asks for no attention, and you may annex it whenever you like. When the allotted turns run out the game ends; if nobody has won by then, the [[concept:victory|score]] decides.

Classic is the full campaign, tens of hours long, for rulers in no hurry. As rulers should be.`,civ3:`Civ3 has a single pace; Sigma and its puppet cities are our own rule.`},"concept:improvements":{desc:`Workers turn wild land into farmland: irrigation adds food, mines add shields, roads add trade and speed. Improvements decide how much every citizen brings in.`,body:`[[unit:worker|Workers]] build [[job:irrigate|irrigation]], [[job:mine|mines]] and [[job:road|roads]]. Irrigation needs fresh water: a river on the tile, a lake next to it or a neighbouring irrigated tile, so canals spread in chains; sea water won't do until [[tech:electricity|Electricity]]. Irrigation and a mine cannot share a tile: the new job removes the old one.

A road adds trade on many tiles and makes each step a third as costly; roads on river tiles wait for [[tech:engineering|Engineering]]. A [[job:railroad|railroad]] costs no movement at all and adds food to irrigation and shields to a mine. [[job:clear|Clearing]] turns forest, jungle and marsh into open land; [[job:plant|planting]] does the reverse.

Later come [[job:fortress|fortresses]], [[job:barricade|barricades]] and terrain buildings such as outposts and colonies ([[concept:colonies|Colonies and Outposts]]). Work speed depends on the terrain, the government — faster under [[gov:democracy|Democracy]], slower in anarchy — and the [[trait:industrious|Industrious]] trait; several workers on one tile pool their effort.

A city works only tiles inside its [[concept:culture|borders]], so improve your own land. Irrigate for growth first, then mine the hills. A [[concept:pollution|polluted]] tile yields nothing, so [[job:clean|clean it up]] at once.`},"concept:space_race":{desc:`The scientific road to victory: assemble a spaceship from parts and be the first to send it to the stars. While it flies, hold your capital at any cost.`,body:`The race opens with [[tech:space_flight|Space Flight]] and the [[wonder:apollo_program|Apollo Program]], a small wonder. It lets only its own builder make parts, so every contender needs an Apollo of its own. The parts are small wonders too: the [[wonder:ss_engine|Engine]], [[wonder:ss_cockpit|Cockpit]], [[wonder:ss_exterior_casing|Exterior Casing]] and the rest. Each needs its own tech, up to [[tech:the_laser|the Laser]] and [[tech:superconductor|Superconductor]].

When the last part is done, the ship launches by itself at the start of your turn. The flight takes several turns; when it arrives you win. If an enemy takes your capital in the meantime, the ship is lost.

The parts are expensive, so spread them across your most productive cities and keep researching the techs some of them need. Fortify the capital before launch: your rivals have nothing left to lose, and they know it. The space victory can be switched off when a game is created; parts can still be built then, but the ship never lifts off.`,fact:`Many space-race “spin-offs” are myths: NASA did not invent Velcro or the orange drink Tang, though it made both famous.`},"concept:trade_network":{desc:`Roads, rivers and harbors link your cities into a network that shares resources. Without a link to the source there are no iron-hungry units and no joy from silks.`,body:`A city gets a resource if it lies inside your borders (or under your [[job:colony|colony]]) and a road links it to the city. Rivers count as roads, and [[building:harbor|harbors]] connect coastal cities over water: along the coast at once, across the sea after [[tech:astronomy|Astronomy]], across the ocean after [[tech:navigation|Navigation]]. [[building:airport|Airports]] link cities through the air, over any sea or foreign land. The network runs through the land of neighbours at peace with you but breaks on enemy territory.

Bonus resources simply improve their tile. Luxuries such as [[resource:wines|wine]] or [[resource:silks|silks]] cheer every linked city, more so with a marketplace. Strategic ones — [[resource:horses|horses]], [[resource:iron|iron]], [[resource:oil|oil]] — are needed by many units and improvements. A resource shows up only once you know the right tech, and it may run dry and reappear somewhere else.

A spare copy can be traded to another civilization if your networks connect the two capitals. A pillaged road cuts a resource off at once, and losing a city or a shift of borders can tear the whole network apart.

Tip: a single road to a colony on a distant hill sometimes decides a war.`,fact:`The Silk Road was never a single road but a web of routes, and few traders ever traveled its whole length: goods passed from hand to hand along the way.`},"concept:commerce":{desc:`Trade from tiles is split between science, taxes and luxuries at rates you set. The ceiling for each rate depends on your government.`,body:`Trade comes from rivers, roads, water and some resources; the [[gov:republic|Republic]] and democracy add more on tiles that already have some. After [[concept:corruption|corruption]] the rest is split by your rates: science drives research, taxes fill the treasury, luxuries cheer the citizens. No rate may exceed the government's limit. Tax collectors and scientists add their share, and the [[building:library|library]], [[building:marketplace|marketplace]] and their successors multiply the result.

The treasury pays upkeep for improvements and for units beyond the free allowance. When gold runs out, the state sells an improvement and surplus troops are disbanded. Anarchy brings taxes and science to a halt.

A tech gets cheaper the more of the civilizations you have met already know it. Research cannot finish faster than a set minimum, nor does it drag on forever: even modest science will get there eventually. Once everything is known, science goes into future techs, which add to your score.

Tip: gold is handy for hurrying, but early in the game science is usually worth more than coins.`,civ3:`Late-era techs are noticeably cheaper here than in the original, because our game is built for fewer turns (D39).`,fact:`The word “tariff” came into English through Italian from the Arabic ta‘rif, “notification.”`},"concept:turns":{desc:`The game is played in turns: civilizations take theirs one after another. Movement points decide how far a unit gets in a turn and what it still has time to do.`,body:`A turn is a round in which every civilization in turn commands its forces. When yours begins, units get their full movement points, cities grow and build, gold and research come in. A unit built this turn moves next turn. The calendar turns turns into years — centuries apiece in antiquity, mere years near the end — and the game itself has a turn limit, set by its pace: [[concept:speed|Sigma or Classic]].

A step onto grassland, plains or desert costs one point; forest, hills and marsh cost more; mountains and jungle cost the most. A [[job:road|road]] cuts a step to a third of a point, a [[job:railroad|railroad]] makes it free, and a city tile counts as a road. Wheeled units enter mountains, jungle and marsh only along a road. A unit with any movement left may always take one more step — no dice involved.

Attacking costs one point, and a unit with points to spare can keep moving afterwards. A unit that sits out a whole turn heals: faster in your own city, fully in one turn with [[building:barracks|barracks]] there (a harbor for ships, an airport for aircraft); inside another civ's borders wounds do not mend. A go-to order walks a unit along the best path over as many turns as it takes.

Tip: build roads between your cities first — they speed up armies and the [[concept:trade_network|trade network]] alike.`,civ3:`Our game is almost half as long as the original, so late-era techs cost less while the proportions within each era stay the same (D39).`,fact:`Go, one of the oldest board games still played, has more possible positions than there are atoms in the observable universe — and it is still played one turn at a time.`},"concept:victory":{desc:`A game is won by conquest, domination, space, diplomacy or culture. If nobody gets there, the best score wins when the last turn comes.`,body:`Conquest means being the last civilization standing: a civ with no cities and no settlers is out. Domination means holding the overwhelming share of the world's land within your borders and as large a share of its people. A [[concept:space_race|space victory]] means your ship arriving. A diplomatic one, winning a [[wonder:the_united_nations|United Nations]] election. A cultural one, amassing vast [[concept:culture|culture]] in one city or across the civilization, far ahead of the runner-up.

Each turn's score counts the tiles within your borders (ocean excluded), happy and content citizens and future techs, multiplied by the [[concept:difficulty|difficulty level]]. The final score is the average over all turns, so steady play beats a late sprint. An early win of any kind adds a bonus for every year left.

Any kind of victory can be switched off when a game is created; the turn limit always applies. You may also retire: your score freezes, and if other humans are still playing, the computer takes over your civilization.

Watch the leader on the graph: when a rival nears domination, the others gladly gang up on it — and on you, if the leader is you.`,civ3:`Games are shorter than in the original: the turn limit comes much sooner, and late-era techs are cheaper so that a game still reaches them (D39).`,fact:`The word “trophy” comes from the Greek tropaion, a monument raised on the spot where the enemy turned and fled.`},"concept:zoc":{desc:`Some units, and any garrison in a fortress, exert a zone of control: enemies passing by draw their fire. It never blocks movement, but it costs hit points.`,body:`Zones of control belong to units with that ability — [[unit:cavalry|cavalry]], tanks, [[unit:army|armies]], the [[unit:aegis_cruiser|AEGIS cruiser]] and a few others — and to any land unit able to fight that stands in a [[job:fortress|fortress]] or barricade. A zone works only against civilizations you are at war with, and only within its own element: land units shoot at land units, ships at ships.

The shot comes when an enemy steps from one tile next to the shooter onto another such tile while the shooter can see it. Its strength is the unit's attack (or its bombard strength) against the target's defence; a hit takes one hit point but never the last, and the move goes on. Each shooter fires at most once a turn, and that shot is shared with defensive bombardment. Workers and settlers are left alone.

A unit that merely walks up to the shooter or walks away from it draws no fire. When plotting a route the game counts the zones it knows of and goes around them where it can.

Tip: a fortress with cavalry at a crossroads turns someone else's march into a slow loss of health.`},"civ:americans":{desc:`A scout from turn one and quick workers let America spread wide and develop land fast. Its [[unit:f_15|signature jet fighter]] arrives only at the very end, a plan measured in millennia.`,history:`The United States declared independence from Britain in 1776 and adopted in 1787 a constitution that is still in force. The Civil War of 1861–1865, under Abraham Lincoln, preserved the Union and ended slavery. By the 20th century the country was the largest industrial power, and in 1969 its astronauts became the first people to walk on the Moon.`,fact:`When the British burned the Capitol in 1814, the young Library of Congress went up with it. Thomas Jefferson sold Congress his own library of more than six thousand volumes to start again.`},"civ:arabs":{desc:`An early scout and cheap temples help Arabia claim land fast and keep cities calm. The [[unit:ansar_warrior|Ansar Warrior]] is a swift medieval rider, ideal for raids deep into enemy land.`,history:`In the 7th century the tribes of the Arabian Peninsula were united under Islam, and Abu Bakr became the first caliph in 632. Within a century the caliphate stretched from Spain to Central Asia. In the Baghdad of Harun al-Rashid and his Abbasid heirs the House of Wisdom translated Greek philosophy and advanced algebra, astronomy and medicine, knowledge that later reached Europe.`,fact:`Tradition credits a woman, Fatima al-Fihri, with founding the al-Qarawiyyin mosque in Fez in 859. It grew into one of the oldest centers of learning in the world.`},"civ:aztecs":{desc:`Well-fed cities and cheap barracks get the Aztecs into the field early. The fast [[unit:jaguar_warrior|Jaguar Warrior]] is available from the start, good for scouting and the first raids.`,history:`The Mexica founded Tenochtitlan on an island in Lake Texcoco around 1325. The Triple Alliance of 1428 made them masters of central Mexico, and their capital, with causeways, canals and chinampa gardens, became one of the largest cities on Earth. The empire fell to the Spanish and their Indigenous allies in 1521, yet Nahuatl is still spoken by well over a million people.`,fact:`Aztec education was unusually broad for its time: boys and girls alike attended school, learning history, songs, religion and, for boys, the arts of war.`},"civ:babylonians":{desc:`Cheap temples and libraries plus a free tech each era make Babylon a culture and science power. The [[unit:bowman|Bowman]] holds a line better than the standard archer.`,history:`Babylon on the Euphrates rose under Hammurabi in the 18th century BC; his law code, carved on a stele, is among the oldest to survive. Babylonian astronomers kept records of the sky for centuries, and their base-sixty counting lives on in our minutes and degrees. The city flourished again under Nebuchadnezzar II before Cyrus the Great took it in 539 BC.`,fact:`A Babylonian clay tablet known as Plimpton 322 holds a table of Pythagorean triples, written more than a thousand years before Pythagoras was born.`},"civ:byzantines":{desc:`A learned seafaring power with cheap libraries and harbors and faster ships. Unlike a plain galley, the [[unit:dromon|Dromon]] can bombard coasts and rival ships.`,history:`The Eastern Roman Empire, ruled from Constantinople, founded in 330, outlived the Western empire by almost a thousand years. Under Justinian and Theodora it codified Roman law and raised the Hagia Sophia. It preserved classical texts and guarded the secret of Greek fire until the city fell in 1453, leaving a deep legacy to the Orthodox world.`,fact:`Around 552, two monks are said to have smuggled silkworm eggs to Constantinople inside hollow canes, letting Byzantium raise its own silk instead of buying it through Persia.`},"civ:carthaginians":{desc:`Quick workers and coastal trade let Carthage build rich harbor cities. The [[unit:numidian_mercenary|Numidian Mercenary]] is a sturdy defender for the early frontier.`,history:`By tradition, Phoenicians from Tyre founded Carthage in 814 BC. It came to dominate the western Mediterranean, and its merchants sailed as far as Britain and West Africa. In the Punic Wars Hannibal crossed the Alps and crushed Rome at Cannae in 216 BC, but Rome destroyed the city in 146 BC.`,fact:`The Carthaginian Mago wrote a farming manual so respected that, after Carthage fell, the Roman Senate ordered it translated into Latin.`},"civ:celts":{desc:`Well-fed cities, cheap temples and short anarchy when changing government. The [[unit:gallic_swordsman|Gallic Swordsman]] outpaces the regular swordsman, made for early raids.`,history:`By the middle of the first millennium BC, Celtic peoples lived from Ireland and Gaul to the Danube and Anatolia. Around 390 BC Gauls under Brennus sacked Rome. Celtic smiths were famed for metalwork and the flowing La Tène style. Caesar conquered Gaul, yet Celtic languages such as Irish, Welsh and Breton are still spoken.`,fact:`Celtic languages are still alive: Welsh, Irish, Scottish Gaelic and Breton are spoken today, and Cornish and Manx have been brought back from the brink.`},"civ:chinese":{desc:`Cheap military buildings and fast workers make China equally strong at building and at war. The [[unit:rider|Rider]] outpaces the knight and strikes first.`,history:`Chinese civilization took shape along the Yellow River, and in 221 BC the state of Qin unified the land. Under the Han and later dynasties came paper, the compass, printing and gunpowder, while civil-service exams chose officials by learning. After many dynasties, China became the People's Republic in 1949.`,fact:`Chinese is the oldest writing system in continuous use: characters on Shang dynasty oracle bones, more than three thousand years old, can still be partly read today.`},"civ:egyptians":{desc:`Cheap temples and fast workers help Egypt tame river valleys and raise wonders early. The [[unit:war_chariot|War Chariot]] hits noticeably harder than a standard chariot.`,history:`Around 3100 BC Upper and Lower Egypt were united into one kingdom along the Nile. The pyramids of Giza, the temples of Karnak and the tombs of the Valley of the Kings have outlasted millennia. The Ptolemaic line ended with Cleopatra VII in 30 BC. Champollion deciphered the hieroglyphs in 1822, opening this history to us again.`,fact:`The Rosetta Stone carries one decree in three scripts — hieroglyphic, demotic and Greek — and with its help Jean-François Champollion announced the decipherment of hieroglyphs in 1822.`},"civ:english":{desc:`Trade and sea power: rich coastal cities, cheap markets and harbors. The [[unit:man_o_war|Man-O-War]] outguns the frigate and rules the waves for a long stretch.`,history:`The Anglo-Saxon kingdoms were united as England in the 10th century. Magna Carta in 1215 and the rise of Parliament set limits on royal power. Under Elizabeth I the country turned back the Armada in 1588 and saw the theater of Shakespeare flourish. England later became the cradle of the Industrial Revolution, and its language spread across the globe.`,fact:`By tradition, at least six ravens live at the Tower of London, because legend says the kingdom will fall if they ever leave.`},"civ:french":{desc:`Rich, productive cities: cheap markets, fast workers and lower corruption. The [[unit:musketeer|Musketeer]] is one of the finest defenders of the gunpowder age.`,history:`The Frankish kingdom of Clovis became the core of France, and Charlemagne was crowned emperor in 800. In 1429 Joan of Arc lifted the siege of Orléans. The Versailles of Louis XIV set the tone for Europe, and the Revolution of 1789 with its Declaration of the Rights of Man reshaped politics worldwide.`,fact:`Revolutionary France also tried decimal time: a day of ten hours, each of a hundred minutes. The clocks were made, but the experiment lasted barely two years.`},"civ:germans":{desc:`A scientific military power: cheap barracks and libraries, frequent promotions. The [[unit:panzer|Panzer]] outruns the regular tank and breaks a front in a single turn.`,history:`For centuries the German lands belonged to the Holy Roman Empire. There, around 1450, Gutenberg began printing books, and in 1517 Luther started the Reformation. Bismarck forged a unified state in 1871. After the devastating wars of the 20th century, Germany reunified in 1990 and became a pillar of European unity.`,fact:`Friedrich Fröbel opened the first kindergarten — a “children’s garden” — in Germany around 1840, and English kept the German word.`},"civ:greeks":{desc:`Trade and science: cheap markets and libraries and a free tech each era. The [[unit:hoplite|Hoplite]] is a sturdy early defender that blunts neighbors' raids.`,history:`The Greek city-states gave the world the democracy of 5th-century BC Athens, the philosophy of Socrates, Plato and Aristotle, drama and the Olympic Games. Alexander the Great marched as far as India, spreading Greek language and culture from Egypt to Bactria. The Hellenic legacy underlies much of Western science and art.`,fact:`The Antikythera mechanism, recovered from a shipwreck in 1901, is an ancient Greek geared device that predicted eclipses and the motions of the Sun and Moon. Nothing so intricate is known for more than a thousand years afterward.`},"civ:hittites":{desc:`An early scout and strong trade let the Hittites spread fast and run a wide realm with little corruption. The [[unit:three_man_chariot|Three-Man Chariot]] is sturdier than the standard one.`,history:`From their capital Hattusa, the Hittites ruled Anatolia in the second millennium BC. Their chariots met Ramesses II at Kadesh around 1274 BC, and the two powers later signed one of the earliest known peace treaties. Hittite is the oldest written Indo-European language. The kingdom collapsed around 1180 BC.`,fact:`Hittite was deciphered in 1915 when Bedřich Hrozný read a line that meant “now you will eat bread and drink water” and noticed that its word watar looked a great deal like “water.”`},"civ:inca":{desc:`An early scout and well-fed cities help the Inca settle fast. The [[unit:chasqui_scout|Chasqui Scout]] crosses mountains with ease and finds good city sites before anyone else.`,history:`Tawantinsuyu, the Inca Empire, grew from Cusco under Pachacuti after 1438 and stretched some 4,000 km along the Andes. Roads with rope bridges carried chasqui runners, and records were kept on knotted quipu cords. The Spanish conquered the empire in the 1530s, but Quechua remains the native language of millions.`,fact:`Machu Picchu became world-famous only in 1911, though local farmers had always known it and Spanish colonial documents mention the site.`},"civ:indians":{desc:`Trade and faith: cheap markets and temples, low corruption and short anarchy. The [[unit:war_elephant|War Elephant]] needs neither horses nor iron, a valuable medieval asset.`,history:`The Indus Valley civilization built planned cities as early as 2600 BC. The Maurya Empire under Ashoka and the Gupta age gave the world a Buddhist legacy, decimal numbers with zero and classical literature. After centuries of Mughal and then British rule, India won independence in 1947 and became the world's largest democracy.`,fact:`English owes a long list of everyday words to Indian languages: shampoo, bungalow, jungle, pajamas, loot and cot among them.`},"civ:iroquois":{desc:`Well-fed, wealthy cities with cheap markets and aqueducts. The [[unit:mounted_warrior|Mounted Warrior]] strikes harder than the regular horseman, made for an early offensive.`,history:`The Haudenosaunee are a league of the Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga and Seneca nations, joined by the Tuscarora in 1722. Tradition holds that the Peacemaker and Hiawatha united them under the Great Law of Peace. The Confederacy was a major power in northeastern North America for centuries and endures today.`,fact:`Mohawk ironworkers from Kahnawake and Akwesasne helped raise many of New York’s skyscrapers, walking narrow steel beams high above the streets.`},"civ:japanese":{desc:`Cheap barracks and temples, frequent promotions and short anarchy. The [[unit:samurai|Samurai]] is a knight who needs no horse, sturdy both in attack and in defense.`,history:`A unified Japanese state took shape by the 7th century, and the Heian era produced The Tale of Genji. From the 12th century shoguns and the samurai class ruled the land. The Tokugawa shogunate, founded in 1603, brought two and a half centuries of peace. After the Meiji Restoration of 1868 Japan rapidly industrialized.`,fact:`Japanese railways are so punctual that a company once issued a formal apology for a train leaving a station twenty seconds early.`},"civ:koreans":{desc:`Trade and science: rich cities, cheap libraries and a free tech each era. The [[unit:hwacha|Hwach'a]] pounds city defenders like a cannon but needs no iron.`,history:`Wang Kon founded the Goryeo state in 918, and the name Korea comes from it. Goryeo printed the Jikji in 1377, the oldest surviving book made with movable metal type. The Joseon dynasty ruled from 1392, and in the 15th century King Sejong created the Hangul alphabet.`,fact:`South Korea celebrates Hangul Day, a national holiday in honor of an alphabet created in the fifteenth century so that ordinary people could learn to read.`},"civ:maya":{desc:`Well-fed, productive cities and fast workers give the Maya a solid realm. The [[unit:javelin_thrower|Javelin Thrower]] captures defeated foes and turns them into workers.`,history:`Maya cities such as Tikal, Copán and Palenque flourished in the Classic period, roughly AD 250–900. The Maya developed a full writing system and a precise calendar, used the concept of zero and tracked Venus from their observatories. Many southern cities emptied by the 10th century, but millions of Maya live in Mexico and Guatemala today.`,fact:`The Maya never vanished: millions of people in Mexico, Guatemala and Belize speak Mayan languages today.`},"civ:mongols":{desc:`An early scout and cheap barracks let the Mongols expand fast and fight early. The [[unit:keshik|Keshik]] crosses hills and mountains easily, striking where no one expects it.`,history:`In 1206 an assembly of the steppe proclaimed Temujin as Genghis Khan. His heirs built the largest contiguous land empire in history, from Korea to Hungary. Under Mongol rule the Silk Road revived, linking China, Persia and Europe, and Kublai Khan founded the Yuan dynasty in China.`,fact:`A later saying held that under Mongol rule a maiden carrying a golden plate could cross the empire unharmed. It is an exaggeration, but the empire did protect its trade roads remarkably well.`},"civ:dutch":{desc:`Well-fed coastal cities, cheap aqueducts and harbors and faster ships. The [[unit:swiss_mercenary|Swiss Mercenary]] is a stubborn medieval defender.`,history:`In 1568 William of Orange led the Dutch revolt against Spain, and in 1648 the Republic of the United Provinces won recognition. The East India Company, founded in 1602, traded from Japan to the Caribbean. The Golden Age produced Rembrandt and Vermeer, while polders reclaimed whole provinces from the sea.`,fact:`New York was once New Amsterdam, and Dutch left traces in American English: “cookie,” “boss,” “stoop,” “Santa Claus” and, quite possibly, “Yankee.”`},"civ:ottomans":{desc:`A learned, hard-working power with cheap libraries and fast workers. The [[unit:sipahi|Sipahi]] is the strongest cavalry of its age, hitting harder than the standard kind.`,history:`Around 1299 Osman I founded a principality in Anatolia. In 1453 Mehmed II took Constantinople, which became Istanbul. Under Suleiman the Magnificent in the 16th century the empire stretched from Hungary to Yemen, and the architect Sinan built his great mosques. The empire ended in 1922, and the Republic of Turkey was proclaimed in 1923.`,fact:`Tulips came to Western Europe from the Ottoman Empire, and the word “tulip” is thought to come from the Turkish for “turban,” which the flower resembles.`},"civ:persians":{desc:`Science and industry: cheap libraries, fast workers and a free tech each era. The [[unit:immortals|Immortals]] are the hardest-hitting infantry of the ancient world.`,history:`Cyrus the Great founded the Achaemenid Empire around 550 BC, the first to reach from the Aegean to the Indus. Darius I built the Royal Road and Persepolis, and the Immortals were an elite corps of ten thousand. Alexander conquered the empire, but Persian culture carried on under the Parthians, the Sasanians and beyond.`,fact:`“Neither snow nor rain nor heat nor gloom of night” — the unofficial motto of the US Postal Service paraphrases Herodotus’ praise of the Persian royal couriers.`},"civ:portuguese":{desc:`An early scout and faster ships make Portugal the first to chart seas and distant shores. The [[unit:carrack|Carrack]] outfights the caravel and can hold its own at sea.`,history:`In the 15th century Prince Henry the Navigator sponsored voyages down the African coast. Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488, Vasco da Gama reached India in 1498, and Cabral landed in Brazil in 1500. Portuguese trade routes linked Europe, Africa, Asia and the Americas, and Portuguese is spoken on all of those continents today.`,fact:`Far more people speak Portuguese in Brazil than in Portugal — about twenty times as many.`},"civ:romans":{desc:`Cheap barracks, frequent promotions and rich cities. The [[unit:legionary|Legionary]] is tougher than a regular swordsman, equally good at taking cities and holding them.`,history:`Rome grew from a town on the Tiber into a republic in 509 BC and an empire under Augustus in 27 BC. Legions, roads and aqueducts bound the Mediterranean together, and Roman law and Latin became foundations of European culture. The Western empire fell in 476, while the Eastern half lived on as Byzantium.`,fact:`Romans loved fast food: thermopolia, street counters selling hot dishes from jars set into the stone, have been found all over Pompeii.`},"civ:russians":{desc:`An early scout and a free tech each era let Russia spread wide and keep up in science. The [[unit:cossack|Cossack]] is cavalry that can attack several times per turn.`,history:`The Russian state traces its roots to the Rus' of the 9th century, later centered on Moscow. Peter the Great built St. Petersburg and a navy in the early 18th century, and Catherine the Great extended the empire to the Black Sea. Russia gave the world Tolstoy, Dostoevsky and Tchaikovsky, and in 1961 Yuri Gagarin became the first human in space.`,fact:`Lake Baikal, the world’s deepest lake, holds roughly a fifth of all the unfrozen fresh surface water on Earth.`},"civ:vikings":{desc:`Cheap barracks, faster ships and coastal trade. The [[unit:berserk|Berserk]] attacks straight from a ship, built for sudden landings on a hostile shore.`,history:`The Viking Age ran roughly from 793 to 1066. Norse seafarers raided and traded from Ireland to Constantinople and the Caspian, settled Iceland and reached North America around 1000. Iceland's Althing, founded in 930, is among the oldest parliaments, and the sagas are treasures of medieval literature.`,fact:`Iceland’s parliament, the Althing, founded around 930, is among the oldest parliaments in the world still at work.`},"civ:spanish":{desc:`Faith and sea power: cheap temples and harbors, faster ships. The [[unit:conquistador|Conquistador]] crosses any terrain as if on roads, the ideal explorer for a new continent.`,history:`The marriage of Isabella of Castile and Ferdinand of Aragon united Spain, and 1492 saw both the end of the Reconquista and Columbus's landfall in the Americas. In the 16th century Spain built a global empire from the Americas to the Philippines. Its Golden Age produced Cervantes and Velázquez, and Spanish is now the native tongue of hundreds of millions.`,fact:`Don Quixote, published in two parts in 1605 and 1615, is often called the first modern novel, and “tilting at windmills” comes straight from it.`},"civ:sumerians":{desc:`Well-fed cities, cheap libraries and a free tech each era. The [[unit:enkidu_warrior|Enkidu Warrior]] guards the first cities more reliably than the ordinary warrior.`,history:`The Sumerian cities of Mesopotamia, such as Uruk, Ur and Lagash, arose in the 4th millennium BC. Around 3200 BC cuneiform appeared there, one of the first writing systems. The Sumerians are credited with early wheels, irrigation networks and the Epic of Gilgamesh, and their culture shaped Akkad, Babylon and Assyria.`,fact:`The Sumerians brewed beer and left a hymn to Ninkasi, goddess of brewing, that doubles as a recipe.`},"civ:zulu":{desc:`An early scout and cheap barracks let the Zulu expand fast and fight early. The swift [[unit:impi|Impi]] replaces the spearman and can plug any gap in the line.`,history:`Under Shaka, from 1816 to 1828, a small Zulu chiefdom became the strongest state in southeastern Africa. Its army, organized into amabutho regiments, was famed for discipline and speed. In 1879 the Zulu defeated a British force at Isandlwana, though they lost the war. The Zulu are today one of South Africa's largest peoples and still have a king.`,fact:`The Zulu name for the Drakensberg mountains, uKhahlamba, means “barrier of spears.”`},"trait:agricultural":{desc:`For civilizations that put people first: city centers and irrigated desert yield extra food, and aqueducts come cheap. Your cities simply outgrow the neighbors'.`,fact:`Judging by their skeletons, early farmers were often shorter and less healthy than the hunter-gatherers before them: farming fed more people, not necessarily better.`},"trait:commercial":{desc:`For a wealthy realm: large cities bring in more commerce, corruption runs lower, and markets and banks cost less. Suits wide empires and rulers who solve problems with gold.`,fact:`At the great medieval trade fairs, special fair courts settled merchants’ disputes quickly, applying commercial custom alongside local law.`},"trait:expansionist":{desc:`Starts with a [[unit:scout|Scout]] and alone can build more, so you find neighbors and the best city sites first. You will see the whole map early, and notice how little of it is yours yet.`,fact:`New Zealand was among the last large lands on Earth to be settled by people, only around the thirteenth century.`},"trait:industrious":{desc:`For builders: workers finish their jobs much faster and large cities produce extra shields. Strong in wonder races and when developing new land quickly.`,fact:`The word “industry” first meant diligence, from the Latin industria. Only in the eighteenth century did it come to mean factories.`},"trait:militaristic":{desc:`For conquerors: barracks, walls and other military buildings cost less, and units earn promotions and produce great leaders far more often.`,fact:`The Latin militia first meant military service itself; only later did the word come to mean the citizens who performed it.`},"trait:religious":{desc:`For a stable realm: changing government costs barely any anarchy, and temples and cathedrals are cheaper. Lets you switch governments to fit the moment and keep cities calm.`,fact:`Many of the world’s oldest manuscripts survived in religious libraries, from Saint Catherine’s Monastery in Sinai, in use since the sixth century, to the Buddhist cave library at Dunhuang.`},"trait:scientific":{desc:`For the progress-minded: a free tech at the start of each era, and cheaper libraries and universities. Built for the race to key discoveries.`,fact:`The word “scientist” was coined only in 1833, by William Whewell. Before that, Newton and his successors were “natural philosophers.”`},"trait:seafaring":{desc:`For a maritime power: coastal cities earn more, ships travel farther and harbors cost less. Shines on island maps and in the race to find new lands.`,fact:`The international nautical mile is exactly 1,852 meters, chosen because it is close to one minute of latitude, so a navigator can estimate distances straight off the side of a chart.`},"tech:alphabet":{quote:`Writing is the painting of the voice.`,quoteBy:`Voltaire`,desc:`The doorway to Writing and Mathematics, plus the [[unit:curragh|curragh]], a small boat for scouting nearby coasts. Learn it early: the whole branch of knowledge waits on it.`,history:`Around 1800 BC, Semitic laborers in Egypt's Sinai mines began using simplified hieroglyphs to stand for sounds. That idea grew into the Phoenician script, a couple dozen signs instead of hundreds. In the 8th century BC the Greeks added vowels, and from them the alphabet passed to the Etruscans, the Romans and, later, the Slavs.`,fact:`The letter A began as a picture of an ox’s head: the Phoenician aleph meant “ox.” Turn a capital A upside down and you can still see the horns.`},"tech:bronze_working":{quote:`Their armour was of bronze, and their houses of bronze, and of bronze were their implements: there was no black iron.`,quoteBy:`Hesiod, Works and Days`,desc:`Cheap insurance for an early capital: it brings the [[unit:spearman|spearman]] and several nations' unique troops, and gives seaside cities the [[wonder:the_colossus|Colossus]]. The road to iron runs through it.`,history:`Bronze, an alloy of copper and tin, appeared in the Near East around 3300 BC. Harder than pure copper and easy to cast, it went into spearheads, sickles and statues alike. Tin was scarce, and merchants traveled from Britain to Afghanistan to find it, so the metal stitched the ancient world together with trade routes.`,fact:`Bronze rings when struck, which is why bells have been cast from it for thousands of years. Bell founders still use a tin-rich alloy known simply as bell metal.`},"tech:ceremonial_burial":{quote:`Man is a noble animal, splendid in ashes, and pompous in the grave.`,quoteBy:`Thomas Browne, Urn Burial`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:temple|temple]], the first tool for keeping growing cities content. A cheap, necessary step on the way to Mysticism and the wonders of faith.`,history:`The oldest known deliberate burials, in the Skhul and Qafzeh caves of Israel, date to roughly 100,000 years ago. Later graves held ochre, ornaments and tools, as if the dead might still need them. Caring for the dead is one of the earliest signs that people had begun to think about time, memory and something larger than themselves.`,fact:`Ancient Egyptians mummified animals by the million — cats, ibises, falcons, crocodiles — often as offerings. In the late nineteenth century a whole cemetery of cat mummies was dug up near Beni Hasan, and a shipload went to England to be ground into fertilizer.`},"tech:code_of_laws":{quote:`Where there is no law, there is no freedom.`,quoteBy:`John Locke`,desc:`Brings the [[building:courthouse|courthouse]], which reins in corruption in far-off cities, and leads to the Republic. Research it once your empire starts to sprawl.`,history:`The oldest surviving law code was drawn up under the Sumerian king Ur-Nammu around 2100 BC. The most famous, Hammurabi's, was carved on a basalt stele around 1754 BC and now stands in the Louvre. The core idea was that the rules were written down in advance and applied to everyone living under them, even if the penalties still depended on rank.`,fact:`Draco’s laws in Athens punished even petty theft with death, so harshly that, as Plutarch reports, they were said to be written in blood rather than ink. That is why “draconian” still means excessively severe.`},"tech:construction":{quote:`I found Rome a city of bricks and left it a city of marble.`,quoteBy:`Augustus, as told by Suetonius`,desc:`The technology of big cities: the [[building:aqueduct|aqueduct]] lifts the growth cap, the [[building:colosseum|colosseum]] cools unrest, and workers learn to build fortresses. The Great Wall lives here too.`,history:`The Romans took building to a new level with concrete, a mix of lime, volcanic ash and rubble that set even underwater. From the 3rd century BC they used it for arches, vaults, bridges and domes. The Pantheon, finished around AD 125, still carries the world's largest unreinforced concrete dome.`,fact:`Roman builders called their concrete opus caementicium, and our word “cement” comes from caementa — not the binder, but the rough chips of stone that went into the mix.`},"tech:trial_and_error":{quote:`Experience is the name everyone gives to their mistakes.`,quoteBy:`Oscar Wilde, Lady Windermere’s Fan`,desc:`What a people without a name studies: everything at once, nothing in particular. It gives nothing and leads nowhere; it ends the day the tribe takes a name, and a little of it goes into the first real discovery.`,history:`In 1898 Edward Thorndike shut hungry cats in puzzle boxes with a latch inside. At first a cat scratched and pushed at random; each time it got out a little faster, until it went straight for the latch. Thorndike called it learning by trial and error. Thomas Edison worked the same way on the light bulb, trying thousands of filaments before carbonized bamboo held.`,fact:`The phrase was made popular by the psychologist Conwy Lloyd Morgan, who watched his fox terrier Tony learn to open the garden gate. Visitors thought the dog clever; Morgan had watched it fail for weeks before it found the latch by poking its head under it.`},"tech:currency":{quote:`For the love of money is the root of all evil.`,quoteBy:`First Epistle to Timothy`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:marketplace|marketplace]], which multiplies a big city's gold and luxuries. Wanted by trading nations and by anyone who hopes to tax without riots.`,history:`Long before coins, Mesopotamians paid in silver by weight and in barley. The first struck coins appeared in Lydia in the 7th century BC, made of electrum, a natural gold-silver alloy, and stamped with the king's guarantee. Coins ended the need to weigh metal at every sale, and soon almost every Greek city minted its own.`,fact:`The word “money” comes from the temple of Juno Moneta in Rome, beside which the city’s coins were struck. The popular claim that “salary” comes from Roman soldiers being paid in salt, by contrast, has no solid evidence behind it.`},"tech:horseback_riding":{quote:`Hast thou given the horse strength? hast thou clothed his neck with thunder?`,quoteBy:`Book of Job`,desc:`Brings the [[unit:horseman|horseman]], a fast scout and raider that finds huts and foreign borders first. Valuable on open plains and for early raids.`,history:`Horses were tamed on the Eurasian steppe; genetics traces all modern domestic horses to the lower Volga and Don around 2200 BC. At first they pulled chariots, and true cavalry appeared only in the 1st millennium BC, among the Assyrians and the Scythians, who learned to shoot a bow at full gallop.`,fact:`Horses are a homecoming story: they evolved in North America, crossed into Asia over the Bering land bridge and died out in the Americas. Columbus brought them back to the Caribbean in 1493, and Spanish expeditions carried them onto the mainland.`},"tech:iron_working":{quote:`Gold is for the mistress, silver for the maid, but Iron, cold Iron, is master of them all.`,quoteBy:`Rudyard Kipling, Cold Iron`,desc:`Reveals [[resource:iron|iron]] on the map and brings the [[unit:swordsman|swordsman]], the first serious assault infantry. Without a deposit inside your borders it does little, so scout for one early.`,history:`Iron smelting was mastered in Anatolia and the Near East during the 2nd millennium BC and spread quickly after the Bronze Age kingdoms collapsed around 1200 BC. Unlike tin, iron ore can be found almost anywhere, and for the first time metal reached ordinary soldiers and farmers. So began the Iron Age.`,fact:`Some of the earliest iron objects were made from meteorites: a dagger buried with Tutankhamun has been shown, by its nickel content, to be forged from meteoric iron. The Egyptians spoke of iron as “metal of the sky.”`},"tech:literature":{quote:`A good book is the precious life-blood of a master spirit.`,quoteBy:`John Milton, Areopagitica`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:library|library]] and the Great Library, the foundation of a scientific nation. Not needed to reach the next era, but scholars tend to miss it.`,history:`Literature began when stories were written down: the Epic of Gilgamesh survives on clay tablets from ancient Mesopotamia. In the 8th century BC the Greeks recorded Homer's poems, and China compiled the Classic of Poetry. Text outlived its tellers, and for the first time one person's thoughts could speak to descendants centuries later.`,fact:`The Tale of Genji, written by a lady of the Japanese court, Murasaki Shikibu, about a thousand years ago, is often called one of the world’s first novels.`},"tech:map_making":{quote:`Geography is an imitation through drawing of the entire known part of the world.`,quoteBy:`Ptolemy, Geography`,desc:`Takes your nation to sea: the [[unit:galley|galley]] ferries troops along the coast and the [[building:harbor|harbor]] feeds seaside cities. Also brings the Great Lighthouse and right-of-passage treaties.`,history:`The oldest known map of the world is a Babylonian clay tablet from the 6th century BC, with Babylon at the center and a 'bitter river' encircling the land. Greek sailors recorded coasts in periploi, lists of harbors and days of sailing. In the 2nd century Ptolemy's Geography gave coordinates for thousands of places and guided mapmakers for another fifteen hundred years.`,fact:`The famous warning “Here be dragons” appears on almost no real old map: the Latin HIC SVNT DRACONES is known only from a couple of early sixteenth-century globes.`},"tech:masonry":{quote:`Not marble, nor the gilded monuments of princes, shall outlive this powerful rhyme.`,quoteBy:`William Shakespeare, Sonnet LV`,desc:`Brings [[building:walls|city walls]] and the [[wonder:the_pyramids|Pyramids]], and lets workers raise outposts to watch the border. An early, cheap step toward Mathematics.`,history:`The stone walls and tower of Jericho, built around 8000 BC, are among the oldest fortifications known. Earlier still, around 9500 BC, rings of carved pillars rose at Gobekli Tepe. The Egyptians refined the craft to precision: blocks of the Great Pyramid fit so closely that a knife blade barely slips between them.`,fact:`Inca masons fitted their stones without mortar so precisely that walls in Cusco have outlasted earthquakes which brought down the Spanish colonial buildings set on top of them.`},"tech:mathematics":{quote:`Let no one ignorant of geometry enter here.`,quoteBy:`said to be carved over Plato’s Academy`,desc:`Brings the [[unit:catapult|catapult]], the first answer to fortified cities, and the Statue of Zeus. A hub technology: no Currency and no Construction without it.`,history:`Sumerians and Babylonians counted in base sixty, which is why we still have 60 minutes and 360 degrees. Egypt's Rhind Papyrus, around 1550 BC, teaches how to divide bread and measure fields. Around 300 BC Euclid's Elements built geometry from a handful of axioms, and it stayed a textbook for more than two thousand years.`,fact:`The word “algorithm” is a Latinized form of al-Khwarizmi, a ninth-century scholar in Baghdad, and “algebra” comes from al-jabr in the title of his book. Every programmer, in other words, quotes him daily.`},"tech:monarchy":{quote:`Uneasy lies the head that wears a crown.`,quoteBy:`William Shakespeare, Henry IV`,desc:`Unlocks [[gov:monarchy|Monarchy]], a solid step up from despotism for a nation that fights often, along with the Hanging Gardens. Not required for the next era.`,history:`Kingship is one of the oldest forms of rule: the Sumerian King List begins its count from the moment 'kingship descended from heaven.' Monarchs relied on dynasty, army and the blessing of the gods. Over time many crowns became constitutional, and the institution survives today in more than forty countries.`,fact:`The British monarch has an “official birthday,” celebrated in summer whatever the real date — a custom from the eighteenth century, when a king born in November preferred his birthday parade in better weather.`},"tech:mysticism":{quote:`The eternal silence of these infinite spaces frightens me.`,quoteBy:`Blaise Pascal, Pensées`,desc:`Unlocks the [[wonder:the_oracle|Oracle]], the wonder for rulers who want calm cities without a temple on every corner. Also leads to Polytheism and on to Monarchy.`,history:`Mysticism is the pursuit of direct, personal contact with the divine, beyond ordinary ritual. Initiates of the Eleusinian Mysteries in ancient Greece swore to keep silent about what they saw, and the secret was kept for nearly two thousand years. Seers, priestesses and dream interpreters served as go-betweens for people and gods in every ancient culture.`,fact:`Tarot cards were invented in fifteenth-century Italy for an ordinary card game, tarocchi. They were tied to fortune-telling only in eighteenth-century France, and the game itself is still played in parts of Europe.`},"tech:philosophy":{quote:`The unexamined life is not worth living.`,quoteBy:`Socrates`,desc:`The first civilization to ponder the meaning of existence gets a free technology. Everyone else gets the Mausoleum and a road to the Republic. The meaning itself is not included.`,history:`Between the 6th and 4th centuries BC, people in distant corners of the world began asking what the world is made of and how one ought to live: Thales and Socrates in Greece, Confucius and Laozi in China, the Buddha in India. Philosophers sought answers in reason and argument rather than legend. Plato founded the Academy, Aristotle the Lyceum, and their students posed questions still debated today.`,fact:`Every academy in the world is named after a grove outside Athens where Plato taught. The grove itself took its name from a local hero, Akademos, of whom almost nothing else is remembered.`},"tech:polytheism":{quote:`These things lie on the knees of the gods.`,quoteBy:`Homer`,desc:`Unlocks the [[wonder:the_temple_of_artemis|Temple of Artemis]], which works as a temple in every city you own, and leads to Monarchy. Good for religious nations that build wonders.`,history:`In the ancient religions of Egypt, Mesopotamia, Greece and India, every force of nature and every craft had its own god: sun, sea, war, wisdom, harvest. The gods quarreled, loved and feuded much like people, and their temples were the centers of city life. Their myths fed art, drama and poetry for thousands of years.`,fact:`Before a siege the Romans sometimes performed an evocatio, a rite inviting the enemy city’s gods to change sides, with the promise of a finer temple in Rome. Juno is said to have accepted the offer from Veii.`},"tech:pottery":{quote:`Shall the clay say to him that fashioneth it, What makest thou?`,quoteBy:`Book of Isaiah`,desc:`Brings the [[building:granary|granary]] for fast growth and settler production, and leads to Map Making. Not a heroic discovery, but empires are, in the end, held together by jars.`,history:`The oldest known pottery comes from Xianrendong Cave in China and is about 20,000 years old, older than farming. Pots stored grain, cooked stew and carried water. The potter's wheel appeared in Mesopotamia in the 4th millennium BC, and shards last so well that archaeologists date entire cultures by them.`,fact:`Monte Testaccio in Rome is an artificial hill made almost entirely of broken olive-oil jars, the remains of tens of millions of amphorae discarded over centuries. Archaeologists consider it a treasure.`},"tech:the_republic":{quote:`Let the good of the people be the supreme law.`,quoteBy:`Cicero`,desc:`Unlocks the [[gov:republic|Republic]], the choice of a peaceful scientific or trading nation: more gold and research, but long wars grow hard to sustain. Not required for the next era.`,history:`In 509 BC the Romans expelled their last king, Tarquin the Proud, and proclaimed the res publica, the 'public thing.' Power was shared by two consuls elected each year and the Senate, while citizens voted in assemblies. The Roman Republic lasted nearly five centuries, and its design inspired Venice, the Dutch Republic and the founders of the United States.`,fact:`Both Roman consuls held full power and could block each other, so for much of the Republic they simply took turns, holding the symbols of command in alternate months.`},"tech:the_wheel":{quote:`Thirty spokes share one hub; it is the empty centre that makes the wheel useful.`,quoteBy:`Lao Tzu, Tao Te Ching`,desc:`Reveals [[resource:horses|horses]] on the map and brings the [[unit:chariot|chariot]], the first strike force on wheels. Reinventing it later is not recommended.`,history:`The wheel appeared around 3500 BC, almost at once in Mesopotamia, the northern Black Sea steppe and Central Europe. The oldest surviving wooden wheel with its axle was found in the Ljubljana Marshes and is about 5,150 years old. Early wheels were solid and heavy; light spoked wheels arrived around 2000 BC and made the war chariot possible.`,fact:`The potter’s wheel probably came before the cart wheel: people were spinning clay before they thought of rolling loads on it.`},"tech:warrior_code":{quote:`All warfare is based on deception.`,quoteBy:`Sun Tzu`,desc:`Brings the [[unit:archer|archer]], the first soldier able to strike from a distance, and several nations' unique missile troops. Leads to Horseback Riding and Monarchy; good for early aggression.`,history:`The bow is among the oldest complex tools: stone arrowheads from Sibudu Cave in South Africa are more than 60,000 years old. As the first armies formed, warriors also developed unwritten codes of honor: how to meet an enemy, whether to spare captives, when retreat was unthinkable. These later grew into Spartan discipline, bushido and the code of chivalry.`,fact:`Spartans prized brevity so much that “laconic” comes from Laconia, their homeland. When Philip of Macedon threatened, “If I enter Laconia, I will raze Sparta,” the Spartans, as Plutarch tells it, replied with one word: “If.”`},"tech:writing":{quote:`The pen is mightier than the sword.`,quoteBy:`Edward Bulwer-Lytton, Richelieu`,desc:`The hub of the ancient tree, leading to Philosophy, Code of Laws, Literature and Map Making. It allows embassies and military alliances: diplomacy starts here.`,history:`Around 3200 BC, scribes in the Sumerian city of Uruk began pressing signs into clay tablets. Humanity's first written texts were not poems or laws but receipts for barley, sheep and beer. Writing arose independently in Egypt, China and Mesoamerica, and soon it was recording treaties, prayers and the deeds of kings.`,fact:`Early Greek inscriptions sometimes run boustrophedon, “as the ox turns”: one line left to right, the next right to left, like a plough going back and forth across a field.`},"tech:astronomy":{quote:`We are all in the gutter, but some of us are looking at the stars.`,quoteBy:`Oscar Wilde`,desc:`Lets trade cross the seas and brings the [[unit:caravel|caravel]] for long voyages, along with the explorer and Copernicus' Observatory. Needed by those hunting for new lands.`,history:`People have watched the sky since prehistory, and in the 1420s Ulugh Beg built an observatory in Samarkand with a giant sextant. In 1543 Nicolaus Copernicus published On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres, which put the Sun at the center of the world. The idea overturned not only astronomy but humanity's sense of its own place in the universe.`,fact:`Many star names we still use — Aldebaran, Betelgeuse, Algol, Rigel — are Arabic, inherited from medieval astronomers who preserved and extended the Greek star catalogues.`},"tech:banking":{quote:`Neither a borrower nor a lender be.`,quoteBy:`William Shakespeare, Hamlet`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:bank|bank]], which raises the tax take of wealthy cities further. Leads to Democracy and Economics, a natural step for a trading nation.`,history:`The word 'bank' comes from the Italian banco, the bench where money changers did business. In the 14th and 15th centuries Florentine houses, above all the Medici, ran branches across Europe and moved money by bills of exchange instead of carting coin. Bankers lent to kings and popes, and their capital paid for much of the Renaissance.`,fact:`Double-entry bookkeeping was first set out in print in 1494 by Luca Pacioli, a friar, mathematician and friend of Leonardo da Vinci. Accountants still call him the father of their profession.`},"tech:chemistry":{quote:`Nothing is lost, nothing is created, everything is transformed.`,quoteBy:`after Antoine Lavoisier`,desc:`Builds nothing on its own, but without it there is no Physics and no Metallurgy, and so no cannons. A pass-through step, yet a mandatory one for both the military and the science branches.`,history:`Chemistry grew out of alchemy, which sought the philosopher's stone but left behind furnaces, stills and the habit of experiment. In 1661 Robert Boyle's The Sceptical Chymist called for testing every claim by experiment. In the late 18th century Lavoisier proved the conservation of mass and drew up the first modern list of chemical elements.`,fact:`Phosphorus was discovered in 1669 by a Hamburg alchemist, Hennig Brand, who boiled down a great deal of urine in search of the philosopher’s stone. He found instead a substance that glowed in the dark.`},"tech:chivalry":{quote:`He was a verray, parfit gentil knyght.`,quoteBy:`Geoffrey Chaucer, The Canterbury Tales`,desc:`Brings the [[unit:knight|knight]], heavy cavalry for taking cities, and a whole stable of national riders from samurai to keshiks. The Knights Templar is here too. Not required for the next era.`,history:`In the 11th and 12th centuries the heavily armored horseman became the core of European armies, and a code of conduct grew up around him: loyalty to one's lord, protection of the weak, generosity and courage. Romances and tournaments turned the ideal into a culture. Knights did not always live up to it, but the word 'chivalry' long outlived the mounted armies.`,fact:`The word “freelance” sounds modern, but Walter Scott coined it in the nineteenth century for medieval mercenaries — knights whose lances served whoever paid for them.`},"tech:democracy":{quote:`Government of the people, by the people, for the people, shall not perish from the earth.`,quoteBy:`Abraham Lincoln`,desc:`Unlocks [[gov:democracy|Democracy]]: minimal corruption and maximum trade for a peaceful nation, though it tolerates long wars worst of all. Not required for the next era.`,history:`In 508 BC the reforms of Cleisthenes in Athens handed decisions to an assembly of citizens, with many offices filled by lot. Ancient democracy was direct and included only free men. Modern representative democracy grew from the English Parliament and the American and French revolutions of the 18th century.`,fact:`In 1893 New Zealand became the first self-governing country where all adult women could vote in national elections — though they could not stand for Parliament there until 1919.`},"tech:economics":{quote:`In the long run we are all dead.`,quoteBy:`John Maynard Keynes`,desc:`Makes turning production into wealth far more lucrative and unlocks [[wonder:smiths_trading_company|Smith's Trading Company]]. The treasury's best friend when nothing is left to build. Optional for the next era.`,history:`Economics as a science began when thinkers asked where the wealth of nations comes from. Mercantilists measured it in gold, the physiocrats in land. In 1776 Adam Smith's The Wealth of Nations described the division of labor and the 'invisible hand' of the market, through which private interest serves the common good.`,fact:`Thomas Carlyle called economics “the dismal science” in 1849 — not because of gloomy forecasts, but in an essay defending slavery, attacking economists who argued for free labor.`},"tech:education":{quote:`The mind is not a vessel to be filled, but a fire to be kindled.`,quoteBy:`Plutarch`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:university|university]], the main source of medieval science. But it also makes the Great Library and the Temple of Artemis obsolete, so decide which matters more.`,history:`Universities as self-governing communities of teachers and students emerged in Europe around 1100: Bologna, Paris, Oxford. Earlier still, in 859, the mosque school of al-Qarawiyyin was founded in Fez. Teaching was in Latin from shared textbooks, so a student could move from Bologna to Paris and pick up where he had left off.`,fact:`Medieval Oxford students fought pitched “town and gown” battles with locals. The St Scholastica Day riot of 1355 began as an argument over wine in a tavern, and for centuries afterward the town paid the university a yearly penance.`},"tech:engineering":{quote:`Compare, if you will, the idle Pyramids or the useless, though famous, works of the Greeks with these aqueducts.`,quoteBy:`after Frontinus, The Aqueducts of Rome`,desc:`Brings the [[unit:trebuchet|trebuchet]] for breaking walls, lets roads cross rivers, and teaches workers to plant forests. Leads to Invention.`,history:`The word 'engineer' goes back to the Latin ingenium, a clever device, usually a siege engine. In the 12th century the counterweight trebuchet appeared in Europe and the Middle East, hurling stones hundreds of yards. The same masters built water mills, stone bridges and Gothic cathedrals, judging their vaults more by experience than by formula.`,fact:`Frontinus, who ran the aqueducts of Rome around the end of the first century, wrote a manual on them and complained of landowners secretly tapping the pipes. Water theft is as old as plumbing.`},"tech:feudalism":{quote:`No free man shall be seized or imprisoned, except by the lawful judgement of his equals or by the law of the land.`,quoteBy:`Magna Carta`,desc:`Brings the [[unit:pikeman|pikeman]], a dependable guard against cavalry, plus the Feudalism government and the Art of War. Leads to Chivalry and Invention.`,history:`The feudal order took shape in Western Europe in the 9th and 10th centuries as central power weakened. A lord granted a vassal land, a fief, in exchange for loyalty and military service, while peasants worked the land under his protection. The word 'feudalism' itself was coined by 17th- and 18th-century scholars; medieval Japan and other lands developed similar systems.`,fact:`Under Japan’s system of alternate attendance, most daimyo spent every other year in the shogun’s capital, Edo, and left their families there; schedules varied by domain. The costly processions kept lords loyal, strained their budgets and built a nationwide road network.`},"tech:free_artistry":{quote:`A thing of beauty is a joy for ever.`,quoteBy:`John Keats, Endymion`,desc:`Unlocks [[wonder:shakespeares_theater|Shakespeare's Theater]], which all but ends unrest in one city. Not required for the next era: a luxury for peaceful democracies.`,history:`During the Renaissance the artist rose from guild craftsman to celebrated creator, the pride of cities and princes. Michelangelo and Leonardo bargained with patrons as equals, and in 1563 Florence opened its Academy of Design. By the end of the 16th century public theaters such as London's Globe sold art for a penny to anyone who came.`,fact:`The Mona Lisa became world-famous partly because it was stolen: in 1911 a museum worker walked out of the Louvre with it under his smock, and it was missing for more than two years. Crowds came to stare at the empty wall.`},"tech:gunpowder":{quote:`Blessed were the times which lacked the dread fury of those devilish engines of artillery.`,quoteBy:`after Cervantes, Don Quixote`,desc:`Reveals [[resource:saltpeter|saltpeter]] on the map and brings the [[unit:musketman|musketman]], a defender that locks cities against spears and swords for a long time. One of the era's key technologies.`,history:`Gunpowder was discovered by Chinese alchemists around the 9th century while searching for an elixir of immortality; a 1044 military manual already gives recipes for incendiary mixtures. Along the Silk Road the secret reached the Islamic world and Europe by the 13th century. Cannons and arquebuses changed war: castle walls and knightly armor no longer kept anyone safe.`,fact:`Gunpowder needs saltpeter, and states hunted it in odd places: English “saltpetermen” once had the right to dig up the floors of barns, dovecotes and even homes for nitrate-rich earth.`},"tech:invention":{quote:`The true creator is necessity, who is the mother of our invention.`,quoteBy:`Plato, The Republic`,desc:`Unlocks [[wonder:leonardos_workshop|Leonardo's Workshop]], which makes upgrading troops cheaper, and the [[unit:longbowman|longbowman]]. Leads to Gunpowder.`,history:`The Renaissance was an age of inventors: Leonardo da Vinci filled his notebooks with designs for flying machines, pumps and mechanisms. In 1474 Venice passed the first patent law, protecting an inventor's rights for ten years. The notion that a new machine could be invented, not just inherited, changed how people thought about technology.`,fact:`Plenty of inventions were found by accident: the microwave oven began when a radar engineer noticed that a candy bar in his pocket had melted.`},"tech:magnetism":{quote:`Sailors touch a needle to the magnet, and it turns until its point looks to the north.`,quoteBy:`after Alexander Neckam`,desc:`Lets trade cross the ocean and brings the [[unit:frigate|frigate]] and the [[unit:galleon|galleon]], a fleet that can fight and ferry troops far from shore. The Great Lighthouse goes obsolete.`,history:`The Chinese knew about lodestone in antiquity, and in 1088 the scholar Shen Kuo described a magnetic needle for navigation. In Europe the compass is first mentioned around 1190. In 1600 William Gilbert's On the Magnet showed that the Earth itself is a giant magnet, explaining why the needle points north.`,fact:`The word “magnet” comes from Magnesia, a region of Greece rich in lodestone. Pliny the Elder tells of a shepherd whose iron-nailed sandals stuck to a magnetic rock.`},"tech:metallurgy":{quote:`They shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their spears into pruninghooks.`,quoteBy:`Book of Micah`,desc:`Brings the [[unit:cannon|cannon]] for breaching cities and the [[building:coastal_fortress|coastal fortress]]. It also makes the Great Wall and the Statue of Zeus obsolete. Leads to Military Tradition.`,history:`In the late Middle Ages blast furnaces made cast iron available in quantity, and founders learned to cast enormous cannon. In 1540 the Italian Biringuccio described casting and smelting in his Pirotechnia, and in 1556 Agricola's De Re Metallica appeared, the metalworker's standard textbook for the next two centuries.`,fact:`Aluminum was once more precious than gold. Napoleon III is said to have served his most honored guests on aluminum plates, and the capstone set on the Washington Monument in 1884 is a small aluminum pyramid.`},"tech:military_tradition":{quote:`No plan survives first contact with the enemy.`,quoteBy:`after Moltke the Elder`,desc:`Brings [[unit:cavalry|cavalry]], the fastest strike before the age of engines, and the Military Academy. Not required for the next era, but close to essential for a conqueror.`,history:`Around 1600 Maurice of Nassau introduced constant drill and training into the Dutch army, and all of Europe soon followed. Armies became permanent, and officers began to be trained in schools: both Saint-Cyr and West Point were founded in 1802. Regimental colors, uniforms and regulations passed the experience of war from one generation to the next.`,fact:`Many military ranks hide humbler words: “sergeant” comes from the Latin for “serving,” and “corporal” from the Italian caporale, from capo, “head.”`},"tech:monotheism":{quote:`Hear, O Israel: The Lord our God is one Lord.`,quoteBy:`Book of Deuteronomy`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:cathedral|cathedral]], a powerful remedy for unrest in big cities. Leads to Theology and Chivalry; the medieval branch stalls without it.`,history:`Belief in a single God is one of the most influential ideas in history. Its tradition goes back to ancient Israel and continued in Christianity and Islam. For centuries temples, monasteries and cathedrals were centers of learning, art and community life, and religious ideas deeply shaped law, philosophy and culture.`,fact:`In medieval Toledo, Christian, Jewish and Muslim scholars worked side by side translating Arabic and Greek learning into Latin, and much of ancient science reached Europe through their hands.`},"tech:music_theory":{quote:`Without music, life would be a mistake.`,quoteBy:`Friedrich Nietzsche`,desc:`Unlocks [[wonder:js_bachs_cathedral|J.S. Bach's Cathedral]], which calms your whole empire. Not required for the next era. It wins no wars, yet outlasts nearly all of them.`,history:`Pythagoras noticed that pleasing harmonies arise from simple ratios of string lengths. In the 11th century Guido of Arezzo devised the staff and syllable names for notes, so music could be learned from the page and not only by ear. In 1722 came both Rameau's Treatise on Harmony and the first book of Bach's Well-Tempered Clavier.`,fact:`The tritone was nicknamed diabolus in musica, “the devil in music,” but the story that the medieval Church banned it is a later myth: theorists merely advised avoiding the unsettling interval.`},"tech:navigation":{quote:`Water, water, every where, nor any drop to drink.`,quoteBy:`Samuel Taylor Coleridge`,desc:`Lets trade cross the ocean, allows trading maps and unlocks [[wonder:magellans_voyage|Magellan's Voyage]]. Not required for the next era, but maritime powers need it.`,history:`In the 15th century Portuguese sailors backed by Prince Henry learned to find latitude from the Sun and stars with the quadrant and astrolabe. That opened the way around Africa and across the Atlantic. Longitude at sea was measured reliably only in the 18th century, when John Harrison built an accurate marine chronometer.`,fact:`Marshall Islanders mapped not coasts but ocean swells: their stick charts, made of palm ribs tied together, with shells for islands, showed how waves bend around land.`},"tech:physics":{quote:`If I have seen further, it is by standing on the shoulders of giants.`,quoteBy:`Isaac Newton`,desc:`Builds nothing itself, but opens the way to Magnetism and its ocean fleets, and to the Theory of Gravity. A bridge from the alchemy of the past to the science of the future.`,history:`Aristotle taught that heavy bodies fall faster than light ones, and almost no one challenged him for two thousand years. Galileo Galilei tested the claim with inclined planes and in 1638 set out the laws of motion in his Two New Sciences. Physics became a science in which measurement, not authority, settles the argument.`,fact:`James Joule, after whom the unit of energy is named, ran the family brewery and did physics on the side. The story goes that on his honeymoon he set out to measure the water temperature at the top and bottom of a waterfall.`},"tech:printing_press":{quote:`Printing, gunpowder and the magnet have changed the whole face and state of things throughout the world.`,quoteBy:`Francis Bacon, Novum Organum`,desc:`Allows trading contacts with other nations and leads to Democracy. It builds nothing of its own and is not required for the next era.`,history:`Movable type of baked clay was invented in China by Bi Sheng in the 1040s, and in 1377 Korean printers produced the Jikji with metal type. Around 1440 Johannes Gutenberg in Mainz combined metal type with a screw press, and his Bible appeared around 1455. Within fifty years Europe had printed millions of books.`,fact:`Gutenberg never grew rich from his invention: his financier, Johann Fust, sued him, took over the workshop and went on printing with Gutenberg’s equipment.`},"tech:theology":{quote:`Faith seeking understanding.`,quoteBy:`Anselm of Canterbury`,desc:`Unlocks the [[wonder:sistine_chapel|Sistine Chapel]], which strengthens cathedrals, and leads to Printing Press and Education. The Oracle goes obsolete.`,history:`Theology is the systematic study of faith through reason; medieval universities called it the queen of the sciences. In the 13th century Thomas Aquinas, in his Summa Theologiae, joined Christian thought with the philosophy of Aristotle, while Islamic and Jewish scholars did similar work with the Greek legacy.`,fact:`The question of how many angels can dance on the head of a pin is often used to mock medieval theologians, but no medieval scholar is known to have asked it; it seems to be a later jibe.`},"tech:theory_of_gravity":{quote:`Nature and Nature’s laws lay hid in night: God said, Let Newton be! and all was light.`,quoteBy:`Alexander Pope`,desc:`Unlocks [[wonder:newtons_university|Newton's University]], a wonder that turns a strong science city into the empire's research engine. Worth it when your plan is to win through knowledge.`,history:`In 1687 Isaac Newton published the Principia, showing that a falling apple and the orbiting Moon obey the same law of universal gravitation. Celestial mechanics became an exact science and shaped physics for two centuries. The apple was never consulted.`,fact:`Newton spent more time on alchemy and biblical chronology than on physics. When his papers were auctioned in 1936, the economist John Maynard Keynes bought many and called him “not the first of the age of reason, but the last of the magicians.”`},"tech:advanced_flight":{quote:`Oh! I have slipped the surly bonds of Earth and danced the skies on laughter-silvered wings.`,quoteBy:`John Gillespie Magee, High Flight`,desc:`Brings [[unit:paratrooper|paratroopers]], [[unit:helicopter|helicopters]], and radar towers. Optional for the next era, but it gives your army vertical reach: strikes where no road leads.`,history:`Through the 1930s and 1940s aviation moved from wood-and-fabric biplanes to all-metal monoplanes, and in 1939 Igor Sikorsky flew the VS-300, the template for practical helicopters. World War II made airborne assault an operation of its own, as paratroopers seized bridges and airfields behind the lines from Crete to Normandy.`,fact:`The jet engine was invented twice, independently and at almost the same time, by Frank Whittle in Britain and Hans von Ohain in Germany — and neither knew of the other’s work.`},"tech:amphibious_war":{quote:`Once more unto the breach, dear friends, once more.`,quoteBy:`William Shakespeare, Henry V`,desc:`Brings [[unit:marine|Marines]], who can attack straight from their ships. Essential for island empires and for cracking coastal fortresses; landlocked rulers can safely skip it.`,history:`Landing under fire on a defended beach long looked close to hopeless, and Gallipoli in 1915 seemed to prove it. Between the wars the US Marine Corps worked out a full doctrine of amphibious assault, with purpose-built landing craft and supply from the sea. World War II tested those ideas on Pacific atolls and the beaches of Normandy.`,fact:`The Allies built two entire artificial harbors, codenamed Mulberry, in Britain and towed them across the Channel after D-Day, so they could land supplies without first capturing a port.`},"tech:atomic_theory":{quote:`It was almost as incredible as if you fired a fifteen-inch shell at a piece of tissue paper and it came back and hit you.`,quoteBy:`Ernest Rutherford`,desc:`Builds nothing on its own, but it is the gate to [[tech:electronics|Electronics]] and the power plants beyond. Pure science that pays off one step later.`,history:`Democritus imagined indivisible particles, but John Dalton made the idea scientific around 1808 by explaining the fixed proportions of chemical reactions. J. J. Thomson found the electron in 1897, and Ernest Rutherford uncovered the nucleus in 1911. The atom turned out to be not indivisible but built, and that opened the way to all of 20th-century physics.`,fact:`The word “atom” means “uncuttable” in Greek, so splitting one is, strictly speaking, a contradiction in terms. Physicists kept the name anyway.`},"tech:combustion":{quote:`The automobile engine will come, and then I will consider my life’s work complete.`,quoteBy:`Rudolf Diesel`,desc:`Modernizes your navy with [[unit:destroyer|destroyers]], cruisers, and transports, and leads to Flight and Mass Production. Skip it and your industrial power stays ashore and a century behind.`,history:`The internal combustion engine grew out of 19th-century experiments: Nikolaus Otto built a reliable four-stroke engine in 1876, and Rudolf Diesel introduced compression ignition in the 1890s. Compact and powerful, it gave the world the automobile, the airplane, and the motor ship, and made oil the raw material of the age.`,fact:`The first long car journey was made in 1888 by Bertha Benz, who took her husband’s motor car without telling him and drove her sons about sixty miles to visit her mother, buying fuel at a pharmacy on the way.`},"tech:communism":{quote:`Workers of the world, unite!`,quoteBy:`Marx and Engels`,desc:`Unlocks [[gov:communism|Communism]] as a government and the [[building:police_station|Police Station]]. Appeals to sprawling empires that need a firm grip on distant provinces; not required to advance.`,history:`In 1848 Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels published The Communist Manifesto, predicting that capitalism would give way to a classless society with common ownership. In the 20th century, one-party states with planned economies arose under that banner, from the Soviet Union to China. They industrialized quickly, at the cost of harsh repression and enormous loss of life.`,fact:`Karl Marx spent years as the European correspondent of an American newspaper, the New-York Daily Tribune, filing hundreds of articles — some of them actually written by Engels.`},"tech:electricity":{quote:`He snatched the lightning from the sky and the sceptre from tyrants.`,quoteBy:`Turgot, of Benjamin Franklin`,desc:`Lets workers [[job:irrigate|irrigate]] land far from rivers and lakes, so dry plains can finally feed cities. Also leads to Scientific Method and Replaceable Parts, the backbone of the industrial tree.`,history:`Electricity had been a curiosity since antiquity, but it was tamed in the 19th century. Alessandro Volta built the first battery in 1800, Michael Faraday discovered electromagnetic induction in 1831, and by the 1880s Edison and Tesla were arguing over which current should light the cities. Within fifty years it went from a laboratory spark to a grid powering factories and homes.`,fact:`Benjamin Franklin invented the lightning rod and deliberately never patented it. He also gave electricity some of its everyday words: battery, positive, negative, charge.`},"tech:electronics":{quote:`Mr. Watson, come here, I want to see you.`,quoteBy:`Alexander Graham Bell`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:hydro_plant|Hydro Plant]], Civil Defense, and the [[wonder:hoover_dam|Hoover Dam]], a gift to every industrial city. Required on the road to Advanced Flight.`,history:`Electronics began with the ability to steer a stream of electrons. John Ambrose Fleming built the vacuum diode in 1904, and in 1906 Lee de Forest added a grid to make the triode, the first amplifier. Vacuum tubes made radio, sound recording, and long-distance telephony possible, and later radar and the first computers.`,fact:`The panic over the 1938 radio drama The War of the Worlds is famous, but historians now think it was greatly exaggerated by newspapers eager to show that radio was a dangerous rival.`},"tech:espionage":{quote:`What enables the wise sovereign to strike and conquer is foreknowledge.`,quoteBy:`Sun Tzu`,desc:`Unlocks the [[wonder:intelligence_agency|Intelligence Agency]] and the [[wonder:secret_police_hq|Secret Police HQ]]. For rulers who want to know more about their neighbors than they say, and to keep an empire in line.`,history:`Sun Tzu devoted a whole chapter of his treatise to spies, but permanent intelligence services are a modern invention. Britain's Secret Service Bureau appeared in 1909, followed by the CIA, the KGB, and many quieter colleagues. Officially, none of them contributed to this article.`,fact:`Polish mathematicians broke the German Enigma machine years before the Second World War and in 1939 handed their methods to the British and French, giving Bletchley Park its head start.`},"tech:fascism":{quote:`Those who cannot remember the past are condemned to repeat it.`,quoteBy:`George Santayana`,desc:`Unlocks [[gov:fascism|Fascism]], a government built for total mobilization and prolonged war. Optional, and of little use to a peaceful realm.`,history:`Fascism arose in Italy after World War I: Benito Mussolini founded the movement in 1919 and took power in 1922. The regime fused a cult of the leader, extreme nationalism, the crushing of opposition, and state control over the economy. Its German variant, Nazism, unleashed World War II and the Holocaust, and after 1945 fascism became a byword for political catastrophe.`,fact:`The Italian regime boasted that it made the trains run on time. Historians note that most railway improvements came before it took power, and the famous punctuality was largely propaganda.`},"tech:flight":{quote:`It is possible to fly without motors, but not without knowledge and skill.`,quoteBy:`Wilbur Wright`,desc:`Takes war into the sky with [[unit:fighter|fighters]], bombers, flak, airports, and airfields. It also retires [[wonder:the_colossus|the Colossus]], so harbor towns must find new income.`,history:`On December 17, 1903, Wilbur and Orville Wright made the first controlled powered flight, twelve seconds over the dunes at Kitty Hawk. Barely a decade later airplanes flew over the fronts of World War I, first as scouts and soon as fighters and bombers. By 1919 aviators had crossed the Atlantic nonstop.`,fact:`The first passengers of a Montgolfier balloon, in 1783, were a sheep, a duck and a rooster. All three landed safely, and only then did people go up themselves.`},"tech:industrialization":{quote:`And was Jerusalem builded here, among these dark Satanic Mills?`,quoteBy:`William Blake`,desc:`The heart of the industrial age: [[building:factory|factories]], coal plants, and [[wonder:universal_suffrage|Universal Suffrage]]. City production surges, and so does the smoke above it.`,history:`The Industrial Revolution began in Britain in the late 18th century, as spinning machines, Watt's steam engines, and the factory system moved production from workshops to mills. In the 19th century the wave spread across Europe, the United States, and Japan. Cities swelled and peasants became wage workers; wealth arrived together with slums, child labor, and the first labor movements.`,fact:`The Luddites, who smashed textile machines in England from 1811, were not against technology as such but against machines used to cut wages and skill. Their leader, “Ned Ludd,” probably never existed.`},"tech:ironclads":{quote:`Damn the torpedoes! Full speed ahead!`,quoteBy:`Admiral David Farragut`,desc:`Brings the [[unit:ironclad|Ironclad]], the first armored steamship and master of coastal waters. Optional, but a naval power should not skip it: wooden sailing fleets struggle against it.`,history:`In 1859 France launched La Gloire, the first oceangoing ironclad, and Britain answered with the iron-hulled HMS Warrior. In 1862 the USS Monitor and CSS Virginia fought for hours at Hampton Roads; the duel ended in a draw but showed that wooden ships of the line had become obsolete overnight.`,fact:`HMS Warrior outclassed every warship afloat when she was launched, never fired a shot in anger and was obsolete within a decade. She survives as a museum ship in Portsmouth.`},"tech:mass_production":{quote:`Nothing is particularly hard if you divide it into small jobs.`,quoteBy:`Henry Ford`,desc:`Brings the navy to full strength with [[unit:battleship|battleships]], submarines, [[unit:carrier|carriers]], and the Commercial Dock. Leads to Motorized Transportation and Amphibious War.`,history:`In 1913 Henry Ford introduced the moving assembly line, cutting Model T assembly from about twelve hours to roughly an hour and a half. The car became affordable to the very workers who built it. The method spread to every industry, and in World War II assembly lines turned out tanks, aircraft, and ships at unprecedented speed.`,fact:`In 1914 Ford doubled his workers’ pay to five dollars a day — not out of charity, but because turnover on the monotonous line was so high that keeping trained workers was cheaper.`},"tech:medicine":{quote:`Cure sometimes, treat often, comfort always.`,quoteBy:`attributed to Edward Trudeau`,desc:`Unlocks nothing directly, but opens the way to [[tech:sanitation|Sanitation]] and its hospitals, and to Scientific Method. A quiet but necessary step of the industrial age.`,history:`Medicine is as old as humanity, but it became a modern science around the turn of the 19th century. Edward Jenner introduced smallpox vaccination in 1796, Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch proved that germs cause disease, and Joseph Lister brought antiseptics into surgery. Deaths after operations fell sharply, and doctors finally began washing their hands.`,fact:`In 1847 the Hungarian doctor Ignaz Semmelweis showed that when physicians washed their hands in chlorinated lime, deaths in his maternity ward fell dramatically. Most colleagues ignored him.`},"tech:motorized_transportation":{quote:`The poetry of motion! The real way to travel! The only way to travel!`,quoteBy:`Kenneth Grahame, The Wind in the Willows`,desc:`Brings the [[unit:tank|Tank]] and with it fast, decisive land warfare. Required on the way to Advanced Flight; without it, you march on foot against engines.`,history:`The British first sent tanks into battle on the Somme in 1916: slow and unreliable, but able to cross trenches. Between the wars trucks, tractors, and armored vehicles became common, and armies traded horses for engines. In 1939 and 1940, armored spearheads backed by aircraft showed how fast war could move.`,fact:`Early British tank crews sent messages back to headquarters by carrier pigeon, released through a hatch.`},"tech:nationalism":{quote:`A nation is a daily plebiscite.`,quoteBy:`Ernest Renan`,desc:`Brings [[unit:rifleman|Riflemen]], the draft, war mobilization, mutual protection pacts, and trade embargoes. If a big war is coming, research it early in the era.`,history:`The idea that a state belongs to its nation took hold during the French Revolution, when the levée en masse of 1793 put an entire people under arms. In the 19th century national movements unified Italy and Germany and strained multinational empires. Nationalism inspired struggles for independence and also fed the rivalries that led to the world wars.`,fact:`“We have made Italy; now we must make Italians,” runs a remark attributed to Massimo d’Azeglio after unification — at the time only a small share of the people spoke standard Italian.`},"tech:refining":{quote:`I believe the power to make money is a gift from God.`,quoteBy:`John D. Rockefeller`,desc:`Reveals [[resource:oil|Oil]], the fuel for everything that moves in the new century, and leads to Combustion. Scout the deposits before your rivals do.`,history:`In the 1850s chemists learned to distill crude oil into kerosene, which quickly replaced whale oil in lamps. Edwin Drake drilled the first commercial well in Pennsylvania in 1859, and John D. Rockefeller founded Standard Oil in 1870. With the automobile, gasoline went from a refining byproduct to the main product, and oil became a strategic resource.`,fact:`Petroleum jelly was found on oil rigs: in 1859 a young chemist, Robert Chesebrough, saw oil workers smearing a waxy residue from the pumps on cuts and burns, refined it and sold it as Vaseline.`},"tech:replaceable_parts":{quote:`Ten persons, therefore, could make among them upwards of forty-eight thousand pins in a day.`,quoteBy:`Adam Smith, The Wealth of Nations`,desc:`Workers labor much faster, and you gain [[unit:infantry|Infantry]], Artillery, Guerillas, and access to [[resource:rubber|Rubber]]. A key tech of the era for war and construction alike.`,history:`Parts made so precisely that any one fits any copy were demonstrated by the French gunsmith Honoré Blanc in the 1780s. In the United States, Eli Whitney and the Springfield and Harpers Ferry armories pushed the idea further. Repairs no longer needed a skilled fitter, and standardization paved the way for the assembly line.`,fact:`Even screw threads were once a local custom: every workshop cut its own, and a bolt from one factory might not fit a nut from another until Joseph Whitworth in Britain and William Sellers in America proposed standards.`},"tech:sanitation":{quote:`Cleanliness is indeed next to godliness.`,quoteBy:`John Wesley`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:hospital|Hospital]], which lets big cities grow past their limit, and ends disease on flood plains. Optional, but indispensable for large cities.`,history:`In the 19th century growing cities were ravaged by cholera. In 1854 John Snow traced a London outbreak to a contaminated water pump, and after the Great Stink of 1858 Joseph Bazalgette built London's sewer network. Clean water and proper drainage saved more lives than any medicine of the era.`,fact:`Cities had filtered water since the nineteenth century, but routine chlorination, which began in American cities in 1908, sharply cut deaths from typhoid — one of the quietest triumphs of public health.`},"tech:scientific_method":{quote:`Take nobody’s word for it.`,quoteBy:`motto of the Royal Society`,desc:`Unlocks [[wonder:theory_of_evolution|Theory of Evolution]], a generous research boost for whoever gets there first. Leads on to Atomic Theory and Electronics.`,history:`Francis Bacon, who urged reliance on observation, and Galileo, who tested hypotheses by measurement, laid the foundations of the scientific method. In the 19th century science became a profession, with research laboratories, journals, and rigorous rules for experiment. Hypothesis, test, publication, replication: that cycle sped up discovery more than any single genius.`,fact:`Peer review is younger than it looks: many famous journals began sending every paper to outside referees only in the second half of the twentieth century. In 1936 Einstein withdrew a paper in protest when an American journal sent it to a reviewer.`},"tech:steam_power":{quote:`I sell here, sir, what all the world desires to have — power.`,quoteBy:`Matthew Boulton`,desc:`Reveals [[resource:coal|Coal]] and unlocks [[job:railroad|railroads]], which move troops and goods almost instantly; the Hanging Gardens and Knights Templar fade. Industry starts here.`,history:`Thomas Newcomen's steam pumps were draining mines from 1712, but James Watt made the engine efficient enough for factories between the 1760s and 1780s. The Stockton and Darlington Railway opened in 1825, and steamships soon crossed oceans. Steam was the first power that did not depend on muscle, wind, or rivers.`,fact:`James Watt coined the word “horsepower” to sell his engines to mine owners by comparing them with the horses they would replace. The unit of power named after him now appears on every light bulb.`},"tech:steel":{quote:`The man who dies rich dies disgraced.`,quoteBy:`Andrew Carnegie, The Gospel of Wealth`,desc:`Builds nothing by itself, but together with Refining it leads to [[tech:combustion|Combustion]], and from there to a modern navy and air power. There is no way around it.`,history:`Steel had been made for centuries, but slowly and at great cost. In 1856 Henry Bessemer found a way to blow air through molten pig iron, and the open-hearth process soon produced steel by the thousand tons. Cheap steel gave the world rails, bridges, skyscrapers, and armor plate; by 1900 it was the measure of industrial might.`,fact:`Harry Brearley of Sheffield is usually credited with stainless steel: in 1913 he developed a chromium steel that resisted corrosion and saw its promise for cutlery. Metallurgists elsewhere in Europe and America were working on similar alloys at the same time.`},"tech:the_corporation":{quote:`No soul to be damned, and no body to be kicked.`,quoteBy:`attributed to Lord Thurlow`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:stock_exchange|Stock Exchange]] and [[wonder:wall_street|Wall Street]], pillars of a wealthy empire. It also leads to Steel and Refining, so there is no skipping it.`,history:`Joint-stock companies were nothing new, as the East India companies show, but 19th-century limited liability laws made the corporation an ordinary way of doing business. Investors risked only their stake, and capital poured into railroads, steel, and oil. Some corporations grew richer than states, and the first antitrust laws followed.`,fact:`Some of the world’s oldest companies are family firms: the Japanese hot-spring inn Nishiyama Onsen Keiunkan was run by one family for more than fifty generations, from the early eighth century.`},"tech:computers":{quote:`Pray, Mr. Babbage, if you put into the machine wrong figures, will the right answers come out?`,quoteBy:`a question put to Charles Babbage`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:research_lab|Research Lab]], Mech Infantry, and the [[wonder:seti_program|SETI Program]]. The key science tech of the modern age, leading to Miniaturization and the Laser.`,history:`Charles Babbage designed his Analytical Engine in the 1830s but never managed to build it. The first electronic computers arrived in the 1940s: Britain's Colossus and America's ENIAC, packed with thousands of vacuum tubes. The transistor and the integrated circuit shrank computers from a room to a desk, and then to a pocket.`,fact:`The word “computer” originally meant a person: for centuries, people hired to do calculations by hand were called computers, and during the Second World War many of them were women.`},"tech:ecology":{quote:`When we try to pick out anything by itself, we find it hitched to everything else in the Universe.`,quoteBy:`John Muir`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:solar_plant|Solar Plant]] and [[building:mass_transit_system|Mass Transit]]: industry without the smog. Research it when your cities start choking on pollution.`,history:`Ernst Haeckel coined the word ecology in 1866, but it became a mass movement only a century later. Rachel Carson's Silent Spring (1962) exposed the harm of pesticides, the first Earth Day was held in 1970, and governments began passing laws to protect clean air and water.`,fact:`The ozone hole is an environmental success story: after the 1987 Montreal Protocol phased out the chemicals that destroyed ozone, the layer began a slow recovery that scientists expect to complete within this century.`},"tech:fission":{quote:`The unleashed power of the atom has changed everything save our modes of thinking.`,quoteBy:`Albert Einstein`,desc:`Reveals [[resource:uranium|Uranium]] and unlocks nuclear submarines, [[wonder:the_manhattan_project|the Manhattan Project]], and the United Nations. After this, the world is never the same again.`,history:`In December 1938 Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann found that a uranium nucleus struck by a neutron splits apart; Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch explained the process and named it fission. In 1942 Enrico Fermi started the first nuclear reactor in Chicago, and in 1945 fission was used in atomic bombs.`,fact:`Lise Meitner worked out the physics of fission while walking in the Swedish snow with her nephew Otto Frisch, scribbling calculations on scraps of paper. She was left out of the Nobel Prize, but element 109, meitnerium, bears her name.`},"tech:genetics":{quote:`Endless forms most beautiful and most wonderful have been, and are being, evolved.`,quoteBy:`Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species`,desc:`Unlocks two peaceful wonders, [[wonder:cure_for_cancer|Cure for Cancer]] and [[wonder:longevity|Longevity]]. For a realm already ahead in science and ready to invest in its citizens' well-being.`,history:`In 1953 James Watson and Francis Crick, drawing on Rosalind Franklin's X-ray images, described the double helix of DNA. In 1973 Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer moved a gene from one organism into another, launching genetic engineering. The Human Genome Project finished its map of human DNA in 2003.`,fact:`Gregor Mendel published his work on pea plants in 1866 and drew little attention for decades. Only around 1900 did his laws become famous, when other scientists reached similar results.`},"tech:integrated_defense":{quote:`He who defends everything defends nothing.`,quoteBy:`Frederick the Great`,desc:`Unlocks [[wonder:strategic_missile_defense|Strategic Missile Defense]], a shield against nuclear missiles. The top of the military tree, worth it once rivals have ICBMs.`,history:`Integrated defense links radars, satellites, interceptors, and command centers into a single network. Its ancestor was Britain's Dowding system of 1940, which fed radar reports into one headquarters. The Cold War built NORAD and missile defense programs on that idea, and in 1983 the United States announced the Strategic Defense Initiative.`,fact:`Before radar, Britain built huge concrete “sound mirrors” along its coast to hear approaching aircraft. Several still stand on the shore of Kent.`},"tech:miniaturization":{quote:`So, naturalists observe, a flea hath smaller fleas that on him prey; and these have smaller fleas to bite ’em.`,quoteBy:`Jonathan Swift`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:offshore_platform|Offshore Platform]], which makes sea tiles productive, and [[wonder:the_internet|the Internet]]. Leads to Genetics and Robotics.`,history:`The transistor was invented at Bell Labs in 1947, and in 1958 and 1959 Jack Kilby and Robert Noyce independently created the integrated circuit. In 1965 Gordon Moore observed that the number of components on a chip doubled about every year, later revised to every two. The trend held for half a century and put a computing center in a phone.`,fact:`The computer that guided Apollo to the Moon had a tiny fraction of the memory in any modern phone. Its programs were literally woven by hand into “rope memory,” wires threaded through or around tiny magnetic rings.`},"tech:nuclear_power":{quote:`Human history becomes more and more a race between education and catastrophe.`,quoteBy:`H. G. Wells, The Outline of History`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:nuclear_plant|Nuclear Plant]], a powerful energy source for factory cities. Leads to [[tech:the_laser|the Laser]] and from there to Robotics and Smart Weapons.`,history:`In 1951 the experimental EBR-I reactor in Idaho generated the first electricity from nuclear energy, enough to light four bulbs. In 1954 the Obninsk plant in the Soviet Union became the first connected to a power grid, followed by Calder Hall in Britain in 1956. The accidents at Three Mile Island and Chernobyl later cooled enthusiasm for decades.`,fact:`A banana is very slightly radioactive because of its potassium, and scientists sometimes use the informal “banana equivalent dose” to explain tiny radiation doses to the public.`},"tech:recycling":{quote:`Nature resolves everything into its component atoms and never reduces anything to nothing.`,quoteBy:`Lucretius, On the Nature of Things`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:recycling_center|Recycling Center]], which keeps pollution down in industrial cities. Modest, but useful for any large industrial power.`,history:`People had long remelted metal and turned rags into paper, but recycling became mass practice only in the 20th century. During the world wars governments collected scrap metal and paper for the war effort, and the 1970s brought the first curbside collection programs. The three-arrow recycling symbol was designed in 1970 by a student, Gary Anderson.`,fact:`During the Second World War, American households saved kitchen fat for the war effort: its glycerin went into explosives, and butchers paid a little for every pound brought in.`},"tech:robotics":{quote:`If every instrument could accomplish its own work, chief workmen would not want servants, nor masters slaves.`,quoteBy:`Aristotle, Politics`,desc:`Unlocks the [[building:manufacturing_plant|Manufacturing Plant]], Radar Artillery, the AEGIS Cruiser, and the spaceship's [[wonder:ss_stasis_chamber|Stasis Chamber]]. Peak production and a firm step toward the stars.`,history:`The word robot comes from Karel Čapek's 1920 play R.U.R. The first industrial robot, Unimate, joined a General Motors line in 1961, lifting hot die castings. By the end of the century robots welded car bodies, assembled chips, and rolled across Mars. Unlike Čapek's originals, they have so far shown no interest in revolt, which is exactly what a careful robot would do.`,fact:`The Three Laws of Robotics come not from engineering but from science fiction: Isaac Asimov set them out in a 1942 short story. Roboticists still cite them, mostly to explain why real machines need something better.`},"tech:rocketry":{quote:`The dream of yesterday is the hope of today and the reality of tomorrow.`,quoteBy:`Robert H. Goddard`,desc:`Re-arms your forces wholesale: [[unit:jet_fighter|jet fighters]], TOW infantry, mobile SAMs, cruise missiles, SAM batteries, and [[resource:aluminum|Aluminum]]. Leads to Space Flight.`,history:`Gunpowder rockets were known in China by the 13th century, but Konstantin Tsiolkovsky worked out the theory of rocket flight in 1903. Robert Goddard launched the first liquid-fueled rocket in 1926, and in 1944 the German V-2 became the first to reach the edge of space. After the war rockets became both weapons and the way to orbit.`,fact:`In 1920 The New York Times mocked Robert Goddard for believing a rocket could work in a vacuum, with nothing to push against. In July 1969, as Apollo 11 flew to the Moon, the paper printed a correction.`},"tech:satellites":{quote:`The greater the velocity with which it is projected, the farther it goes before it falls to the Earth.`,quoteBy:`Isaac Newton`,desc:`Reveals the whole map, and unlocks the [[unit:icbm|ICBM]] and the spaceship's [[wonder:ss_thrusters|Thrusters]]. Knowing where everything is makes half of any strategy.`,history:`On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, a polished sphere with four antennas whose beeping signal radio amateurs tracked around the world. Within a few years came satellites for communications, weather, and reconnaissance, and from the 1990s GPS made orbit part of everyday life.`,fact:`The orbit used by many communications satellites, in which a satellite hangs over one spot on Earth, was described in 1945 by the writer Arthur C. Clarke; it is sometimes called the Clarke orbit.`},"tech:smart_weapons":{quote:`War is the realm of uncertainty.`,quoteBy:`Carl von Clausewitz, On War`,desc:`Opens nothing by itself, but together with Satellites it leads to [[tech:integrated_defense|Integrated Defense]] and missile defense. For rulers who fear a nuclear strike.`,history:`Guided bombs were first used in World War II, but they became truly accurate with laser guidance over Vietnam in the early 1970s. The 1991 Gulf War showed the world cruise missiles and bombs that could hit a single building. Later, GPS satellite guidance made precision cheap.`,fact:`GPS, which now guides precision weapons, was built for the military, and for years the signal open to civilians was deliberately degraded. That limitation was switched off in 2000, and car navigation took off.`},"tech:space_flight":{quote:`Provide ship or sails adapted to the heavenly breezes, and there will be some who will brave even that void.`,quoteBy:`Johannes Kepler`,desc:`Unlocks the [[wonder:apollo_program|Apollo Program]], the first spaceship parts, and the tactical nuke. The race to the stars, and to victory, starts here.`,history:`On April 12, 1961, Yuri Gagarin orbited Earth aboard Vostok 1 and became the first human in space. Eight years later, on July 20, 1969, Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed on the Moon during the Apollo program. Space went from science fiction to an arena of rivalry and, later, of cooperation aboard orbital stations.`,fact:`Life insurance for the Apollo 11 crew would have cost a fortune, so before launch the astronauts signed hundreds of envelopes for their families to sell if they did not come back.`},"tech:stealth":{quote:`An invisible man is a man of power.`,quoteBy:`H. G. Wells, The Invisible Man`,desc:`Brings the [[unit:stealth_fighter|Stealth Fighter]] and [[unit:stealth_bomber|Stealth Bomber]], the peak of military aviation. Useful against enemies hiding behind strong air defenses.`,history:`Stealth relies on shapes and coatings that deflect and absorb radar waves. The design drew on equations by Soviet physicist Pyotr Ufimtsev, and Lockheed turned them into the F-117, which first flew in 1981 and was officially acknowledged only in 1988. For years the plane that did not show up on radar did not show up in press releases either.`,fact:`In its early, secret years the F-117 flew only at night, and its strange silhouette is often credited with feeding UFO sightings around its Nevada base.`},"tech:superconductor":{quote:`Through measurement to knowledge.`,quoteBy:`Heike Kamerlingh Onnes`,desc:`Unlocks the spaceship's Fuel Cells and [[wonder:ss_life_support_system|Life Support System]]. Required for the space race and on the road to Integrated Defense.`,history:`In 1911 Heike Kamerlingh Onnes cooled mercury with liquid helium and found that its electrical resistance vanished completely. The explanation came only in 1957 from Bardeen, Cooper, and Schrieffer. High-temperature superconductors were discovered in 1986, and today superconducting magnets run MRI scanners and particle accelerators.`,fact:`MRI scanners, the most common practical use of superconductors, make hospitals major consumers of liquid helium — a gas so light that once released it rises through the atmosphere and eventually escapes into space.`},"tech:synthetic_fibers":{quote:`There might be a way found out to make an artificial glutinous composition, much resembling that out of which the silkworm draws its clue.`,quoteBy:`Robert Hooke, Micrographia`,desc:`Unlocks [[unit:modern_armor|Modern Armor]], Modern Paratroopers, and two spaceship parts, the Storage Supply and Exterior Casing. Matters for war and for the space race alike.`,history:`In 1935 Wallace Carothers synthesized nylon at DuPont, and nylon stockings caused near riots in stores when they went on sale in 1940. Nylon soon went to war as parachute cloth, followed by polyester, acrylic, and Kevlar, created by Stephanie Kwolek in 1965, a fiber stronger than steel of the same weight.`,fact:`The popular story that “nylon” stands for “New York” and “London” is a myth: DuPont explained that the name grew out of earlier proposals such as “No-Run” and was reshaped until it sounded like a word.`},"tech:the_laser":{quote:`Hail, holy Light, offspring of Heaven first-born!`,quoteBy:`John Milton, Paradise Lost`,desc:`Unlocks the spaceship's [[wonder:ss_planetary_party_lounge|Planetary Party Lounge]] and leads to Robotics and Smart Weapons. A key step in the late tree.`,history:`Einstein described stimulated emission in 1917, but Theodore Maiman built the first working laser, using a ruby crystal, only in 1960. Early on it was called a solution looking for a problem. The problems turned up: welding, surgery, fiber-optic communication, barcode scanners, and measuring the distance to the Moon.`,fact:`The mirrors left on the Moon by Apollo crews still bounce back laser pulses fired from Earth. Their echoes show that the Moon is drifting away from us by about an inch and a half a year.`},"unit:ancient_cavalry":{desc:`Mounted troops you can only receive, never build. Good for raids and finishing off weakened units, but uneasy when asked to hold a position.`,history:`Ancient cavalry grew out of chariot warfare: by the mid-first millennium BC riders had pushed chariots off most battlefields. Lacking stirrups and a rigid saddle, horsemen worked the flanks, pursuing, encircling and throwing javelins rather than charging a formed line head-on.`,fact:`Parthian horsemen perfected the “Parthian shot”: feigning retreat, then turning in the saddle to shoot at their pursuers. Whether the English “parting shot” grew out of it is disputed.`},"unit:crusader":{desc:`A heavy shock infantryman you can only be granted, never build. Hits hard but marches slowly, so it shines near your own cities and roads.`,history:`The Crusades began in 1096 and ran on and off for nearly two centuries; participants took a vow and sewed a cross onto their clothing. Crusading hosts mixed knights, squires and large numbers of foot soldiers, and they lost more men to disease, hunger and the long road than to battle.`,fact:`The Fourth Crusade never reached the Holy Land. In 1204 it sacked Constantinople instead, partly to pay off its debts to Venice.`},"unit:leader":{desc:`A great leader emerges from elite victories and never fights. Spend it to rush an important build, or gather an [[unit:army|Army]] around it.`,history:`In every age, wars were often decided by individual commanders able to raise, inspire and hold together a large force. Their authority rested on success: a general with several victorious campaigns behind him could lead men where orders alone would fail.`,fact:`Hannibal crossed the Alps with elephants, but few of them survived the following winter. By most accounts only one lived on, perhaps the one named Surus.`},"unit:scientific_leader":{desc:`Three choices, one scientist: finish any build at once, start an age of science in the capital, or found an [[building:academy|Academy]] where he stands. A moment, a season, or forever.`,history:`For much of history, science advanced through individuals, from Archimedes and al-Khwarizmi to Newton and Curie, whose work reshaped how whole eras understood the world. Their discoveries were often grasped only decades later, and many academies and laboratories grew up around such figures.`,fact:`Archimedes is said to have run naked through Syracuse shouting “Eureka!” after a bath. The story comes from Vitruvius, writing two centuries later, so it may be embellished; the principle of buoyancy is real.`},"unit:archer":{desc:`The first shooter: hits harder than a warrior and fires on attackers from inside a city. Fragile up close; later gives way to the [[unit:longbowman|Longbowman]].`,history:`The bow is one of humanity's oldest complex tools: stone-tipped arrows go back tens of thousands of years. In the armies of Egypt, Assyria and Persia, archers formed the backbone of the host, wearing the enemy down before the infantry closed in.`,fact:`Archery left English several idioms: “a second string to your bow,” and “upshot,” which was originally the final shot of an archery match.`},"unit:army":{desc:`Several units under a [[unit:leader|Leader]], fighting as one. An Army shrugs off blows that would break any single unit and grinds through the most stubborn defenses.`,history:`Ancient states learned early to merge scattered warbands into standing armies with unified command, supply and discipline. Assyria, Rome and Qin China all showed that a coordinated force wins through organization rather than sheer numbers.`,fact:`Napoleon organized his forces into corps, each a small army with its own infantry, cavalry and artillery, so that they could march apart and fight together.`},"unit:bowman":{desc:`Babylon's take on the [[unit:archer|Archer]]: it strikes just as hard but defends noticeably better, so it can both raid and guard cities in the early centuries.`,history:`Babylonian armies relied on archers and chariots, and the city's massive walls needed bowmen to man them. Under Hammurabi and later in the Neo-Babylonian kingdom of Nebuchadnezzar II, archers served both on the ramparts and in the field.`,fact:`The composite bows of the ancient Near East were built of wood, horn and sinew glued together. Damp weather could soften the glue, so archers carried their bows in protective cases.`},"unit:catapult":{desc:`A siege engine that never charges in but pounds the adjacent tile, softening defenders and wrecking improvements. Helpless without an escort; later gives way to the [[unit:trebuchet|Trebuchet]].`,history:`Tradition credits the catapult to Syracuse around 399 BC, built for the tyrant Dionysius. Greek and later Roman engineers developed torsion engines that hurled stones and bolts using the force of twisted sinew and hair.`,fact:`The Greek katapeltes means roughly “shield-piercer”: the first catapults were giant crossbows that shot bolts, not stones.`},"unit:chariot":{desc:`The first fast unit: it outruns infantry and is handy for scouting and raiding workers, but weak in a real fight and unhappy off the roads. Requires [[resource:horses|Horses]].`,history:`War chariots appeared on the steppe around 2000 BC and within a few centuries spread from Egypt to China. A light, spoke-wheeled car carried a driver and an archer, and long remained a symbol of nobility and royal power.`,fact:`Chariot racing in Constantinople was so passionate that the Blue and Green racing factions became political forces. Their riot of 532, the Nika riot, nearly toppled Emperor Justinian.`},"unit:chasqui_scout":{desc:`The Inca version of the [[unit:scout|Scout]]: it can hold its own in a skirmish and crosses hills and mountains where others bog down. Ideal for exploring rugged country.`,history:`Chasquis were the relay runners of the Inca Empire, carrying messages along thousands of miles of mountain roads. Handing off at tambo way stations, they could move news from Cusco to the coast in days, with knotted quipu cords recording the numbers.`,fact:`The Incas kept records not in writing but on khipu, knotted cords whose colors, positions and knots stored numbers and perhaps more. Scholars still have not fully deciphered them.`},"unit:curragh":{desc:`The first boat: a cheap way to chart the coastline and nearby islands. Open sea is best admired from the shore.`,history:`The curragh is an Irish boat of hides stretched over a wicker frame. Legend has Saint Brendan sailing one into the Atlantic, and Irish monks did reach the Faroes and Iceland in such craft. The design worked well enough that people still build them today.`,fact:`Curraghs are still built and raced off the west coast of Ireland, these days usually covered with tarred canvas instead of ox hide.`},"unit:dromon":{desc:`Byzantium's answer to the [[unit:galley|Galley]]: hits harder, carries troops, and can bombard from the sea with lethal effect. The strongest ancient navy afloat.`,history:`The dromon was the main Byzantine warship from the 6th to the 12th century, a two-banked galley with oars and lateen sails. Its most feared weapon was Greek fire, an incendiary mixture sprayed through a siphon that kept burning even on water.`,fact:`Emperor Constantine VII advised his son to tell foreigners who asked for Greek fire that its secret had been given by an angel and must never leave the empire.`},"unit:enkidu_warrior":{desc:`Sumeria's replacement for the [[unit:warrior|Warrior]] and spearman: cheap, available from the start, and a far better city guard than a plain warrior.`,history:`Enkidu is the wild man of the Epic of Gilgamesh who becomes the king of Uruk's friend and companion. Sumerian city-states fielded spearmen with large shields in a dense phalanx, as shown on the famous Stele of the Vultures from Lagash.`,fact:`The Stele of the Vultures, which shows Sumerian spearmen locked in a phalanx, is also one of the oldest known records of a border dispute — over irrigated fields between Lagash and Umma.`},"unit:galley":{desc:`The transport of antiquity: ferries a small force along the coast and to nearby islands. Hugs the shore, since open water can easily sink it.`,history:`Oar-driven galleys ruled the Mediterranean for thousands of years, from the Phoenicians and Greeks to the Venetians. They did not depend on the wind, but their low sides handled storms badly, so they stayed close to land.`,fact:`A full-size replica of an Athenian trireme, the Olympias, was built in the 1980s and rowed by volunteers. Its speed convinced historians that the ancient accounts were not exaggerated.`},"unit:gallic_swordsman":{desc:`The Celtic [[unit:swordsman|Swordsman]]: just as dangerous on the attack but much quicker, striking before the enemy can organize a defense.`,history:`Celtic warriors were known for long iron swords and headlong charges; in 390 BC the Gauls sacked Rome. Gallic smiths were among the best in Europe, and the Romans later borrowed their mail armor.`,fact:`Polybius claimed that Gallic swords bent after a blow and had to be straightened underfoot. Archaeology tells a kinder story: many Celtic blades were very well made.`},"unit:hoplite":{desc:`Greece's replacement for the spearman: the finest defender of its age, able to hold a city against horsemen and swordsmen alike. Do not ask it to attack.`,history:`Hoplites were heavily armed citizen-soldiers of the Greek city-states, carrying a large round shield and a spear. The phalanx relied on each man's shield covering his neighbor, and at Marathon in 490 BC and Plataea in 479 BC it stopped Persian armies.`,fact:`The marathon race honors a legend: a runner who carried news of the victory to Athens and died after delivering it. Herodotus does not mention that run; he tells of a runner sent to Sparta for help instead.`},"unit:horseman":{desc:`A fast, cheap rider for scouting, raids and pursuit. Attacks with confidence but defends poorly. Requires [[resource:horses|Horses]].`,history:`Mounted combat spread in the early first millennium BC, as Scythians, Assyrians and Persians learned to shoot and fight from horseback. A rider was cheaper than a chariot, went where a cart could not, and soon made the chariot obsolete.`,fact:`Nailed iron horseshoes became common in Europe only in the Middle Ages. Before that, some Romans fitted their horses with strap-on iron “hipposandals.”`},"unit:immortals":{desc:`Persia's [[unit:swordsman|Swordsman]]: it strikes harder than any infantry of its day, letting Persia wage an early war of conquest.`,history:`Herodotus called the elite Achaemenid corps of ten thousand the Immortals: every fallen man was replaced at once, so the number never dropped. Armed with spears, bows and wicker shields, they fought at Thermopylae in 480 BC.`,fact:`The name “Immortals” may be a mistake: one theory holds that Herodotus confused the Persian word for “companions” with a similar one meaning “immortal.”`},"unit:impi":{desc:`The Zulu spearman: just as solid on defense but far more mobile, able to plug a gap wherever it opens.`,history:`Impi were the regiments of the Zulu army, reorganized by Shaka in the early 19th century. He introduced the short stabbing assegai and the "horns of the buffalo" formation that enveloped an enemy. At Isandlwana in 1879, impi destroyed a British force.`,fact:`During the battle of Isandlwana in 1879 there was a partial eclipse of the Sun, a detail remembered by several survivors.`},"unit:jaguar_warrior":{desc:`A cheap, fast Aztec foot soldier available very early. Good for scouting and early raids, but not for holding ground.`,history:`Jaguar warriors were an elite Aztec military order open to men who had captured several enemies in battle. They wore jaguar skins and wielded the macuahuitl, a wooden club edged with obsidian, aiming to take prisoners rather than kill.`,fact:`The Aztecs called the jaguar ocelotl. English borrowed that word, by a slip, for a different and much smaller cat: the ocelot.`},"unit:javelin_thrower":{desc:`The Maya [[unit:archer|Archer]]: tougher on defense, and it enslaves defeated foes, turning each skirmish into extra hands for your workforce.`,history:`Maya warriors fought with darts, spears and the atlatl, a spear-thrower that cast a missile farther and harder. Wars between city-states were often fought to seize captives, who were then put to work or offered in rituals.`,fact:`Mesoamerican ballplayers used solid rubber balls made by mixing latex with the juice of morning glory vines — a recipe chemists have reproduced in the lab.`},"unit:legionary":{desc:`Rome's [[unit:swordsman|Swordsman]]: it attacks just as well but defends like a spearman. It takes territory and then keeps it.`,history:`The Roman legionary was a professional foot soldier with a scutum shield, a short gladius and throwing pila. After the Marian reforms around 107 BC the legions became a standing army, and legionaries built roads, bridges and camps as well as fighting.`,fact:`Roman legionaries were barred from marrying while in service until about AD 197. Many had unofficial families anyway, and the law eventually had to catch up.`},"unit:mounted_warrior":{desc:`The Iroquois take on the [[unit:horseman|Horseman]]: noticeably harder-hitting while just as fast. An excellent raider for early wars.`,history:`Horses reached North America with Europeans, and by the 18th century many peoples of the eastern woodlands and plains fought on horseback. The Iroquois Confederacy of five, later six, nations was one of the most powerful forces in the continent's northeast.`,fact:`Iroquois women held real political power: clan mothers chose the chiefs and could remove them from office.`},"unit:numidian_mercenary":{desc:`Carthage's spearman: sturdier than the standard model and even able to counterattack. A reliable defensive anchor until the age of gunpowder.`,history:`Carthage kept a small citizen army and hired heavily from subject and neighboring peoples. Numidians from North Africa were famed for light cavalry and steady infantry, and they fought in Hannibal's army at Cannae in 216 BC.`,fact:`The Numidian king Masinissa switched sides and helped Rome win at Zama in 202 BC. He reigned for more than half a century and was said to have fathered a son in his eighties.`},"unit:scout":{desc:`A fast, defenseless explorer for the opening turns: it reveals the map, finds villages and spots good city sites. Keep it out of fights.`,history:`Scouting is among the oldest military trades: before marching, armies sent men ahead to learn the roads, fords and enemy strength. Good scouts were valued above many warriors, since knowing the ground often decided a campaign.`,fact:`Roman scouts were called exploratores, the forerunners of every later “explorer.”`},"unit:settler":{desc:`The founder of new cities and the main tool of expansion. It is expensive and costs you population, but each new city repays it many times over.`,history:`Colonization drove history: Phoenicians and Greeks founded dozens of cities around the Mediterranean, and later settlers spread across Siberia and the American West. A new town began with a well, a wall and a first harvest. Most of the world map was drawn by people with a wagon and a bad idea that worked.`,fact:`Many Greek colonies are still living cities: Marseille began as Massalia, Naples as Neapolis — literally “new city” — and Istanbul as Byzantium.`},"unit:spearman":{desc:`The first true defender: cheap, a solid city guard against early attacks, and especially good against mounted troops. Pointless on the offensive.`,history:`The spear was the most common weapon of the ancient world: easy to make, and a line of spearmen could stop both infantry and chariots. Bronze made the points tougher, and dense formations from Sumer to Greece turned spearmen into the core of armies.`,fact:`The bayonet was a way of turning a musket back into a spear. Long after the pike vanished, infantry still trained to receive cavalry behind a hedge of points.`},"unit:swordsman":{desc:`The main assault infantry of antiquity: a strong attacker for early wars, able to take cities. Mediocre on defense. Requires [[resource:iron|Iron]].`,history:`Swords became mass weapons once iron grew cheaper than bronze. A short blade demanded training and close contact, so swordsmen served as shock troops. Roman, Celtic and Chinese soldiers with iron swords decided many ancient battles.`,fact:`The Roman gladius gave its name to the gladiator, and also to the gladiolus — a “little sword,” named for its blade-shaped leaves.`},"unit:three_man_chariot":{desc:`The Hittite [[unit:chariot|Chariot]]: noticeably better both on attack and defense, letting the Hittites open an early war with an edge in speed and strength.`,history:`The Hittites were known for heavy chariots with a three-man crew: a driver, a spearman and a shield-bearer. At Kadesh around 1274 BC, thousands of Hittite chariots nearly destroyed the army of Ramesses II.`,fact:`The oldest known horse-training manual was written for the Hittites by a horse master from Mitanni named Kikkuli. Modern experiments have tested his interval-training regime on real horses.`},"unit:war_chariot":{desc:`Egypt's [[unit:chariot|Chariot]]: a stronger attacker at the same price, making Egypt's early expansion more dangerous for its neighbors.`,history:`The chariot reached Egypt with the Hyksos and became the main weapon of the New Kingdom. Light Egyptian chariots carried a driver and an archer and relied on speed and firepower; pharaohs had themselves carved riding one to victory.`,fact:`Ramesses II had the battle of Kadesh carved on temple walls across Egypt as a glorious victory, though it ended in a stalemate.`},"unit:warrior":{desc:`The cheapest unit of the opening: guards a city, scouts the area and fends off barbarians. Ages fast and gives way to the [[unit:swordsman|Swordsman]].`,history:`Long before armies there were armed tribesmen with clubs, axes and spears. They defended herds and hunting grounds, raided neighbors and followed a chief. In time such war bands gave rise to the first professional forces.`,fact:`English borrowed “war” from Old French, which took it from a Germanic word meaning “confusion.” Anyone who has seen a battle would agree.`},"unit:worker":{desc:`Builds roads, irrigation, mines and everything else that makes land worth having. Without workers, even a great capital is a village with ambitions.`,history:`Every great kingdom stood on the labor of builders: the canals of Mesopotamia, the roads of Rome and the terraces of the Inca were made by countless unnamed hands. Their tools barely changed for centuries: a hoe, a basket, a shovel and patience.`,fact:`Gangs who built the Great Pyramid left their own names painted on the hidden blocks, such as “Friends of Khufu.” The pyramid has a signature, just not the one you expect.`},"unit:ansar_warrior":{desc:`Arabia's [[unit:knight|Knight]]: faster than any rider of its era, able to strike deep and withdraw before the enemy can respond.`,history:`The Ansar were the supporters of the Prophet Muhammad in Medina; the word later came to mean devoted fighters. In the 7th century, Arab light cavalry on swift horses and camels advanced within a few decades from Arabia to Spain and Persia.`,fact:`Arabic gave English part of its military vocabulary: “admiral” comes from amir al-, “commander of,” and “arsenal” from dar as-sina‘a, “house of manufacture.”`},"unit:berserk":{desc:`The Scandinavian replacement for the [[unit:longbowman|Longbowman]]: a fearsome attacker that can storm ashore straight from a ship and assault coastal cities.`,history:`Berserkers were warriors of the Norse sagas who fought in a trance-like fury, reputedly feeling no pain. The name probably means "bear shirt." From the 9th century, Vikings raided the coasts of Europe, landing straight from their longships.`,fact:`The Lewis chessmen, carved from walrus ivory in the twelfth century, include rooks shown as berserkers biting the tops of their shields.`},"unit:cannon":{desc:`Gunpowder artillery that breaks walls and thins out defenders far better than the [[unit:trebuchet|Trebuchet]]. Cannot defend itself and needs an escort.`,history:`Cannon appeared in China and Europe in the 14th century, and by the 15th they decided sieges: in 1453 huge bombards breached the walls of Constantinople. Medieval fortresses had to be rebuilt with low, angled bastions to survive gunfire. Castle architects took it personally.`,fact:`The Dardanelles Gun, a bronze bombard cast for the Ottomans in 1464, was given to Queen Victoria in 1866. It now sits at Fort Nelson in England, in two pieces that screw together.`},"unit:caravel":{desc:`The first ship you can trust along foreign coasts: faster and roomier than a galley. Deep ocean is still a gamble.`,history:`Caravels were small, nimble ships with lateen sails, developed by the Portuguese in the 15th century to sail down the African coast. They handled well against the wind; Bartolomeu Dias sailed caravels, and two of Columbus's three ships in 1492 were caravels.`,fact:`Portugal tried to keep its sea routes secret, and charts of the African coast were treated as state property. Historians still argue over how strict that secrecy was.`},"unit:carrack":{desc:`Portugal's [[unit:caravel|Caravel]]: tougher in a fight and unafraid of the open ocean, so Portugal can seek new lands overseas before anyone else.`,history:`The carrack was a large multi-masted ship with high castles fore and aft, the workhorse of the Age of Discovery. Portuguese carracks sailed around Africa to India, and the Victoria, the first ship to circle the globe with Magellan's expedition, was a small carrack.`,fact:`In 1592 the English captured the Portuguese carrack Madre de Deus. Her cargo of spices, silks and jewels was worth nearly half of England’s royal treasury.`},"unit:cavalry":{desc:`The strongest mounted troops before engines: fast, far-ranging strikers for deep raids and for seizing lightly held cities.`,history:`Cavalry of the musket age fought with sabers, pistols and carbines. Napoleon's cuirassiers, hussars and dragoons won battles with headlong charges, and horsemen stayed relevant until World War I, when machine guns ended the cavalry charge.`,fact:`The word “hussar” came from Hungary, and so did the flamboyant uniform, with a fur-trimmed jacket slung over one shoulder, which spread to armies across Europe.`},"unit:conquistador":{desc:`Spain's [[unit:explorer|Explorer]]: moves through any terrain just as easily but can also fight. A scout that can claim what it finds.`,history:`Conquistadors were 16th-century Spanish soldier-adventurers who toppled the Aztec and Inca empires. The small forces of Cortes and Pizarro relied on horses, steel, firearms, local allies and the epidemics that ravaged the Americas ahead of them.`,fact:`Silver from Potosí flooded the world: the Spanish “piece of eight” became the first global currency and the model for the US dollar.`},"unit:cossack":{desc:`Russia's [[unit:cavalry|Cavalry]]: it can attack several times per turn, cutting through a chain of weakened units and breaking deep into a defense.`,history:`Cossacks were free military communities on Russia's southern and eastern frontiers, known from the 15th century. Superb horsemen, they guarded borders, ranged far afield and helped open Siberia; in 1812 Cossack regiments harried Napoleon's retreating army.`,fact:`The Zaporozhian Cossacks’ legendary reply to the Ottoman sultan, a letter of colorful insults, inspired Ilya Repin’s famous painting. Whether the letter itself is authentic is still debated.`},"unit:explorer":{desc:`A defenseless but tireless traveler that treats all terrain like a road. Built to open up the interiors of continents quickly.`,history:`The Age of Discovery made exploring a profession: Magellan, Cook, La Perouse and dozens of others sailed past the edges of known maps. Their expeditions, often state-funded, turned blank spaces into routes for merchants and settlers.`,fact:`Lewis and Clark crossed North America and back with the loss of just one man, probably to appendicitis — an extraordinary record for an expedition of its kind.`},"unit:frigate":{desc:`A fast warship that can bombard coasts and enemy vessels. Good for guarding sea lanes and pressuring coastal cities.`,history:`The frigate was a fast three-masted ship with a single gun deck, the "eyes of the fleet" from the 17th to the 19th century. Frigates scouted, escorted convoys and hunted merchant ships; the famous USS Constitution was launched in 1797.`,fact:`USS Constitution earned the nickname “Old Ironsides” in 1812, when British shot was seen bouncing off her thick oak hull. She is still a commissioned ship of the US Navy.`},"unit:galleon":{desc:`A roomy ocean transport that carries a large landing force across open sea. Barely fights itself and needs an escort.`,history:`The galleon was a large sailing ship of the 16th and 17th centuries, combining a cargo hold with gun decks. Spain's treasure fleets carried American silver in galleons, and the Manila galleons linked Mexico and the Philippines for over two centuries.`,fact:`The Manila galleons carried Chinese silk and porcelain to Mexico in exchange for American silver, forming the first lasting trade route across the Pacific.`},"unit:hwacha":{desc:`Korea's [[unit:cannon|Cannon]]: needs no iron and its barrage is lethal, finishing off units an ordinary bombardment would only wound.`,history:`The hwach'a was a Korean cart-mounted launcher that fired up to a hundred gunpowder-propelled arrows in one volley. At Haengju in 1593, hwach'a helped a small garrison repel a Japanese army many times its size.`,fact:`A hwach’a fired singijeon, “magic machine arrows,” each carrying a small gunpowder tube. Modern reconstructions have fired them successfully.`},"unit:keshik":{desc:`The Mongol [[unit:knight|Knight]]: cheaper, needs no iron, and crosses hills and mountains where heavy cavalry bogs down.`,history:`The keshig was the imperial guard of the Mongol khans, founded by Genghis Khan; it drew on sons of the nobility and served as both bodyguard and a school for commanders. Mongol horse archers with several remounts each covered distances faster than any army of their age.`,fact:`Mongol envoys carried a paiza, a metal tablet of authority. Anyone who showed one could demand horses and lodging anywhere in the empire, as Marco Polo described.`},"unit:knight":{desc:`The heavy cavalry of the Middle Ages: strong, mobile and reasonably sturdy. The main striking force before gunpowder. Requires [[resource:horses|Horses]] and iron.`,history:`The knight was an armored horseman who held land in return for service to a lord. In the 11th to 13th centuries a charge with couched lances could sweep infantry away, but longbows, pikes and gunpowder gradually ended the knight's dominance.`,fact:`The word “knight” comes from Old English cniht, “boy, servant.” Its German cousin, Knecht, still means “farmhand.”`},"unit:longbowman":{desc:`A powerful medieval missile unit: dangerous on the attack and, when defending a city, it showers attackers with arrows, but it is helpless in melee.`,history:`The English longbow, a yew stave as tall as a man, took years of practice to master and could pierce armor at long range. In the Hundred Years' War, longbowmen decided the battles of Crecy in 1346 and Agincourt in 1415.`,fact:`Archaeologists can recognize longbowmen by their skeletons: years of drawing heavy bows left thickened, uneven shoulder and arm bones, as found among the crew of the Mary Rose.`},"unit:man_o_war":{desc:`England's answer to the [[unit:frigate|frigate]]: it hits harder, pounds coastlines more often, and sometimes sails defeated ships home as prizes. Master of the seas until steam arrives.`,history:`Man-of-war was the English term for a powerful sailing warship from the 16th to the 19th century. Ships of the line carried two or three gun decks and anywhere from seventy to over a hundred cannon. The most famous, HMS Victory, was Nelson's flagship at Trafalgar in 1805 and is still afloat in Portsmouth.`,fact:`After Trafalgar, Nelson’s body was carried home in a cask of brandy. The tale that sailors drank from it, giving navy rum the nickname “Nelson’s blood,” is just that — a tale.`},"unit:medieval_infantry":{desc:`Heavy foot for the offensive: it succeeds the [[unit:swordsman|swordsman]] and does respectable work against cities, but leave the holding to pikemen. Requires iron.`,history:`After Rome fell, cavalry dominated European warfare for centuries, but by the 1300s infantry was back. Flemish militia crushed French knights at Courtrai in 1302, and English men-at-arms increasingly fought dismounted. Swords, axes, maces and halberds made a well-drilled foot soldier dangerous even to an armored rider.`,fact:`The Bayeux Tapestry, which shows the infantry and cavalry of 1066, is not actually a tapestry but an embroidery — about 230 feet of it.`},"unit:musketeer":{desc:`France's take on the [[unit:musketman|musketman]]: noticeably sturdier on defense and quick to return fire. The best city guard of its age.`,history:`The King's Musketeers were a guard company raised by Louis XIII in 1622. They fought both mounted and on foot, and a place in the company was a prized start for young nobles. Dumas gave them literary immortality; the treasury proved less sentimental, disbanding the company in 1776 to save money.`,fact:`The real d’Artagnan had a less romantic job than his literary double: in 1661 it was he who arrested the finance minister Nicolas Fouquet and guarded him for years afterward.`},"unit:musketman":{desc:`The first gunpowder defender: it holds a city far better than a pikeman, but don't send it to attack. Requires [[resource:saltpeter|saltpeter]].`,history:`Handheld firearms reached Europe in the 15th century with the arquebus, followed in the 16th by the heavier musket. Reloading was slow, so shooters stood behind pikemen, the formula behind the Spanish tercio. By the late 1600s the flintlock and the bayonet had made the pike obsolete.`,fact:`“Lock, stock and barrel,” meaning everything, names the three main parts of a musket. A “flash in the pan” was priming powder that burned without firing the gun.`},"unit:pikeman":{desc:`A cheap, dependable defender for the age of knights and a worthy successor to the [[unit:spearman|spearman]]. It barely attacks; its job is to stand there.`,history:`The long pike goes back to the Macedonian phalanx and was revived in the Middle Ages by the Scots and the Swiss. Scottish schiltrons held off English cavalry at Bannockburn in 1314, and Swiss squares were Europe's most feared infantry in the 1400s. Pikemen stayed in the line until the bayonet arrived around 1700.`,fact:`Pike-and-shot armies needed so much coordination that illustrated drill manuals, showing every posture step by step, were copied all over seventeenth-century Europe — among the first modern military textbooks.`},"unit:privateer":{desc:`A raider flying no colors: it sinks merchants and warships without a declaration of war, leaving victims guessing whom to blame. Weak in a fair fight, which it avoids.`,history:`A privateer sailed under a letter of marque, a government license to seize enemy ships and split the proceeds with the crown. Francis Drake and many others operated this way, and their victims saw little difference from piracy. The distinction mattered mostly to whoever signed the paperwork. The Declaration of Paris of 1856 outlawed privateering among most naval powers.`,fact:`The US Constitution still gives Congress the power to “grant letters of marque and reprisal,” a clause that has gone unused for about two centuries.`},"unit:rider":{desc:`China's replacement for the [[unit:knight|knight]]: faster than ordinary heavy horse, just as strong. Ideal for deep raids and finishing off battered armies.`,history:`The earliest known depictions of paired stirrups come from 4th-century China, an invention that reshaped mounted combat across Eurasia. The Tang dynasty ran vast state stud farms and bought horses from steppe neighbors by the tens of thousands. Its cavalry, heavy and light, carried Tang power deep into Central Asia.`,fact:`Emperor Taizong of Tang had six of his favorite war horses carved in stone reliefs for his tomb. Two of them are now in a museum in Philadelphia.`},"unit:samurai":{desc:`Japan's replacement for the [[unit:knight|knight]]: a foot warrior who strikes like cavalry and defends noticeably better. Needs no horses, only iron.`,history:`The samurai emerged between the 10th and 12th centuries as mounted archers serving the aristocracy, and from 1185 military shoguns effectively ruled Japan. The familiar code of bushido was largely written down later, during the peaceful Edo period. The Meiji reforms abolished the class, and an 1876 edict banned the wearing of swords.`,fact:`In 1876 Japan banned the wearing of swords in public — one of several Meiji reforms that took apart the privileges of the samurai class.`},"unit:sipahi":{desc:`The Ottoman replacement for [[unit:cavalry|cavalry]]: it hits far harder and pins down enemies that wander too close. The finest shock troops of their day.`,history:`Sipahis were the heavy cavalry of the Ottoman Empire. Provincial sipahis held a timar, the right to land revenues, in exchange for serving with their own retainers, while separate sipahi regiments served the sultan's household. Their importance faded as standing armies grew, and Mahmud II abolished the timar system in 1831.`,fact:`The Ottoman “sipahi” and the British-Indian “sepoy” share a Persian root meaning “soldier” — the same word, carried by two empires.`},"unit:swiss_mercenary":{desc:`The Dutch replacement for the [[unit:pikeman|pikeman]]: same price, sturdier defense. Holds cities well until muskets arrive.`,history:`Swiss pikemen made their name destroying the armies of Charles the Bold of Burgundy at Grandson and Morat in 1476. Kings and popes across Europe hired them afterward, and the saying "no money, no Swiss" summed up the contract terms. The Papal Swiss Guard has served in the Vatican since 1506.`,fact:`The Swiss Guard swears in its recruits each year on May 6, the anniversary of the Sack of Rome in 1527, when most of the guard died covering the pope’s escape.`},"unit:trebuchet":{desc:`Siege engine for cracking walls and wearing down garrisons before the assault. Stronger than the [[unit:catapult|catapult]], but helpless on its own; leave it unguarded and someone else gets a trebuchet.`,history:`The traction trebuchet, pulled by a crew, came from China; the counterweight version appeared in the Mediterranean in the 12th century and could hurl stones weighing hundreds of pounds. At Stirling in 1304 Edward I built a giant engine called the Warwolf and refused the castle's surrender until he had tried it out. The garrison did surrender. The test went ahead anyway.`,fact:`A counterweight trebuchet is a pure gravity machine, which is why physics teachers love it: building a small one is a classic school lesson in potential energy turning into motion.`},"unit:war_elephant":{desc:`India's replacement for the [[unit:knight|knight]]: it needs neither horses nor iron, and grows tougher than ordinary cavalry with experience. A great pick when strategic resources are scarce.`,history:`War elephants served Indian armies from the first millennium BC. In 326 BC King Porus fielded them against Alexander at the Hydaspes, and Chandragupta Maurya later traded five hundred elephants to Seleucus for territory. Elephants remained part of the Delhi Sultanate's and the Mughals' armies well into the age of cannon.`,fact:`Carthage’s war elephants were probably not the African bush elephants we know, but a smaller North African forest elephant that has since died out.`},"unit:artillery":{desc:`Long-range guns of the industrial age: they shell the enemy from a distance and soften defenses before an assault. Defenseless themselves, so keep them covered.`,history:`In the second half of the 19th century rifled breech-loading guns dramatically extended range and accuracy. The French 75 of 1897, with its recoil-absorbing mechanism, could fire rapidly without re-aiming and set the pattern for decades. In World War I artillery caused more battlefield casualties than any other weapon.`,fact:`The “Paris Gun” of 1918 shelled Paris from about 75 miles away. Its shells flew so high and so long that the gunners had to allow for the rotation of the Earth.`},"unit:battleship":{desc:`The mightiest surface ship: it rules the waves and flattens coastal defenses well before a landing. Expensive, and vulnerable to submarines and aircraft.`,history:`In 1906 Britain's HMS Dreadnought, with uniform big guns and steam turbines, made every existing battleship obsolete overnight. The great clash of battle fleets came at Jutland in 1916. World War II proved air power stronger: battleships were sunk from the air at Pearl Harbor, and Japan's giant Yamato fell to carrier aircraft in 1945.`,fact:`The Iowa-class battleships fired their guns in combat from the Second World War to the Gulf War of 1991 — nearly half a century of service.`},"unit:bomber":{desc:`Long-range air power: it hammers troops, cities and supply lines far behind the front, and can wipe targets out entirely. Without fighter cover it bleeds.`,history:`Strategic bombing began in World War I with Zeppelins and Gotha bombers raiding London. In World War II fleets of B-17 Flying Fortresses and Avro Lancasters leveled industrial cities. Historians still debate how much it actually shortened the war.`,fact:`Night bombers of the Second World War navigated by radio beams, and both sides fought a hidden “battle of the beams,” bending and jamming each other’s signals.`},"unit:carrier":{desc:`A floating airfield that brings aircraft to any coastline. It barely fights on its own, so without [[unit:destroyer|destroyers]] and cruisers in escort it's an easy target.`,history:`Britain's HMS Argus got the first full-length flight deck in 1918, and Japan's Hosho, commissioned in 1922, was the first ship designed from the keel up as a carrier. At Midway in 1942 the opposing fleets never came within sight of each other; aircraft decided everything. From then on the carrier replaced the battleship as the capital ship of the fleet.`,fact:`In 1942 the Doolittle Raid launched army bombers from the deck of USS Hornet. They could take off from a carrier but could not land on one, so every crew flew on to China or beyond.`},"unit:cruiser":{desc:`A fast, sturdy escort: it shepherds transports, chases light ships off and shells the shore. Later upgrades to the [[unit:aegis_cruiser|AEGIS Cruiser]].`,history:`The name comes from cruising, independent operations far from the main fleet. Protected and armored cruisers of the late 19th century could patrol trade routes across the globe. The Washington Naval Treaty of 1922 capped heavy cruisers at 10,000 tons and 8-inch guns, and navies promptly built right up to the limit.`,fact:`The German “pocket battleships” of the 1930s were officially armored ships, Panzerschiffe, squeezed under treaty limits with diesel engines and heavy guns. In 1940 the survivors were reclassified as heavy cruisers.`},"unit:destroyer":{desc:`A fast, affordable warship that can spot submarines. The fleet's best sub hunter and scout, and the successor to the [[unit:ironclad|ironclad]].`,history:`Destroyers appeared in the 1890s as torpedo-boat destroyers; Britain's HMS Havock of 1893 was the first. They soon carried torpedoes themselves and became the workhorses of every navy. In World War II destroyers armed with sonar and depth charges guarded Atlantic convoys against U-boats.`,fact:`In 1940 Britain received fifty old American destroyers in exchange for leases on naval bases — one of the first steps toward the wartime alliance.`},"unit:fighter":{desc:`An air superiority fighter: it intercepts enemy aircraft and escorts your bombers. Weak against ground targets. Later becomes the [[unit:jet_fighter|Jet Fighter]].`,history:`Air combat was born in World War I, when Fokker's synchronization gear in 1915 let a machine gun fire through a spinning propeller. By World War II fighters could decide entire campaigns: in the Battle of Britain in 1940, Spitfires and Hurricanes stopped Germany from gaining the air control it needed to invade.`,fact:`The Red Baron is remembered in a red Fokker triplane, but he scored most of his victories in Albatros biplanes.`},"unit:flak":{desc:`An anti-aircraft gun to shield cities from air raids: it fires on any plane striking its tile. Nearly useless on the ground. Its entire outlook on life is up.`,history:`Flak is short for the German Flugabwehrkanone, anti-aircraft cannon. AA guns first appeared in World War I, and by World War II whole belts of batteries ringed major cities. The famous German 88 mm gun proved so effective that it was turned against tanks as well. The American proximity fuze greatly improved the odds of hitting anything.`,fact:`Anti-aircraft shells did not have to hit a plane. Early ones burst at a preset height; the proximity fuze, which set a shell off as it passed near its target, was one of the war’s best-kept secrets.`},"unit:guerilla":{desc:`A stubborn fighter that needs no resources and serves for defense and raids alike: it fires on attackers, and in a pinch a city can draft it on the spot.`,history:`Guerrilla, Spanish for little war, entered common use during the Spanish resistance to Napoleon's armies in 1808–1814. In the 20th century guerrilla warfare grew into a full military doctrine: small bands avoided pitched battles and wore down far stronger opponents with raids and ambushes.`,fact:`Spanish guerrilla means “little war,” and its fighters were guerrilleros. English borrowed the name of the war for the people who waged it.`},"unit:helicopter":{desc:`An air taxi for infantry: it lifts foot soldiers deep behind the front and over mountains. It can't fight, so keep it away from flak and fighters.`,history:`Igor Sikorsky's VS-300, the first practical single-rotor helicopter, flew in 1939, and his R-4 of 1942 became the first in mass production. In Korea helicopters evacuated the wounded, and in Vietnam the UH-1 Huey made moving troops by air the basis of air cavalry tactics.`,fact:`The word helicopter joins the Greek helix, “spiral,” and pteron, “wing.” The everyday split into “heli” and “copter” is linguistically wrong, and nobody minds.`},"unit:infantry":{desc:`The backbone of industrial-age defense: it holds cities and front lines far better than the [[unit:rifleman|rifleman]]. Requires [[resource:rubber|rubber]].`,history:`The machine guns and quick-firing artillery of World War I drove infantry into trenches, where defense outmatched any attack. By World War II foot soldiers carried semi-automatic rifles, submachine guns and mortars, and trucks made armies more mobile. Millions of conscripts bore the brunt of both wars.`,fact:`The Christmas truce of 1914, when British and German soldiers met in no man’s land, is well documented in letters and diaries. The famous football matches are less certain, though some accounts mention a kickabout.`},"unit:ironclad":{desc:`The first armored warship: it makes short work of wooden fleets and bombards coastlines. Slow, and happiest near the shore. Later becomes the [[unit:destroyer|destroyer]].`,history:`France's Gloire of 1859 and Britain's Warrior of 1860 opened the age of the ironclad. At Hampton Roads in 1862 the Monitor and the Virginia pounded each other for hours to little effect. The duel ended in a draw, but every wooden navy in the world became obsolete that day.`,fact:`The Monitor’s turret was so novel that sailors nicknamed her a “cheesebox on a raft.” She had less than two feet of hull above water, which helped in battle and did not help in a storm.`},"unit:marine":{desc:`Shock infantry for landings: it assaults a coast straight from a ship, no beachhead needed. Strong on the attack, ordinary on defense.`,history:`Naval infantry dates back to the 17th century: Britain's Royal Marines trace their origins to 1664, the U.S. Marine Corps to 1775. In the 1930s marines became amphibious assault specialists, and in World War II they stormed Pacific islands from Guadalcanal to Tarawa and Iwo Jima in 1945.`,fact:`The US Marine Corps celebrates its birthday on November 10, 1775; legend places its first recruiting at Tun Tavern in Philadelphia.`},"unit:panzer":{desc:`Germany's replacement for the [[unit:tank|tank]]: just as powerful but faster, so it breaks through deeper and gets more done each turn. The spearhead of a lightning offensive.`,history:`German tanks of the 1930s, the Panzer III and IV, were not better armed or armored than the best enemy designs. Their edge was organization: tanks massed in divisions, fitted with radios and working closely with aircraft and motorized infantry. That combination broke through French defenses in six weeks in 1940.`,fact:`German tank crews of 1940 were a small elite. Most of the German army still marched on foot, and its supplies moved by horse.`},"unit:paratrooper":{desc:`Troops from the sky: they drop from your city straight behind enemy lines, seize key ground and hold it decently until the main force arrives.`,history:`Mass parachute drops were first rehearsed in Soviet exercises of the 1930s. In 1941 Germany took Crete by airborne assault, but losses were so heavy it never tried another on that scale. Allied paratroopers landed in Normandy the night before D-Day and at Arnhem in 1944.`,fact:`Allied paratroopers in Normandy carried small tin “crickets” to find each other in the dark: one click was answered by two.`},"unit:rifleman":{desc:`The mass defender of the age of national armies: sturdier than the [[unit:musketman|musketman]] and requires no resources at all. Well suited to conscription.`,history:`Rifled guns were more accurate than smoothbores but slow to load, until Claude-Étienne Minié designed his expanding bullet in 1849. The rifle became every soldier's weapon, and universal conscription filled the vast armies of the 19th century. The American Civil War showed how brutally rifles punished close-order formations.`,fact:`The British 95th Rifles wore dark green instead of red so they could hide in cover — one of the first steps toward camouflage.`},"unit:submarine":{desc:`An invisible hunter: it stalks ships and strikes without warning, staying hidden. Vulnerable to destroyers and in an open fight.`,history:`The American Turtle made the first submarine attack in 1776, and the Confederate H.L. Hunley became the first to sink a warship in 1864. In both world wars German U-boats came close to cutting Britain's sea lanes, and the Allies won the Battle of the Atlantic at enormous cost.`,fact:`The first submarine attack, by the one-man Turtle in 1776, failed when its pilot could not fix the explosive charge to the hull of a British ship.`},"unit:tank":{desc:`An armored fist for breakthroughs: it can attack repeatedly in one turn and pins down nearby enemies. The main striking force of its age.`,history:`British Mark I tanks first went into action on the Somme in September 1916. The name was a cover story; factory workers were told they were building water tanks for the desert. The secret lasted until roughly the first battle, the name somewhat longer. By 1918 tanks were attacking by the hundreds.`,fact:`Inside early tanks, flying metal flakes and engine fumes were so bad that crews wore leather helmets and chain-mail face masks.`},"unit:transport":{desc:`A troopship for carrying armies across the ocean. Nearly defenseless, so it relies on escorts and, failing that, optimism. Succeeds the [[unit:galleon|galleon]].`,history:`In both world wars troops and supplies crossed the ocean aboard transports sailing in guarded convoys. American yards built about 2,700 standardized Liberty ships in World War II, assembling each in a matter of weeks. The Normandy landings depended on thousands of vessels, including tank landing ships that ran straight onto the beach.`,fact:`The standard shipping container, introduced in the 1950s, changed trade more than any ship design: loading that once took days of dock work now takes hours.`},"unit:aegis_cruiser":{desc:`The pinnacle of the surface fleet: it spots submarines, pins down enemy movement and strikes farther than the old [[unit:cruiser|cruiser]]. A reliable shield for any task force.`,history:`The Aegis system pairs a powerful phased-array radar with computerized fire control able to track and engage many aircraft at once. The first ship to carry it, the cruiser USS Ticonderoga, entered service in 1983. The name comes from the aegis, the shield of Zeus in Greek myth.`,fact:`A phased-array radar steers its beam without turning: thousands of small antennas shift the timing of their signals so the beam points where it is needed.`},"unit:cruise_missile":{desc:`A single-use precision strike: it destroys its target and itself in the process. Great against dug-in defenders and ships when you'd rather not risk aircraft.`,history:`The ancestor of the cruise missile was the German V-1 flying bomb launched at London in 1944. Modern missiles such as the Tomahawk fly low, hugging terrain using stored maps. Tomahawks saw their first large-scale combat use in the Gulf War of 1991.`,fact:`The V-1’s pulsejet made a rasping drone, and Londoners called it the “buzz bomb.” When the noise stopped, they knew it was about to fall.`},"unit:f_15":{desc:`America's replacement for the [[unit:jet_fighter|Jet Fighter]]: longer reach, more strikes, and it attacks unseen. Equally at home in dogfights and against ground targets.`,history:`The F-15 Eagle first flew in 1972, designed for a single purpose: air superiority. Its operators credit it with more than a hundred air-to-air victories without a single loss in air combat. The later F-15E Strike Eagle added long-range ground attack to its résumé.`,fact:`In 1985 an F-15 fired an anti-satellite missile that destroyed a satellite in orbit — the only time a fighter jet has shot down a spacecraft.`},"unit:icbm":{desc:`An intercontinental nuclear missile: it erases armies and cities anywhere on the map. Requires the [[wonder:the_manhattan_project|Manhattan Project]] and changes the world for good.`,history:`The first ICBM, the Soviet R-7, flew in 1957 and that same year launched the first artificial satellite into orbit. American Minuteman missiles and silo-based arsenals on both sides followed. Mutually assured destruction rested on a simple idea: whoever fires first dies second. No ICBM has ever been used in war.`,fact:`The R-7, the first ICBM, made a poor weapon but a superb launcher: its descendants still carry crews to orbit as the Soyuz rocket.`},"unit:jet_fighter":{desc:`The jet-powered heir to the [[unit:fighter|fighter]]: faster, stronger and longer-ranged. Rules the skies until stealth aircraft show up.`,history:`The first operational jet fighter was Germany's Me 262 in 1944, too late to change the war. Over Korea from 1950, MiG-15s and F-86 Sabres fought the first large-scale jet-versus-jet battles. Speeds climbed so high that missiles soon began to replace guns.`,fact:`The MiG-15 owed its engine to Britain: in 1946 Britain sold the Soviet Union Rolls-Royce Nene engines, which were promptly copied.`},"unit:mech_infantry":{desc:`The finest defender of the modern age: it holds a city against almost anything and moves faster than regular [[unit:infantry|infantry]].`,history:`Infantry fighting vehicles let soldiers fight without leaving their armor. The Soviet BMP-1, unveiled in the 1960s, was the first true IFV; the American M2 Bradley entered service in 1981. Mechanized infantry can keep pace with tanks and protect them from enemy anti-tank teams.`,fact:`Before purpose-built fighting vehicles, Second World War infantry often rode into battle clinging to the outside of tanks — fast, but very exposed.`},"unit:mobile_sam":{desc:`A mobile surface-to-air missile system: it travels with the army and downs aircraft striking its tile. Successor to [[unit:flak|flak]]; nearly useless on the ground.`,history:`Surface-to-air missiles announced themselves in 1960, when a Soviet S-75 shot down an American U-2 spy plane over the Urals. Mobile systems such as the 2K12 Kub inflicted heavy losses on Israeli aircraft in the 1973 war. Today such systems escort armies on the move.`,fact:`Mobile air-defense crews learned to “shoot and scoot”: fire, then move at once, before the enemy could find the launcher by its radar emissions.`},"unit:modern_armor":{desc:`The strongest ground strike force: it breaks any defense, can attack repeatedly in one turn and succeeds the [[unit:tank|tank]]. Requires oil, rubber and aluminum.`,history:`The latest generation of tanks, the American M1 Abrams, the German Leopard 2 and the Soviet T-72, appeared in the late 1970s. Composite armor, thermal sights and stabilized guns let them fire accurately on the move, at night and in bad weather. In the 1991 Gulf War such tanks hit targets at well over a mile.`,fact:`Modern tanks carry so much electronics that many have a small auxiliary engine just to power their systems while the main engine is off.`},"unit:modern_paratrooper":{desc:`An improved [[unit:paratrooper|paratrooper]]: it jumps farther and holds harder. Seizes key ground deep in enemy territory and keeps it until help arrives.`,history:`After World War II helicopters took over much of air assault, but parachute units endured. Steerable canopies and high-altitude jumps made landings more precise and harder to detect. Large combat drops still took place, for example in Panama in 1989 and northern Iraq in 2003.`,fact:`In a HALO jump — high altitude, low opening — paratroopers leave the aircraft very high and open their canopies only near the ground, which can help them slip past some short-range defenses unseen.`},"unit:nuclear_submarine":{desc:`A hidden predator with almost unlimited endurance: it sinks ships from ambush and can carry a [[unit:cruise_missile|cruise missile]] to distant shores.`,history:`Nuclear power freed submarines from the need to surface: USS Nautilus entered service in 1954 and passed under the North Pole in 1958. Ballistic missile submarines followed in 1959. Nuclear boats can stay submerged for months, limited mainly by how much food they can carry for the crew.`,fact:`Nuclear submarines make their own oxygen by splitting water with electricity, and their own fresh water by distilling the sea.`},"unit:radar_artillery":{desc:`Heir to [[unit:artillery|artillery]]: more strikes, harder hits, faster movement, and it pins down nearby foes. Burns away defenses before an assault but still needs guarding.`,history:`Modern self-propelled guns and rocket systems, such as the American MLRS of 1983, rely on computers and radar data. Counter-battery radar can plot an enemy gun's position from a shell's trajectory before the shell even lands. Some howitzers can fire several rounds on different arcs so that all of them hit at the same moment.`,fact:`Some modern shells have small fins and satellite receivers that steer them in flight, turning an old-fashioned gun into a precision weapon.`},"unit:stealth_bomber":{desc:`An invisible strategic bomber: it delivers crushing strikes from vast distances and slips past interceptors. Very expensive, but nearly untouchable.`,history:`The B-2 Spirit is a tailless flying wing nearly invisible to radar. It first flew in 1989; only twenty-one were built, at roughly two billion dollars apiece. B-2s flew missions to Kosovo from Missouri and back without landing.`,fact:`On very long missions B-2 crews take turns napping on a cot behind the seats; some flights have lasted well over a day.`},"unit:stealth_fighter":{desc:`A stealth fighter: it strikes from afar unseen, slips through air defenses and finishes off weakened targets. The best way to attack before the enemy realizes anything.`,history:`The first operational stealth aircraft, the F-117 Nighthawk, flew in 1981, and its existence was not officially acknowledged until 1988. Despite the F in its name, it was a strike aircraft that carried no air-to-air weapons at all. The true stealth fighter, the F-22, entered service in 2005.`,fact:`F-117 pilots called themselves “Bandits” and received numbers in the order they qualified — a club so small that the number became part of each pilot’s identity.`},"unit:tactical_nuke":{desc:`A short-range nuclear strike: it destroys an army or a city within reach. Requires the [[wonder:the_manhattan_project|Manhattan Project]] and a willingness to live with the consequences.`,history:`In the 1950s and 1960s the superpowers built thousands of tactical nuclear weapons, from artillery shells to short-range rockets. The American Davy Crockett recoilless gun could lob a nuclear round just a few miles. After 1991 most such weapons were withdrawn from service.`,fact:`Both superpowers built nuclear demolition charges small enough for a two-man team to carry in backpacks, meant to blow up bridges, dams and mountain passes in the path of an invasion.`},"unit:tow_infantry":{desc:`Anti-tank infantry: a sturdy defender that meets attackers with fire before they arrive. Succeeds the [[unit:guerilla|guerilla]] and can be drafted.`,history:`TOW stands for Tube-launched, Optically tracked, Wire-guided; the American missile entered service in 1970. In the 1973 war Soviet-made Malyutka missiles proved that infantry with guided weapons could stop an armored assault. Later systems such as the Javelin went fire-and-forget.`,fact:`The bazooka took its name from a homemade trombone-like instrument played by a radio comedian of the 1930s.`},"building:palace":{desc:`Marks your capital and slowly gathers culture. It comes free with your first city and cannot be built, which spares everyone the question of who paid for it.`,fact:`Versailles began as Louis XIII’s hunting lodge. His son turned it into the seat of government, and at its height thousands of courtiers and servants lived there.`},"building:aqueduct":{desc:`Lets a city grow past its early size cap. Build it when your best cities press against the limit; [[trait:agricultural|Agricultural]] civilizations get it at a discount.`,fact:`Most of a Roman aqueduct ran underground. The famous arches only carried the water across valleys, and the whole line depended on a gentle, steady slope over many miles.`},"building:barracks":{desc:`Land units trained here start as veterans, the wounded heal in a single turn, and old ones can be upgraded on the spot. Worth it in border and military cities, not everywhere — the upkeep adds up.`,fact:`“Tattoo,” the evening drum call that sent soldiers back to barracks, comes from the Dutch doe den tap toe — “turn off the tap” — the order to stop serving beer.`},"building:walls":{desc:`Cheap, sturdy protection for a young town: defenders hold much better and land artillery hits less hard. They only work while the city is still a town.`,fact:`Letters found at Vindolanda, a fort beside Hadrian’s Wall, include a birthday party invitation from one officer’s wife to another — one of the oldest surviving texts written by a woman in Latin.`},"building:colosseum":{desc:`Keeps several unhappy citizens content and adds culture. Pricey to maintain, so build it where a temple alone no longer keeps the peace.`,fact:`The Romans called it the Flavian Amphitheatre. The name Colosseum probably came later, from a colossal statue of Nero that stood nearby.`},"building:courthouse":{desc:`Sharply cuts corruption and waste — the first remedy for distant cities. It also helps the city resist propaganda and the pull of a culturally stronger neighbor.`,fact:`Lawyers are “called to the bar” because of a real railing that separated the court from the public; to be admitted was to be allowed to pass it.`},"building:granary":{desc:`Keeps part of the food store when a city grows, so it grows again sooner. Best in food-rich cities that keep turning out settlers and workers.`,fact:`Cats probably domesticated themselves around early granaries: grain drew mice, mice drew wildcats, and farmers did not object.`},"building:harbor":{desc:`Turns sea tiles into real farmland, so coastal cities grow. Ships built here are veterans and get repaired in a turn, and harbors connect your cities across the water.`,fact:`Tyre was an island with two harbors until Alexander the Great built a causeway to besiege it. Silt has since gathered along it, and the island is now a peninsula.`},"building:academy":{desc:`Founded by a [[unit:scientific_leader|Scientific Leader]], who stays on as its faculty: more research and culture in one city, no upkeep. Cannot be built or sold. Put it where trade is highest.`,fact:`Plato taught in a grove outside Athens sacred to the hero Akademos. The school took the grove’s name, and every academy since has borrowed it.`},"building:library":{desc:`The core science building of the ancient world: speeds research and builds culture. Put it first where trade is high; it leads to the [[building:university|University]].`,fact:`Medieval libraries chained their books to the shelves. A few chained libraries survive, as at Hereford Cathedral in England.`},"building:marketplace":{desc:`Raises tax income and makes luxuries go further, more so the more kinds you have. The backbone of a money city and the step toward a [[building:bank|Bank]].`,fact:`The Grand Bazaar of Istanbul, one of the oldest covered markets in the world, holds thousands of shops along dozens of streets.`},"building:temple":{desc:`The cheapest way to calm an unhappy citizen and give a new city its first culture. Nearly every city wants one sooner or later.`,fact:`Greek temples that we picture in white marble were brightly painted in reds, blues and gold; ultraviolet light still reveals traces of the pigment.`},"building:bank":{desc:`Builds on the marketplace to raise taxes further. Pays off in large trade cities; in small ones it is mostly an expensive place to keep a modest amount of gold.`,fact:`Europe’s first banknotes were issued in Sweden in 1661, partly because Swedish copper money came in plates so heavy that a large payment needed a cart.`},"building:cathedral":{desc:`A powerful cure for unrest: settles many citizens at once and adds solid culture. Needs a temple and costs real upkeep, so it belongs in big cities.`,fact:`Strictly speaking, a gargoyle is a rain spout — the word shares a root with “gargle.” A carved figure that carries no water is called a grotesque.`},"building:coastal_fortress":{desc:`Guards a coastal city against ships and landings, and fires on enemy vessels sailing past. Meant for ports on contested sea lanes.`,fact:`Britain’s Martello towers copy a tower at Mortella Point in Corsica that held off British warships in 1794 — so impressively that Britain built over a hundred of its own.`},"building:university":{desc:`Pushes research further in a city that already has a library, and adds plenty of culture. Expensive, so save it for your best research cities.`,fact:`Oxford is older than the Aztec Empire: teaching there was underway by 1096, while Tenochtitlan was founded around 1325.`},"building:airport":{desc:`Aircraft built here are veterans and get repaired in a turn, and airports link cities into the trade network through the air. Adds a little pollution.`,fact:`Chicago O’Hare’s code is ORD because the airport began as Orchard Field.`},"building:civil_defense":{desc:`Bolsters a city's defenses in the industrial age, when old walls no longer help. Worth it in frontier cities facing invasion.`,fact:`Britain’s “Keep Calm and Carry On” poster was printed in 1939 but almost never displayed. It became famous only after a copy turned up in a secondhand bookshop in 2000.`},"building:coal_plant":{desc:`The earliest power plant: a solid production boost for a factory city, paid for in heavy smog. A stopgap until cleaner power arrives.`,fact:`The Great Smog of London in 1952 killed thousands of people in a few days and led to the Clean Air Act of 1956.`},"building:commercial_dock":{desc:`Adds trade to worked water tiles that already produce some. Good for coastal cities that live off the sea, at the cost of a little pollution.`,fact:`Before containers, a cargo ship could spend almost as long in port, being loaded and unloaded by hand, as it spent at sea.`},"building:factory":{desc:`The heart of an industrial city: a big production boost and the gateway to power plants. Build it where shields are already high, and expect pollution.`,fact:`Richard Arkwright’s Cromford Mill of 1771 is often called the first modern factory: it ran day and night in shifts, and he built a village to house its workers.`},"building:hospital":{desc:`Removes the last cap on growth; without it a large city simply stops getting bigger. Your capital and major cities need one by the industrial age.`,fact:`Florence Nightingale was a pioneering statistician: her “rose diagrams” showed that far more soldiers died of disease in hospitals than of their wounds.`},"building:hydro_plant":{desc:`A power plant for cities by a river or mountains: the same production boost as coal, without the smoke. If the site allows it, the best choice of its era.`,fact:`Norway gets about nine-tenths of its electricity from hydropower.`},"building:police_station":{desc:`Cuts corruption and eases war weariness. Most useful to a [[gov:democracy|Democracy]] fighting a war it promised would be over by harvest.`,fact:`Scotland Yard is named not after Scotland but after a street, Great Scotland Yard, onto which the first headquarters of the Metropolitan Police had a back entrance.`},"building:stock_exchange":{desc:`The top of the money ladder after market and bank: more tax from a rich city. Also required for [[wonder:wall_street|Wall Street]].`,fact:`When the South Sea Bubble burst in 1720, Isaac Newton lost a fortune. He supposedly said he could calculate the motions of heavenly bodies, but not the madness of people.`},"building:manufacturing_plant":{desc:`Another large production boost for industrial giants. Costly and dirty, worth it only in cities already forging armies or wonders.`,fact:`Toyota’s production system gave English the word kanban, a card that signals when parts should be restocked, along with the habit of stopping the whole line when any worker spots a defect.`},"building:mass_transit_system":{desc:`Eliminates pollution caused by population. A must once your big cities start fouling the air simply by living in them.`,fact:`The Moscow Metro was designed as “palaces for the people,” and many of its stations are decorated with chandeliers, mosaics and marble.`},"building:nuclear_plant":{desc:`The strongest power plant, for coastal cities only: a bigger production boost than any other. The choice for an industrial port that needs every shield.`,fact:`Cooling towers have become the symbol of nuclear power, yet many nuclear plants have none, and plenty of coal plants do.`},"building:offshore_platform":{desc:`Draws shields from worked water tiles — a lifeline for coastal cities with poor land. Expensive and dirty, but it puts the sea to work.`,fact:`Retired oil platforms sometimes become artificial reefs: in the Gulf of Mexico, “rigs to reefs” programs leave the steel legs in place for fish and corals.`},"building:recycling_center":{desc:`Sharply cuts pollution from buildings. Needed in industrial cities whose factories and power plants are already poisoning the countryside.`,fact:`Sweden recycles or burns for energy so much of its waste that it has imported rubbish from neighboring countries.`},"building:research_lab":{desc:`The final step of the science chain after the university: more research and some culture. For your top research cities; it adds a little pollution.`,fact:`The Post-it Note grew out of a failed experiment: a 3M chemist made a weak, reusable glue, and years passed before a colleague used it to keep bookmarks from falling out of his hymnal.`},"building:sam_missile_battery":{desc:`Fires on aircraft striking the city. Needed by cities within reach of enemy bombers, and for [[wonder:strategic_missile_defense|Strategic Missile Defense]].`,fact:`The Patriot missile’s name is officially an acronym: Phased Array Tracking Radar to Intercept On Target.`},"building:solar_plant":{desc:`Clean power: a production boost with no pollution. Expensive, but the best long-term choice for a modern industrial city.`,fact:`The first practical silicon solar cell was shown in 1954, and one of its first jobs was powering satellites: Vanguard 1, launched in 1958, is still in orbit.`},"wonder:battlefield_medicine":{desc:`Your troops heal even on foreign soil, where wounds normally won't mend. Built for long campaigns far from home; needs a [[building:hospital|Hospital]] in the city.`,history:`Field care for the wounded grew over centuries, but the turn came in the 19th: Larrey's flying ambulances under Napoleon, Pirogov's triage and anaesthesia in Crimea, Nightingale's hospital reforms, and the Geneva Convention of 1864 that made medics neutral. In the 20th century blood transfusion, antibiotics and helicopter evacuation saved more soldiers than any armour did.`,fact:`The word “ambulance” comes from the French hôpital ambulant, a “walking hospital” that followed an army on the march.`},"wonder:forbidden_palace":{desc:`A backup capital for a sprawling realm: distant provinces measure corruption from it instead of the main [[building:palace|Palace]]. Build it at the heart of your empire's second core.`,history:`Beijing's Forbidden City was built between 1406 and 1420 on the orders of the Ming emperor Yongle. For almost five centuries it housed the Ming and Qing emperors, sealed off from ordinary subjects. Since 1925 it has been the Palace Museum, one of the most visited museums in the world.`,fact:`Puyi, the last emperor, went on living in the Forbidden City for years after his abdication, until he was expelled in 1924. Late in life he worked at a botanical garden in Beijing.`},"wonder:heroic_epic":{desc:`A reward for your first [[unit:army|army]] victory: the city becomes a seat of martial memory, and great leaders emerge more often. Made for rulers who fight often and gladly.`,history:`The heroic epic is among the oldest forms of literature: Gilgamesh, the Iliad, the Mahabharata, the Song of Roland. Long before they were written down, singers passed these tales on for generations, turning the deeds of chieftains into a people's shared memory.`,fact:`The Kyrgyz epic of Manas is often counted among the longest in the world, with some recorded versions running to hundreds of thousands of lines. Singers called manaschi still perform it from memory.`},"wonder:iron_works":{desc:`Turns a city with both [[resource:iron|iron]] and [[resource:coal|coal]] into the industrial heart of the nation: far more shields, at the price of smoke over the countryside.`,history:`In 1709 Abraham Darby at Coalbrookdale began smelting iron with coke instead of charcoal. Ironmaking was freed from the forests, and huge works grew up beside coal and ore fields, laying the foundation of the Industrial Revolution.`,fact:`John Wilkinson, the ironmaster known as “Iron-Mad,” loved his material so much that he built an iron boat, was buried in an iron coffin and had an iron obelisk raised over his grave.`},"wonder:the_colossus":{desc:`A seaside wonder for a trading capital: every tile that already yields trade yields more. Best in a large coastal city working many ocean tiles.`,history:`The bronze statue of the sun god Helios at Rhodes was the work of Chares of Lindos, completed around 280 BC to celebrate the defeat of Demetrius Poliorcetes' siege. It stood for about half a century before an earthquake brought it down in 226 BC. Despite the legend, it almost certainly did not straddle the harbor.`,fact:`Emma Lazarus’s sonnet on the pedestal of the Statue of Liberty, “The New Colossus,” sets the two statues side by side — though Bartholdi’s design grew out of his earlier plan for a lighthouse statue in Egypt.`},"wonder:the_great_library":{desc:`Insurance for the trailing: any technology already known to several rivals simply arrives. Most valuable to rulers who build while others invent.`,history:`The Library of Alexandria was founded under the first Ptolemies in the early 3rd century BC. Tradition holds that scrolls found on ships in the harbor were taken to be copied. Eratosthenes and Aristarchus of Samothrace worked there; the library declined over centuries rather than burning in a single day.`,fact:`Eratosthenes, the library’s chief librarian, measured the Earth by comparing the angle of the noon Sun at Alexandria and at Syene — and came remarkably close to the true size.`},"wonder:the_great_lighthouse":{desc:`A gift to early sailors: ships move farther and never founder at sea, so even frail [[unit:galley|galleys]] can venture off the coast. For those whose road to the neighbors runs over water.`,history:`The Pharos of Alexandria was raised around 280 BC under the Ptolemies, its architect traditionally named as Sostratus of Cnidus. Estimates put it at well over a hundred meters, and the island's name became the word for lighthouse in several languages. Earthquakes had ruined it by the 14th century.`,fact:`The traveler Ibn Battuta visited the Pharos twice in the fourteenth century. The first time he could still go inside; on his return, years later, it lay in such ruin that he could not even reach the door.`},"wonder:the_great_wall":{desc:`Every city of yours counts as walled, and your troops fight far harder against barbarians. A quiet home front for early growth or war.`,history:`Chinese states of the Warring States period were already building border walls; after 221 BC Qin Shi Huang joined them into a single line. Most of the brick wall that survives today dates from the Ming dynasty. Contrary to popular belief, it cannot be seen from orbit with the naked eye.`,fact:`The Great Wall was never one wall: surveys count many separate walls, trenches and ramparts built by different dynasties, adding up to more than thirteen thousand miles.`},"wonder:the_hanging_gardens":{desc:`Contentment across the empire: every city gains a happier citizen, and the host city gains a crowd of them. Lets you grow wide without a temple on every corner.`,history:`Classical writers credited the Hanging Gardens to Nebuchadnezzar II, supposedly built for a queen who missed her native hills. Yet no Babylonian text mentions them and excavation has never found them. Some historians think the story really describes Sennacherib's gardens at Nineveh.`,fact:`A terraced garden in a dry land needs water lifted high. One theory holds that Sennacherib’s engineers used an early water screw — centuries before Archimedes, whose name the device now bears.`},"wonder:the_mausoleum_of_mausollos":{desc:`A cheap cure for unrest in one city, with a little culture on top. Good for a capital that grows faster than it can raise temples.`,history:`The tomb of Mausolus, a Persian satrap of Caria, was completed at Halicarnassus by his widow Artemisia II after his death in 353 BC, with the finest Greek sculptors carving its friezes. Earthquakes toppled it, and the Knights Hospitaller reused its stones for their castle at Bodrum. His name gave us the word mausoleum.`,fact:`Artemisia, it is said, grieved so deeply that she mixed her husband’s ashes into her drink. She also held a contest of orators to praise him — and then built the most famous tomb in the world.`},"wonder:the_oracle":{desc:`Every [[building:temple|temple]] in the empire calms more citizens. An early pick for pious realms and anyone who wants to grow without riots.`,history:`Apollo's oracle at Delphi was active from the 8th century BC: the priestess, the Pythia, gave answers that priests set into verse. Kings and city-states consulted her before wars and before founding colonies. Her replies were famously ambiguous, and the sanctuary was closed in the late 4th century AD.`,fact:`Herodotus tells how King Croesus asked the oracle whether to attack Persia and was told that he would destroy a great empire. He attacked, and destroyed his own.`},"wonder:the_pentagon":{desc:`For a power that already fields several armies: each [[unit:army|army]] holds an extra unit. Useless in a peaceful game.`,history:`The Pentagon, headquarters of the US Department of Defense in Arlington, went up in a record sixteen months between 1941 and 1943. Its five-sided shape came from the original site, and the layout lets a person walk between any two points in about seven minutes. It remains one of the largest office buildings in the world.`,fact:`The Pentagon was designed to save steel, which the navy needed for warships: it is built mostly of reinforced concrete, and its floors were originally linked by ramps rather than elevators.`},"wonder:the_pyramids":{desc:`Every city gains the effect of a [[building:granary|granary]] and grows faster. A fine pick for a wide empire on fertile land.`,history:`The Great Pyramid of Khufu at Giza rose around 2560 BC and remained the tallest structure on Earth for nearly four thousand years. It was built not by slaves but by organized crews of paid laborers, whose village archaeologists have excavated. It is the only one of the Seven Wonders still standing.`,fact:`The pyramids were once faced with polished white limestone that gleamed in the sun. Most of that casing was stripped after a fourteenth-century earthquake and reused in the buildings of Cairo.`},"wonder:the_statue_of_zeus":{desc:`A cheap wonder that regularly hands you free [[unit:ancient_cavalry|ancient cavalry]]. A steady trickle of troops for early wars; needs ivory.`,history:`Phidias made the statue of Zeus at Olympia around 435 BC: a seated god some twelve meters tall, of ivory and gold over a wooden frame. Ancient visitors held it a misfortune to die without seeing it. It was lost in the 5th century AD, probably in a fire at Constantinople.`,fact:`According to Pausanias, the statue’s ivory was kept from cracking with olive oil, and a shallow pool of oil lay before it on the temple floor.`},"wonder:the_temple_of_artemis":{desc:`Every city in the empire counts as having a [[building:temple|temple]]: order and culture everywhere at once. Expensive, but it saves young cities from unrest.`,history:`The Temple of Artemis at Ephesus was rebuilt several times; one version was funded by King Croesus of Lydia. In 356 BC a man named Herostratus burned it down to make himself famous, and the plan unfortunately worked. The rebuilt temple was wrecked by the Goths in the 3rd century AD.`,fact:`The Ephesians tried to punish Herostratus by forbidding anyone ever to speak his name. The historian Theopompus wrote it down anyway, and so the plan failed twice.`},"wonder:copernicus_observatory":{desc:`Turns one city into a scientific capital, greatly boosting its research. Build it where trade is already high and a [[building:library|library]] stands.`,history:`Nicolaus Copernicus, a canon at Frombork, spent decades watching the sky and concluded that the Earth moves around the Sun. His On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres appeared in 1543, the year of his death. The heliocentric model opened the Scientific Revolution.`,fact:`Copernicus’s book carried a preface he never saw, added by the theologian Andreas Osiander, which presented the moving Earth as a mere calculating device rather than a claim about the real world.`},"wonder:js_bachs_cathedral":{desc:`Comfort for the whole nation: every city gains more content citizens. Best in a broad empire where [[building:cathedral|cathedrals]] would cost a fortune.`,history:`From 1723 until his death in 1750, Johann Sebastian Bach served as cantor of St. Thomas Church in Leipzig, writing music for services almost every week. He was largely forgotten afterward, until Mendelssohn's 1829 performance of the St Matthew Passion brought him back for good.`,fact:`Bach had twenty children, about half of whom survived infancy, and several became noted composers. In late eighteenth-century London, “Bach” usually meant his son Johann Christian.`},"wonder:knights_templar":{desc:`An order that regularly turns out [[unit:crusader|Crusaders]] in its city: a steady flow of heavy infantry for medieval wars.`,history:`The Knights Templar were founded around 1119 in Jerusalem to protect pilgrims. These warrior monks built a network of houses across Europe and became, in effect, the first international bankers. In 1307 King Philip IV of France had the members arrested, and in 1312 the pope dissolved the order.`,fact:`The Templars offered something like an early traveler’s check: a pilgrim could deposit money at a Templar house in Europe and draw it in the Holy Land with a letter of credit.`},"wonder:leonardos_workshop":{desc:`Upgrading units costs far less across the empire. A treasure for an army too valuable to disband and too costly to re-equip for a new age.`,history:`Leonardo da Vinci trained in Verrocchio's workshop and later ran his own. His notebooks are full of flying machines, armored carts, bridges and mechanisms, most never built in his lifetime. He died in France in 1519, leaving thousands of pages of drawings.`,fact:`In his famous letter offering his services to the Duke of Milan, Leonardo spent nearly all of it on bridges, cannons and siege engines, and mentioned only near the end that he could also paint.`},"wonder:magellans_voyage":{desc:`All your ships move farther. Valuable to sea powers, overseas colonies and any fleet that has to be everywhere at once.`,history:`In 1519 Ferdinand Magellan sailed from Spain with five ships. He was killed in the Philippines in 1521, but the Victoria, under Juan Sebastian Elcano, limped home in 1522 with a handful of survivors. It was the first voyage around the world.`,fact:`Antonio Pigafetta, the voyage’s chronicler, also recorded some of the first European word lists of the languages he met along the way, including Malay.`},"wonder:military_academy":{desc:`A reward for a victorious [[unit:army|army]]: the academy city can raise armies of its own. For conquerors, not peaceful builders.`,history:`Permanent schools for officers spread across Europe in the 18th century, as war became a matter for engineers and gunners. In 1802 both West Point in the United States and Saint-Cyr in France opened. They turned soldiering into a profession with entrance exams and curricula.`,fact:`Edgar Allan Poe was a cadet at West Point and got himself expelled in 1831, reportedly by deliberately skipping classes, drills and church.`},"wonder:newtons_university":{desc:`Sharply boosts science in one city. Place it alongside a [[building:university|university]] so the gains stack.`,history:`Isaac Newton studied and taught at Trinity College, Cambridge, holding the Lucasian chair from 1669. In 1687 he published the Principia, setting out the laws of motion and universal gravitation. For the first time heaven and Earth obeyed the same equations.`,fact:`The Lucasian chair that Newton held at Cambridge later passed to Charles Babbage, Paul Dirac and Stephen Hawking, among others.`},"wonder:shakespeares_theater":{desc:`Hardly anyone in this city is ever unhappy, and it can grow past the usual limit. Put it in the largest, noisiest city you have.`,history:`Shakespeare's company built the Globe Theatre in 1599 on the south bank of the Thames. In 1613 a cannon fired during Henry VIII set the thatched roof alight and the theater burned down; it was quickly rebuilt. A reconstruction of the Globe opened nearby in 1997.`,fact:`When the reconstructed Globe opened in 1997, it had the first thatched roof allowed in London since the Great Fire of 1666.`},"wonder:sistine_chapel":{desc:`Every [[building:cathedral|cathedral]] in the empire calms more citizens. For large realms that already lean on cathedrals.`,history:`The chapel in the Vatican was built in 1473–1481 under Pope Sixtus IV. In 1508–1512 Michelangelo painted its ceiling, working mostly upright on scaffolding, and in 1536–1541 he added the Last Judgment. The conclave that elects popes still meets there.`,fact:`Some figures in the Last Judgment were later given painted drapery to cover their nudity. The painter who did it, Daniele da Volterra, has been nicknamed “the breeches maker” ever since.`},"wonder:smiths_trading_company":{desc:`Pays the upkeep of harbors, marketplaces, banks and exchanges. The richer your empire's trade network, the more gold stays in the treasury.`,history:`Adam Smith published The Wealth of Nations in 1776, explaining the gains from the division of labor and free trade. By then the East India companies had dominated sea trade for a century and a half. Smith, for the record, thought chartered monopoly companies were a terrible idea. This one is named after him anyway.`,fact:`From 2007 Adam Smith appeared on the Bank of England’s twenty-pound note, together with a pin factory illustrating the division of labor.`},"wonder:sun_tzus_art_of_war":{desc:`Every city counts as having [[building:barracks|barracks]]: new troops leave the gates already seasoned. For a realm fighting long wars.`,history:`The Art of War is attributed to Sun Tzu, a general of the state of Wu who lived around the 5th century BC. Its short chapters say more about intelligence, deception and supply than about courage. He held that the supreme skill is to win without fighting; the book is still read today, and not only by soldiers.`,fact:`In Chinese, The Art of War runs to only about six thousand characters — shorter than many newspaper articles.`},"wonder:hoover_dam":{desc:`Every city gains the effect of a [[building:hydro_plant|Hydro Plant]]: cheap, clean power for factories nationwide. Needs a river or mountains nearby.`,history:`Built on the Colorado River on the Nevada–Arizona border between 1931 and 1936, in the depths of the Great Depression, the dam was finished ahead of schedule. More than two hundred meters tall, it created Lake Mead and brought power and water to the American Southwest.`,fact:`The dam’s builders lived in Boulder City, a town created for them where alcohol and gambling were banned. Las Vegas, down the road, was happy to help with both.`},"wonder:intelligence_agency":{desc:`Your embassies may run spy missions. For rulers who prefer to learn a neighbor's plans in advance rather than from dispatches at the front.`,history:`Permanent state intelligence services took shape in the early 20th century: Britain's Secret Service Bureau, forerunner of MI6, was set up in 1909. After the Second World War, in 1947, the United States created the CIA. The Cold War made intelligence one of the main arenas of rivalry between the powers.`,fact:`A sculpture called Kryptos stands at CIA headquarters, carved with four encrypted messages. Three were cracked in the 1990s; the fourth still resists codebreakers.`},"wonder:secret_police_hq":{desc:`Another seat of power: distant provinces measure corruption from it as from the capital. The building does not exist. Please stop asking about it.`,history:`Political police became a permanent arm of the state in the 19th century; well-known examples include Joseph Fouché's Ministry of Police under Napoleon and the Russian Okhrana, founded in 1881. In the 20th century such agencies grew into vast systems for watching a state's own citizens.`,fact:`Joseph Fouché served as minister of police under the Revolution, Napoleon and the restored king in turn — a career so flexible that his name became a byword for political survival.`},"wonder:theory_of_evolution":{desc:`An instant leap in science: several discoveries at once, no research needed. Good at any time, best in a race for a key technology.`,history:`Charles Darwin developed the idea of natural selection after the voyage of the Beagle in 1831–1836 but was slow to publish. In 1858 Alfred Russel Wallace independently reached the same conclusion, and their papers were presented together. On the Origin of Species followed a year later.`,fact:`The Darwin Awards, a tongue-in-cheek honor dating from the 1990s, “commemorate” people who removed themselves from the gene pool in spectacularly foolish ways. Their most famous tale, a car strapped to a rocket booster, turned out to be an urban legend, and the awards’ own archive files it as one.`},"wonder:universal_suffrage":{desc:`The people bear wars more patiently: war weariness is much lower in every city. For republics and democracies that cannot avoid fighting.`,history:`France adopted universal male suffrage in 1848. In 1893 New Zealand became the first country to give women the vote, and in 1906 Finland also let them stand for office. Over the 20th century, one person, one vote became the norm in most states.`,fact:`The secret ballot is younger than voting: printed official ballots marked in private, known as the “Australian ballot,” were introduced in Victoria and South Australia in 1856 and spread around the world.`},"wonder:wall_street":{desc:`Your treasury earns interest: the more gold you hold, the more it pays, up to a cap. For a wealthy realm that keeps money in reserve.`,history:`The New York street takes its name from the wall that the Dutch settlers of New Amsterdam built across the island in the 17th century. In 1792 brokers signed the Buttonwood Agreement there, the beginning of the New York Stock Exchange. The street's name became shorthand for the whole financial world.`,fact:`New York’s ticker-tape parades got their name from the paper strips of stock-ticker machines, which office workers threw from the windows onto the heroes passing below.`},"wonder:apollo_program":{desc:`Your ticket to the space race: once it stands, your cities can build the parts of the ship to Alpha Centauri. The first step toward a science victory.`,history:`In 1961 President Kennedy set the goal of landing a man on the Moon before the decade was out. In July 1969 Apollo 11 carried Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin to the lunar surface. By 1972 twelve people had walked on the Moon.`,fact:`The flags planted on the Moon were ordinary nylon flags bought off the shelf. Decades of unfiltered sunlight have probably bleached them white.`},"wonder:cure_for_cancer":{desc:`Hope for the whole world, and an extra content citizen in every city of yours. Costly, but peace in a late empire is worth it.`,history:`There is still no single cure for cancer, which is really hundreds of different diseases. Yet since the mid-20th century medicine has come a long way, from the first chemotherapy of the 1940s to the HPV vaccine and immunotherapies that teach the body to fight tumors itself. Many cancers are now curable.`,fact:`Cells taken in 1951 from a patient, Henrietta Lacks, without her knowledge became the first human cells to keep dividing in the lab. The HeLa line has since contributed to countless discoveries, from the polio vaccine to cancer research.`},"wonder:longevity":{desc:`Cities grow faster, adding more people at a time. Strongest in a large empire with food and room to expand.`,history:`In 1900 global life expectancy was a little over thirty years; by the early 21st century it had passed seventy. Clean water, vaccines, antibiotics and falling child mortality did most of the work. Today researchers study the mechanisms of aging itself.`,fact:`Some animals barely age: Greenland sharks can live for centuries, and the tiny jellyfish Turritopsis can turn back into an earlier stage of its life instead of dying.`},"wonder:seti_program":{desc:`Greatly boosts science in one city. Put it in your leading research center, where a [[building:research_lab|Research Lab]] already stands.`,history:`In 1960 astronomer Frank Drake's Project Ozma pointed a radio telescope at two nearby stars to listen for artificial signals. In 1977 a puzzling burst, the Wow! signal, was recorded and never heard again. So far, nobody has called back. We are still holding.`,fact:`In 1974 the Arecibo radio telescope beamed a deliberate message toward the star cluster M13, some 25,000 light-years away. If anyone answers, the reply will arrive in about fifty thousand years.`},"wonder:ss_cockpit":{desc:`Where the crew sits and steers the ship to Alpha Centauri. The ship cannot launch without it, so build it alongside the other parts.`,history:`The first crewed cabin to reach space was the Vostok 1 capsule, in which Yuri Gagarin orbited the Earth on April 12, 1961. Later the cabins of Gemini, Soyuz and Apollo became true workplaces, with crews flying the spacecraft by hand.`,fact:`The Apollo command module gave its three astronauts roughly the living space of a large car, and they lived in it for more than a week.`},"wonder:ss_docking_bay":{desc:`The joint that holds the ship to Alpha Centauri together. One of the cheapest parts, a sensible job for a smaller city.`,history:`The first docking of two spacecraft in orbit was performed by Neil Armstrong and David Scott aboard Gemini 8 in March 1966. A year later the Soviet Kosmos 186 and 188 made the first automatic docking. Without docking there would have been no lunar landings and no space stations.`,fact:`To catch up with a target in orbit, a spacecraft first slows down: dropping to a lower orbit makes it circle faster. Early crews had to unlearn every instinct from driving and flying.`},"wonder:ss_engine":{desc:`The main engine of the ship to Alpha Centauri, the heaviest and most expensive part. Give it to your most productive city.`,history:`The five F-1 engines of the Saturn V's first stage remain among the most powerful single-chamber liquid rocket engines ever flown. An interstellar ship would need something else entirely: engineers have studied nuclear pulse and ion drives, and ion engines already push interplanetary probes.`,fact:`The Saturn V’s first stage burned through all its fuel in under three minutes, and the whole stack lifted off so slowly that it took several seconds just to clear the launch tower.`},"wonder:ss_exterior_casing":{desc:`The hull plating that shields the ship to Alpha Centauri on its long journey. An expensive part, best built in a large industrial city.`,history:`In 1946 astronomer Fred Whipple proposed protecting spacecraft from micrometeoroids with a thin outer bumper that shatters a particle before it reaches the hull. Such Whipple shields guard the modules of the International Space Station today. An interstellar ship would need far tougher armor.`,fact:`The gold-colored foil on many spacecraft is usually not gold at all, but layers of aluminized plastic film that reflect heat and keep the inside from roasting or freezing.`},"wonder:ss_fuel_cells":{desc:`The power source for the systems of the ship to Alpha Centauri. A cheap part, easily handed to a secondary city.`,history:`A fuel cell produces electricity directly from the reaction of hydrogen and oxygen. The principle was discovered in the 1830s, and fuel cells first flew in space aboard Gemini in 1965. On Apollo they supplied both power and drinking water for the crew.`,fact:`William Grove built the first fuel cell in 1839 and called it a “gas voltaic battery.” It took more than a century, and the space race, before anyone found it truly useful.`},"wonder:ss_life_support_system":{desc:`Air, water and warmth for the crew of the ship to Alpha Centauri. The flight cannot begin without it, so build it in time.`,history:`Life support has evolved from the oxygen tanks of Vostok to the recycling loops of orbital stations. On the ISS most of the water is recovered from humidity and waste. An interstellar voyage would need an almost fully closed system, which no one has yet built.`,fact:`In the early 1990s eight people sealed themselves inside Biosphere 2, a glass world in the Arizona desert, to test a closed life-support system. Oxygen fell so low that extra oxygen had to be injected.`},"wonder:ss_planetary_party_lounge":{desc:`The crew's recreation deck on the ship to Alpha Centauri. Engineers classify it as mission-critical and decline to elaborate. A cheap part.`,history:`Space agency psychologists have long known that a crew on a long flight needs more than air: it needs somewhere to unwind. Aboard Mir and the ISS, crews shared meals, watched films and celebrated birthdays, and since 2010 the ISS Cupola has been the favorite spot to relax and photograph the Earth.`,fact:`The first musical instrument played in space was a harmonica smuggled aboard Gemini 6 in 1965. The crew played “Jingle Bells” after radioing a sighting of a mysterious object in a polar orbit, piloted by a man in a red suit.`},"wonder:ss_stasis_chamber":{desc:`Lets the crew sleep through the long journey to Alpha Centauri. Requires advanced robotics, so it comes late in the race.`,history:`Long artificial sleep for space travel began in science fiction, but it is studied in earnest. Doctors already use therapeutic hypothermia to slow metabolism after injury, and in the 2010s NASA funded studies of putting crews into a hibernation-like torpor.`,fact:`Tardigrades, microscopic animals that survive drying out by shutting down almost completely, were exposed to open space in 2007 — and some of them survived.`},"wonder:ss_storage_supply":{desc:`The supply hold for the crew of the ship to Alpha Centauri. A cheap part, worth finishing along the way.`,history:`Resupply of orbital stations began in 1978, when the first Progress cargo ship delivered fuel, water and food to Salyut 6. Cargo flights have been routine ever since. An interstellar crew would have to be stocked for years ahead, with no deliveries along the way.`,fact:`Tortillas replaced bread on the Space Shuttle because they leave no crumbs — and crumbs in weightlessness drift into equipment and eyes.`},"wonder:ss_thrusters":{desc:`Maneuvering thrusters without which the ship to Alpha Centauri cannot steer. Unlocked with [[tech:satellites|Satellites]].`,history:`Small attitude thrusters turn a spacecraft and guide it precisely to its target. Gemini 3 made the first true orbital maneuvers in 1965, and the thruster clusters on Apollo let it dock with the lunar module. No docking is possible without them.`,fact:`Many attitude thrusters burn propellants that need no igniter at all: the two liquids burst into flame the moment they meet, which makes the engine simple and reliable.`},"wonder:strategic_missile_defense":{desc:`Intercepts most nuclear strikes aimed at your cities. Needed once your neighbors start fielding [[unit:icbm|ICBMs]].`,history:`Missile defenses were first built in the 1960s; the ABM Treaty of 1972 limited them in the USSR and the US to preserve mutual deterrence. In 1983 the US announced the Strategic Defense Initiative, with interceptors in orbit. No one has yet learned to reliably stop every warhead.`,fact:`Hitting a missile with a missile has been compared to hitting a bullet with a bullet. The first test to destroy a warhead by direct collision, with no explosives at all, succeeded in 1984.`},"wonder:the_internet":{desc:`Every city in the empire counts as having a [[building:research_lab|Research Lab]]. A powerful push toward the end of the science race.`,history:`In 1969 the first ARPANET message was sent between California universities: the letters LO, from LOGIN, got through before the system crashed. The network has been saying more ever since. In 1989–1991 Tim Berners-Lee at CERN created the World Wide Web.`,fact:`In 1971 Ray Tomlinson picked the @ sign for email addresses simply because it was on the keyboard and almost never used. In Italian it is a “snail,” in Russian a “little dog.”`},"wonder:the_manhattan_project":{desc:`Opens nuclear weapons to every power, not just the builder. Build it only if you are ready for the threat to become everyone's.`,history:`The Manhattan Project was the American program to build the atomic bomb, launched in 1942; Robert Oppenheimer directed its laboratory at Los Alamos. The Trinity test took place on July 16, 1945, and in August bombs destroyed Hiroshima and Nagasaki. The world entered the nuclear age.`,fact:`Secrecy was so strict that many of the tens of thousands of workers at Oak Ridge did not know what they were making until the news broke in August 1945.`},"wonder:the_united_nations":{desc:`Holds the elections that can bring a diplomatic victory. For rulers who would rather win by vote than by war.`,history:`The UN Charter was signed in San Francisco in June 1945, and the organization began work that October. It replaced the League of Nations, which had failed to prevent the Second World War. Today almost every state in the world is a member, and its headquarters stand in New York.`,fact:`The UN has six official languages — Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian and Spanish — and its interpreters work in all of them at once during General Assembly debates.`},"resource:cattle":{desc:`A grassland or plains tile that feeds a city and adds a little production too. Settle beside a herd and the town grows without stalling its build queue.`,fact:`The English “fee” comes from Old English feoh, which meant both “cattle” and “property.” For a long time they were the same thing.`},"resource:dyes":{desc:`A luxury from forests and jungles. Once connected to your roads it keeps citizens content across the empire, and the tile itself adds a trickle of trade.`,fact:`The first commercially successful synthetic dye, mauveine, was found by accident in 1856 by an eighteen-year-old chemist, William Perkin, who was trying to make quinine.`},"resource:fish":{desc:`The richest food on the water: a fishing ground feeds a coastal city and adds trade. Worth settling by the shore for, and worth a [[building:harbor|Harbor]] later.`,fact:`Cod fed Europe for centuries, and some historians think Basque fishermen worked the cod banks off Newfoundland before Columbus — a claim still debated.`},"resource:furs":{desc:`A luxury of cold forests and tundra. Besides keeping citizens content, it gives the tile production and trade — a rare reason to love the frozen fringe.`,fact:`The Hudson’s Bay Company, founded in 1670 for the fur trade, is one of the oldest commercial companies in North America.`},"resource:game":{desc:`Game turns a lean forest, tundra or marsh tile into a well-fed one. A useful food source for cities that got no grassland of their own.`,fact:`Bison once roamed the North American plains in the tens of millions. By the 1880s only a few hundred were left, and conservation brought them back.`},"resource:gems":{desc:`The most lucrative luxury: gems in mountains and jungle yield rich trade and please the whole nation. Worth a road even into an awkward corner.`,fact:`Diamonds are not especially rare. Their price was long held up by a cartel controlling supply, and “A diamond is forever” was an advertising slogan of 1947.`},"resource:gold":{desc:`A vein in the mountains or hills with rich trade — a direct line to gold and science. The city that works it fills the treasury noticeably.`,fact:`Gold can be beaten so thin that light passes through the leaf, glowing faintly green.`},"resource:incense":{desc:`A luxury of deserts and hills: link it to your roads and citizens grow calmer. Proof that a desert can smell better than its reputation.`,fact:`The incense trade made the Nabataeans rich, and their capital, Petra, carved into rose-red cliffs, grew on caravan tolls.`},"resource:ivory":{desc:`A luxury of plains and forests that keeps citizens content. It is also required to build the [[wonder:the_statue_of_zeus|Statue of Zeus]].`,fact:`Piano keys were faced with ivory until the mid-twentieth century, which is why pianists still speak of “tickling the ivories.”`},"resource:oasis":{desc:`Water in the middle of the desert: an oasis gives food where there would be none. Put a city beside it and the sand stops being empty.`,fact:`Siwa Oasis in Egypt was home to the oracle of Amun, and Alexander the Great crossed the desert to consult it.`},"resource:silks":{desc:`A rich luxury of forests and jungles: generous trade and contented citizens. A good excuse to push a road into the thicket.`,fact:`The thread of a single silkworm cocoon can be more than half a mile long.`},"resource:spices":{desc:`A luxury of jungles and forests: connected spices please citizens and add trade to the tile. Most valuable when your realm has few other luxuries.`,fact:`In 1667 the Dutch let the English keep New Amsterdam — renamed New York back in 1664 — and kept Run, a tiny nutmeg island in the Banda Sea. At the time it looked like a good deal.`},"resource:sugar":{desc:`Sugarcane on plains and hills gives a little food and trade. A modest but welcome boost to an ordinary tile.`,fact:`Sugar beet became a major crop thanks to Napoleon: when the British blockade cut off cane sugar, he backed beet-sugar factories across France.`},"resource:tobacco":{desc:`Tobacco on grassland and hills adds a little trade. Your citizens rate it far higher than your treasurer does.`,fact:`In 1604 King James I published A Counterblaste to Tobacco, calling smoking “loathsome to the eye, hateful to the nose, harmful to the brain.”`},"resource:tropical_fruit":{desc:`Fruit makes a hungry jungle tile a little more nourishing and adds trade. A help for a city hemmed in by the thicket.`,fact:`In eighteenth-century Britain a pineapple was such a luxury that people rented one to display at a party.`},"resource:whales":{desc:`Whales in open sea give food, production and trade at once — one of the best tiles a port city can work, especially with a [[building:harbor|Harbor]].`,fact:`Whale oil lit many lamps of the early nineteenth century until kerosene from petroleum pushed it out of lighting. Whaling did not end there: it later grew even larger for other products.`},"resource:wheat":{desc:`Grain on plains, grassland and flood plains — a generous source of food. A city by the wheat grows fast and can afford citizens on poorer tiles.`,fact:`Einkorn, one of the first wheats ever domesticated in the Fertile Crescent, is still grown in a few places today.`},"resource:wines":{desc:`Vineyards on hills, grassland and plains: a luxury that keeps citizens content, plus a little food and trade.`,fact:`Most European vines now grow on American roots: in the nineteenth century the phylloxera aphid destroyed much of Europe’s vineyards, and grafting onto resistant American rootstock saved them.`},"resource:horses":{desc:`A strategic resource revealed by [[tech:the_wheel|The Wheel]]: without horses there are no chariots, horsemen or knights. Secure a herd early — cavalry decides many wars.`,fact:`Horses can doze standing up, thanks to a system that locks their legs, but they must lie down for REM sleep.`},"resource:iron":{desc:`Revealed by [[tech:iron_working|Iron Working]] in hills and mountains. Swordsmen, pikemen, knights and early cannon and warships need it; a deposit can run dry.`,fact:`The Earth’s core is mostly iron, and its churning creates the magnetic field that shields the planet and turns every compass needle north.`},"resource:saltpeter":{desc:`Revealed by [[tech:gunpowder|Gunpowder]]: no saltpeter, no musketmen, cannon or cavalry. Look in deserts, hills and mountains; a deposit can be exhausted.`,fact:`The Haber-Bosch process, which makes ammonia from air, ended the world’s dependence on mined saltpeter; fertilizer made this way now helps feed about half of humanity.`},"resource:coal":{desc:`The fuel of the industrial age, revealed by [[tech:steam_power|Steam Power]]. It gives a tile strong production and is needed for ironclads and the [[wonder:iron_works|Iron Works]].`,fact:`Newcastle was so famous for coal that “carrying coals to Newcastle” became the English phrase for a pointless errand.`},"resource:oil":{desc:`The lifeblood of a modern army: without oil there are no tanks, aircraft or modern fleet. Revealed by [[tech:refining|Refining]], it runs out sooner than most.`,fact:`People used natural oil and asphalt long before wells: Mesopotamians waterproofed boats with bitumen, and the La Brea Tar Pits in Los Angeles trapped Ice Age animals.`},"resource:rubber":{desc:`Revealed by [[tech:replaceable_parts|Replaceable Parts]], rubber equips infantry, marines, tanks and helicopters. Look for it in forests, jungles and marshes.`,fact:`Charles Goodyear discovered vulcanization in 1839 and died deep in debt. The tire company that bears his name was founded decades later and had nothing to do with him.`},"resource:aluminum":{desc:`The metal of the rocket age, revealed by [[tech:rocketry|Rocketry]]: jet aircraft, modern armor, cruise missiles and nuclear weapons need it. A deposit can run out.`,fact:`Americans write “aluminum” and Britons “aluminium,” and the chemist Humphry Davy, who named it, tried more than one spelling himself.`},"resource:uranium":{desc:`Revealed by [[tech:fission|Fission]]: nuclear weapons, nuclear submarines and Aegis cruisers need it. It runs out faster than anything else — use it while you have it.`,fact:`Uranium glass, tinted with a little uranium, glows bright green under ultraviolet light, and was popular for tableware in the early twentieth century.`},"terrain:coast":{desc:`Coastal waters give food and good trade, and fish make them richer still. A shoreline city gains a navy and an income — a fine place for a capital.`,fact:`Canada has the longest coastline of any country in the world.`},"terrain:desert":{desc:`Nearly barren ground: a little production and nothing else. Oases, irrigation and what lies beneath the sand — incense, saltpeter, later oil — redeem it.`,fact:`Several thousand years ago the Sahara was green, with lakes, hippos and crocodiles, as rock paintings in the middle of today’s desert show.`},"terrain:flood_plain":{desc:`The most fertile ground on the map: a flood plain city grows rapidly. The price is disease, striking citizens and troops until [[tech:sanitation|Sanitation]].`,fact:`The ancient Egyptians called their country Kemet, “the black land,” after the dark silt the floods left behind.`},"terrain:forest":{desc:`A source of production and cover for infantry: forests slow movement but help defense. Furs, silks, rubber and uranium hide beneath; clear it if you need fields.`,fact:`By a recent estimate there are about three trillion trees on Earth — more than there are stars in the Milky Way.`},"terrain:grassland":{desc:`The backbone of a well-fed realm: grassland gives food, irrigates easily and lets armies pass without delay. The best ground for early cities and farms.`,fact:`Grass grows from its base, not its tip, which is why it survives grazing and mowing — and why lawns are possible at all.`},"terrain:hills":{desc:`The best defensive ground: hills greatly strengthen defenders and extend vision, and mines yield strong production. A city on a hill is a fortress by itself.`,fact:`There is no universal line between a hill and a mountain; in Britain, tradition long put it at about a thousand feet.`},"terrain:jungle":{desc:`Hard ground to live or fight in: little food, slow going and disease. But gems, silks and spices grow there, and cleared jungle becomes grassland.`,fact:`Tropical rainforests cover a small share of the Earth’s land but hold more than half of its species of plants and animals.`},"terrain:marsh":{desc:`Damp, unhealthy ground: no city may be founded here, and wheels need a road. Clearing turns it into grassland, and oil may lie beneath.`,fact:`Peatlands cover only a few percent of the land, yet they store roughly twice as much carbon as all the world’s forest biomass.`},"terrain:mountains":{desc:`A near-impregnable stronghold: mountains give defenders a huge edge and long sight. No food, but iron, gold and uranium lie within, and mines yield strong production.`,fact:`Mount Everest is still rising by a few millimeters a year, as India keeps pushing into Asia.`},"terrain:ocean":{desc:`Deep water that yields nothing and forgives nothing. Trade cannot cross it until [[tech:navigation|Navigation]] and Magnetism; until then, it is mostly scenery.`,fact:`Roughly half the oxygen we breathe comes from the ocean, made by microscopic plankton.`},"terrain:plains":{desc:`The dependable middle ground: plains give both food and production, irrigate well and are easy to cross. Wheat, cattle and horses are found here.`,fact:`The Eurasian Steppe runs from Hungary to Manchuria, and for thousands of years horses, languages and empires traveled along it.`},"terrain:sea":{desc:`Open sea between the coast and the ocean: a little food and trade, plus fish and whales. Trade routes cross it once you learn [[tech:astronomy|Astronomy]].`,fact:`The Sargasso Sea is the only sea with no coastline: its borders are drawn by ocean currents.`},"terrain:tundra":{desc:`Cold, meager ground that does little for growth. It is valued for furs and game, later for oil and aluminum; forest can be planted on it.`,fact:`Permafrost has kept Ice Age animals so well that mammoths have been found with flesh and hair still intact.`},"terrain:volcano":{desc:`Rich in production, nearly impregnable and with a superb view. No city may stand on it, and it may erupt — it has never once asked for permission.`,fact:`The eruption of Tambora in 1815 caused the “Year Without a Summer.” In that gloomy summer by Lake Geneva, Mary Shelley began writing Frankenstein.`},"job:clear":{desc:`Workers cut forest down to plains and drain jungle and marsh into grassland. It takes a while, but turns hard land into farmland and ends disease there.`,fact:`By the time of the Domesday Book in 1086, most of England’s ancient forest had already been cleared for farming.`},"job:colony":{desc:`Instantly stakes a claim to a tile beyond your city borders — usually to secure a valuable resource that no city of yours can reach.`,fact:`Trading posts abroad were once called “factories,” after the merchants, or factors, who ran them. The word meant a warehouse long before it meant a mill.`},"job:irrigate":{desc:`Canals on grassland, plains, desert and flood plain add food to a tile. The core improvement for growing cities.`,fact:`The word “rival” comes from the Latin rivalis, “one who uses the same stream” — a neighbor sharing your irrigation channel.`},"job:mine":{desc:`A mine adds production to a tile, and the most on hills and mountains. Build it where a city needs output rather than food.`,fact:`The steam engine got its first real job in the mines: Newcomen’s engines pumped water out of shafts long before anyone put one on rails.`},"job:clean":{desc:`Workers clear pollution from a tile and restore its yields. Slow in mountains and jungle. The cheaper option is not polluting, which nobody has ever tried.`,fact:`The Thames, declared biologically dead in the 1950s, now supports seals and many species of fish.`},"job:road":{desc:`Roads speed movement, add trade on most tiles and connect resources to your cities. The first thing a worker should build.`,fact:`“All roads lead to Rome” had a monument: the Golden Milestone in the Forum, said to list distances to the empire’s great cities. It was a symbol, though; road mileages were usually counted from the city gates.`},"job:barricade":{desc:`A stronger fortification on the site of a fortress: defenders behind it hold even harder. Place it at chokepoints and borders. Requires [[tech:construction|Construction]].`,fact:`Historians still argue how far Haussmann’s broad Paris boulevards of the 1850s and 1860s were meant to make barricades harder to build.`},"job:fortress":{desc:`A fortification outside a city: troops inside defend better. Raise it on border hills and key passes. Requires [[tech:construction|Construction]].`,fact:`Vauban built or remodeled more than a hundred fortresses for Louis XIV, and twelve of them are now a UNESCO World Heritage site.`},"job:outpost":{desc:`A watchtower raised almost instantly that keeps an eye on the surroundings — so no army appears at your gates unannounced. Requires [[tech:masonry|Masonry]].`,fact:`Plenty of cities grew around frontier forts: Fort Dearborn, built in 1803 beside an existing settlement, helped Chicago grow, and Fort Pitt gave its name to Pittsburgh.`},"job:plant":{desc:`With [[tech:engineering|Engineering]] workers plant forest on grassland, plains and tundra, trading food for production where a city lacks output.`,fact:`China’s own “Great Green Wall” has planted billions of trees across the north to hold back the Gobi Desert.`},"job:airfield":{desc:`A field airstrip outside your cities, built quickly. It gives aircraft a base closer to the front. Requires [[tech:flight|Flight]].`,fact:`The shortest commercial runway in the world, on the Caribbean island of Saba, is only about 1,300 feet long, with cliffs at both ends.`},"job:radar":{desc:`A radar tower: your units nearby attack and defend better under its guidance. Place it by the front and key cities. Requires [[tech:advanced_flight|Advanced Flight]].`,fact:`Early radar operators sometimes saw “angels” on their screens — echoes from flocks of birds and swarms of insects — and radar ornithology was born.`},"job:railroad":{desc:`With [[tech:steam_power|Steam Power]] workers lay track over roads: troops move across the network almost instantly, and the whole country becomes one front.`,fact:`The golden spike that completed the first US transcontinental railroad in 1869 was pulled out right after the ceremony and now sits in a museum.`},"gov:anarchy":{desc:`The interregnum after a revolution: corruption is ruinous, rich tiles lose yield and nothing can be rushed. Buildings cost no upkeep, which is small comfort.`,history:`From the Greek anarchia, 'without a ruler.' Historians use the word for stretches when central power collapses, such as the civil war in 12th-century England known simply as the Anarchy. In the 19th century thinkers like Proudhon and Kropotkin recast it as a program for a stateless society. It has never polled well with kings.`,fact:`During the twelfth-century English civil war known as the Anarchy, the Empress Matilda escaped from besieged Oxford Castle across the frozen Thames, reportedly dressed in white against the snow.`},"gov:despotism":{desc:`Where every civilization starts: rich tiles lose part of their yield and corruption is high, but you can rush work with your own citizens. Leave it as soon as you can.`,history:`To the Greeks a despotes was the master of a household, and later a ruler bound by no law or council. Many early kingdoms worked this way, with one will deciding everything. Aristotle contrasted despotic rule with rule over free people, and in the 18th century Montesquieu made despotism the classic model of arbitrary power.`,fact:`“Enlightened despotism” was a real eighteenth-century ideal: Frederick the Great called himself “the first servant of the state” while answering to no one.`},"gov:monarchy":{desc:`A steady ancient government for a growing realm: moderate corruption, no war weariness, and martial law to keep order. Large cities support big garrisons for free.`,history:`Monarchy, rule by a single hereditary sovereign, is among the oldest forms of government; Sumer and Egypt already had kings. Medieval kings leaned on nobles and the church, and early modern Europe saw the rise of absolute monarchy. Most surviving monarchies are now constitutional: the monarch reigns while parliament governs.`,fact:`The Sumerian King List, one of the oldest lists of rulers, credits some of its earliest kings with reigns of tens of thousands of years before it settles into human lifespans.`},"gov:republic":{desc:`A government for traders and scholars: more trade from tiles and low corruption. Armies cost more to support, though, and long wars breed discontent.`,history:`The word comes from Latin res publica, 'the public thing.' After expelling its kings around 509 BC, Rome was governed for nearly five centuries by elected consuls and a senate. Venice and Genoa carried the idea through the Middle Ages, and the American and French revolutions revived it for the modern world.`,fact:`Plato’s Republic describes nothing like a republic in the Roman sense: its ideal city is ruled by philosopher-kings. The title is a later translation of a Greek word for “constitution.”`},"gov:democracy":{desc:`Peak trade, minimal corruption, fast workers and immunity to propaganda. The price is public patience: no government suffers more from a long war.`,history:`Democracy, 'rule by the people,' took shape in Athens in the late 6th century BC, where citizens decided public business in an open assembly. Modern representative democracy grew out of the English parliamentary tradition and the revolutions of the 18th century; universal suffrage reached most countries only in the 20th.`,fact:`Athenian jurors were chosen by lot on the morning of the trial, so no one could know in advance whom to bribe.`},"gov:feudalism":{desc:`Built for many small towns, each supporting troops for free; citizens can be spent to rush production. It does little for big cities, and long wars still wear people down.`,history:`Feudalism took shape in Western Europe as the Carolingian empire broke apart in the 9th century: lords granted land, the fief, to vassals in return for military service and loyalty. Power was split among castles and mounted retinues. Growing towns, money economies and standing armies wore the system down by the end of the Middle Ages.`,fact:`A knight’s military service to his lord was often limited to about forty days a year; a king who needed a longer campaign had to pay — one reason medieval rulers turned to hired soldiers.`},"gov:communism":{desc:`Corruption is the same in every city, near the capital or far from it — a gift to a sprawling empire. Troops are cheap, martial law is strong and wars cause no weariness.`,history:`The ideal of a classless society without private property is old, but Marx and Engels gave it modern form in The Communist Manifesto of 1848. After the 1917 revolution a one-party state was built on it in Russia, and similar regimes spread widely in the 20th century. Central planning drove rapid industrialization at the cost of tight control and enormous human losses.`,fact:`The Paris Commune of 1871 governed the city for about two months, and Marx hailed it as the first workers’ government.`},"gov:fascism":{desc:`A government of total mobilization: the fastest workers, large free garrisons in big cities and no war weariness. Made for long wars, and for little else.`,history:`Fascism, an ultranationalist dictatorship, arose in Italy after the First World War; Mussolini took power in 1922. It subordinated society to the state and its leader, crushed opposition and glorified war, and the related Nazi regime in Germany started the Second World War. The Axis defeat in 1945 brought these regimes down.`,fact:`George Orwell, who was shot through the throat fighting fascists in Spain, complained in 1944 that the word had been stretched until it meant little more than “something not desirable.”`}};export{e as default};